-- Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab) gap-filling MCQs: 374 new questions on topics, tables, figures and worked examples
-- that the first set (BIO-0001 to BIO-0678, migration 0014) did not test or tested thinly. By chapter: chapter 13 (Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation)) BIO-5001 to BIO-5017, chapter 14 (Human Urinary System) BIO-5018 to BIO-5038, chapter 15 (Human Nervous System) BIO-5039 to BIO-5083, chapter 16 (Human Endocrine System) BIO-5084 to BIO-5113, chapter 17 (Human Reproductive Systems) BIO-5114 to BIO-5133, chapter 18 (Inheritance) BIO-5134 to BIO-5170, chapter 19 (Chromosome and DNA) BIO-5171 to BIO-5216, chapter 20 (Biotechnology) BIO-5217 to BIO-5239, chapter 21 (Immunity) BIO-5240 to BIO-5268, chapter 22 (Biostatistics) BIO-5269 to BIO-5295, chapter 23 (Pharmacology) BIO-5296 to BIO-5314, chapter 24 (Evolution) BIO-5315 to BIO-5339, chapter 25 (Ecology) BIO-5340 to BIO-5374.
-- Same template and rules as 0014: original wording, the book page each answer is checked against, a reason for every
-- wrong option. They use their own id block (BIO-5001 up) so they never meet the ids the BIO counter hands out, and
-- the counter is left alone. Not in the free set. INSERT OR IGNORE skips rows already there; if the site owner's admin
-- account doesn't exist (a fresh local database) nothing is inserted.

-- Banks. Each question's own chapter is its MDCAT chapter; FSc Part 2 lists every question under its textbook chapter;
-- AKU reuses the MDCAT chapter (ecology too, not biostatistics or pharmacology), as in 0014.
-- NUMS now follows the NUMS MDCAT 2026 syllabus, which has its own 13 Biology units (lib/banks.ts numsBiology). It has
-- no excretion/homeostasis, chromosomes and DNA, biotechnology, biostatistics, pharmacology or ecology unit, and it
-- puts the immune system under "Life processes in animals and plants". So this migration also takes the Biology 12
-- chapter 13, 14, 19 and 20 questions out of the NUMS bank and moves chapter 21 (Immunity) to that unit.

-- Inserted in batches of 20 to stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5001', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulators and osmoconformers', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 4 (§13.2.1)', 'According to the book, most vertebrates living in aquatic and terrestrial environments are', '["isotonic to their surroundings at all times", "osmoconformers", "osmoregulators", "ammonotelic"]', 2, 'Osmoregulators keep internal water and solute concentrations constant despite changes outside; the book says most aquatic and terrestrial vertebrates are osmoregulators.', '{"0": "Being isotonic to the surroundings describes osmoconformers.", "1": "Only some marine vertebrates (hagfishes, sharks, rays) and invertebrates are osmoconformers.", "3": "Ammonotelic refers to excreting ammonia, not to osmotic regulation; most land vertebrates are not ammonotelic."}'),
  ('BIO-5002', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulators and osmoconformers', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 4 (§13.2.1)', 'Which of these vertebrates is named in the book as an osmoconformer?', '["Freshwater bony fish", "Kangaroo rat", "Sparrow", "Shark"]', 3, 'The book lists all marine invertebrates, some freshwater invertebrates and some marine vertebrates such as hagfishes, sharks and rays as osmoconformers.', '{"0": "Freshwater fishes are osmoregulators that pump salts in and make dilute urine.", "1": "The kangaroo rat is a terrestrial osmoregulator that conserves water.", "2": "Birds are osmoregulators with uricotelic excretion."}'),
  ('BIO-5003', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation in water', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 4 (§13.2.2)', 'Freshwater fishes continuously lose Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions to the water by diffusion. They make up for this loss by', '["secreting salts through rectal glands", "actively transporting salts back into their bodies", "drinking large amounts of water", "producing small amounts of concentrated urine"]', 1, 'The book states that to compensate for salt loss, freshwater animals actively transport salts back into their bodies.', '{"0": "Rectal glands remove salts and occur in some marine fishes.", "2": "Freshwater animals rarely drink; drinking would add to their water load.", "3": "They produce large volumes of dilute urine to get rid of excess water."}'),
  ('BIO-5004', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation in water', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 5 (§13.2.2)', 'To conserve water, marine bony fishes produce', '["very small amounts of highly concentrated urine", "large volumes of dilute urine", "no urine at all, relying only on the gills", "urine that is isotonic to fresh water"]', 0, 'Marine fishes lose water by osmosis, so they drink sea water and conserve water by making very little, highly concentrated urine.', '{"1": "This is the freshwater strategy for removing excess water.", "2": "They still produce some urine and also remove salts through the kidneys.", "3": "Their problem is water loss, so their urine is concentrated, not dilute."}'),
  ('BIO-5005', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation on land', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 6 (§13.2.2)', 'Many desert animals reduce evaporative water loss by', '["staying underground during the heat of the day and emerging at night", "basking in the sun at midday", "producing large volumes of dilute urine", "excreting ammonia, which needs little water"]', 0, 'The book describes desert animals that stay underground by day and come out at night when the air is cooler and more humid.', '{"1": "Basking would increase heat gain and water loss.", "2": "That wastes water; desert animals conserve it.", "3": "Ammonia needs the most water (about 500 mL per gram) for excretion."}'),
  ('BIO-5006', 'Homeostasis', 'Osmoregulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 4 (§13.2)', 'The osmotic pressure of a solution is a measure of its', '["tendency to lose solutes by active transport", "ability to diffuse from a hypertonic to a hypotonic region", "temperature at which water evaporates", "tendency to take in water by osmosis"]', 3, 'As the book recalls, osmotic pressure measures a solution''s tendency to take in water by osmosis.', '{"0": "Active transport of solutes is not what osmotic pressure measures.", "1": "Water moves from the dilute (hypotonic) to the less dilute (hypertonic) side, and osmotic pressure is a property of the solution, not a diffusion ability.", "2": "This is unrelated to osmosis."}'),
  ('BIO-5007', 'Homeostasis', 'Nitrogenous wastes', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 6 (§13.2.3)', 'Ammonia is formed in the liver when', '["glucose is oxidised in the absence of oxygen", "urea combines with carbon dioxide", "amino groups removed from amino acids and nucleic acids combine with H⁺", "uric acid is broken down by the kidneys"]', 2, 'The book states that the -NH₂ group removed from amino acids and nucleic acids combines with H⁺ in the liver to form ammonia.', '{"0": "Glucose contains no nitrogen, so it cannot yield ammonia.", "1": "Urea is made from ammonia, not the other way round.", "3": "Uric acid is formed from purines and is not the source of ammonia."}'),
  ('BIO-5008', 'Homeostasis', 'Nitrogenous wastes', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 6 (§13.2.3)', 'About how much water is needed to dilute 1 g of urea to a non-toxic level?', '["500 mL", "50 mL", "1 mL", "5 litres"]', 1, 'Urea is less toxic than ammonia; one gram needs about 50 mL of water, compared with 500 mL for ammonia and 1 mL for uric acid.', '{"0": "This is the figure for ammonia.", "2": "This is the figure for uric acid.", "3": "No nitrogenous waste in the book needs this much."}'),
  ('BIO-5009', 'Homeostasis', 'Nitrogenous wastes', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 6 (§13.2.3)', 'Mammals convert ammonia into urea in the liver and excrete it through the kidneys. Animals that excrete urea as their major nitrogenous waste are called', '["ureotelic", "osmoconformers", "uricotelic", "ammonotelic"]', 0, 'The book names animals whose main nitrogenous waste is urea ureotelic.', '{"1": "This term is about matching the osmotic concentration of the surroundings, not waste type.", "2": "Uricotelic animals excrete mainly uric acid.", "3": "Ammonotelic animals excrete mainly ammonia."}'),
  ('BIO-5010', 'Homeostasis', 'Nitrogenous wastes', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 7 (§13.2.3)', 'Uric acid can be excreted with very little water mainly because it', '["is more toxic than ammonia", "is poorly soluble in water and forms precipitates", "is made without using any energy", "dissolves very easily in water"]', 1, 'Uric acid is less soluble in water and precipitates, so it can be excreted with only about 1 mL of water per gram.', '{"0": "It is the least toxic of the three wastes.", "2": "Converting ammonia to uric acid uses a lot of energy.", "3": "High solubility would need more water, not less."}'),
  ('BIO-5011', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulation', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 7 (§13.3)', 'Very low temperatures affect the lipid bilayer of cell membranes by making it', '["permanently denatured like an enzyme", "more selectively permeable", "rigid and brittle", "lose its selective permeability"]', 2, 'The book notes that low temperatures make membranes rigid and brittle, while high temperatures make them lose selective permeability.', '{"0": "Denaturation is described for enzymes at high temperatures.", "1": "Temperature extremes do not improve permeability control.", "3": "This is the effect of high temperatures."}'),
  ('BIO-5012', 'Homeostasis', 'Heat source', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 7 (§13.3.1)', 'A snake basking in direct sunlight to warm itself gains heat mainly by', '["conduction", "burning brown adipose tissue", "shivering", "radiation"]', 3, 'The book describes basking in the sun as gaining heat by radiation, and sitting on a warm rock as conduction.', '{"0": "Conduction is heat gained by contact, such as lying on a warm rock.", "1": "Brown fat is used by endotherms, not ectotherms.", "2": "Shivering is an internal heat source used by endotherms."}'),
  ('BIO-5013', 'Homeostasis', 'Heat source', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 7 (§13.3.1)', 'In very cold climates, many ectotherms survive the coldest months by', '["growing thick fur or feathers", "shivering continuously", "raising their metabolic rate with thyroxine", "becoming dormant"]', 3, 'The book says ectotherms in very cold climates become dormant during the coldest months.', '{"0": "Insulation like hair and feathers belongs to endotherms (mammals and birds).", "1": "Shivering is an endotherm response.", "2": "Ectotherms have a low metabolic rate and rely on outside heat."}'),
  ('BIO-5014', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulatory ability', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 8 (§13.3.2)', 'Which statement about poikilotherms is correct according to the book?', '["Birds are poikilotherms", "Their body temperature stays almost constant", "All invertebrates are poikilotherms", "They need large amounts of food to keep warm"]', 2, 'The book states that all invertebrates are poikilotherms, as are fishes, amphibians and reptiles among vertebrates.', '{"0": "Birds and mammals are homeotherms.", "1": "Their body temperature varies considerably with the environment.", "3": "Poikilotherms need much less food because they do not fuel a constant temperature."}'),
  ('BIO-5015', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulation in humans', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 8 (§13.3.3)', 'Which of the following is NOT a human response to a fall in body temperature below 37 °C?', '["Vasodilation of skin capillaries", "Shivering", "Increase in metabolic rate", "Vasoconstriction"]', 0, 'Vasodilation increases heat loss and is a response to overheating; cooling triggers vasoconstriction, shivering and a rise in metabolic rate.', '{"1": "Shivering produces heat by spasmodic contraction of skeletal muscles.", "2": "A higher metabolic rate produces more body heat.", "3": "Vasoconstriction reduces blood flow to the skin to save heat."}'),
  ('BIO-5016', 'Homeostasis', 'Feedback mechanisms', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 3 (§13.1.2)', 'Apart from labour and childbirth, which process does the book give as an example of positive feedback?', '["Regulation of body temperature", "Blood clotting", "Regulation of blood glucose", "Regulation of hormone secretion"]', 1, 'Positive feedback occurs in processes needing rapid, large changes such as blood clotting, labour and childbirth.', '{"0": "This is a negative feedback example.", "2": "This is a negative feedback example.", "3": "This is a negative feedback example."}'),
  ('BIO-5017', 'Homeostasis', 'Thermoregulation in humans', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 13 Homeostasis (Thermoregulation and Osmoregulation), p. 8 (§13.3.3)', 'A person walks into a very cold room and starts to shiver. Which sequence correctly traces this response?', '["Skeletal muscles → hypothalamus → skin thermoreceptors", "Skin thermoreceptors → pituitary → thyroid → sweat glands", "Skin thermoreceptors → hypothalamus → skeletal muscles", "Hypothalamus → skin thermoreceptors → sweat glands"]', 2, 'Skin thermoreceptors (receptors) signal the hypothalamus (control centre), which signals skeletal muscles (effectors) to shiver; this is negative feedback.', '{"0": "Muscles are the effectors at the end of the loop, not the start.", "1": "Sweat glands cool the body; shivering is carried out by skeletal muscles.", "3": "The order is reversed and sweat glands are used for cooling, not warming."}'),
  ('BIO-5018', 'Homeostasis', 'Urinary system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 13 (§14.1)', 'The upper parts of both kidneys are partly protected by the', '["sternum and clavicles", "pelvic girdle", "1st and 2nd lumbar vertebrae only", "11th and 12th ribs"]', 3, 'The book states that the upper parts of both kidneys are partially protected by the 11th and 12th ribs.', '{"0": "These lie far above the kidneys, in the chest and shoulder region.", "1": "The pelvic girdle surrounds the urinary bladder, not the kidneys.", "2": "The kidneys lie beside the vertebral column, but the protection named in the book is from the ribs."}'),
  ('BIO-5019', 'Homeostasis', 'Urinary system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 13 (§14.1)', 'The hollow, distensible muscular organ that stores urine lies in the', '["upper abdominal cavity beside the vertebral column", "pelvic cavity", "renal pelvis", "thoracic cavity"]', 1, 'The urinary bladder is a hollow, distensible muscular organ in the pelvic cavity that acts as the urine reservoir.', '{"0": "This is where the kidneys lie, not the bladder.", "2": "The renal pelvis is a funnel inside the kidney that collects urine; it does not store it.", "3": "The thoracic cavity holds the heart and lungs."}'),
  ('BIO-5020', 'Homeostasis', 'Structure of kidney', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 13 (§14.1.1)', 'Which structure sits on top of each kidney and is enclosed with it in the fibrous renal capsule?', '["Adrenal gland", "Thyroid gland", "Urinary bladder", "Pancreas"]', 0, 'Each kidney has an adrenal gland on top; the kidney and its adrenal gland are surrounded by a fibrous renal capsule.', '{"1": "The thyroid lies in the neck.", "2": "The bladder lies in the pelvic cavity.", "3": "The pancreas lies in the abdomen but not on top of the kidney."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5021', 'Homeostasis', 'Nephron', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 14 (§14.1.1)', 'About what percentage of nephrons are cortical nephrons with relatively short loops of Henle?', '["100%", "50%", "85%", "15%"]', 2, 'Cortical nephrons make up the majority (about 85%) of nephrons; juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops reaching deep into the medulla.', '{"0": "Juxtamedullary nephrons also exist, so not all nephrons are cortical.", "1": "The book gives a clear majority for cortical nephrons, not half.", "3": "This is roughly the share of juxtamedullary nephrons."}'),
  ('BIO-5022', 'Homeostasis', 'Selective reabsorption', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 16 (§14.1.3)', 'About how much of the water in the glomerular filtrate is returned to the blood in the proximal convoluted tubule?', '["100%", "75%", "20%", "5%"]', 1, 'Most reabsorption occurs in the proximal convoluted tubule, where about 75% of the water returns to the capillaries by osmosis.', '{"0": "Further water is reabsorbed in the loop of Henle, distal tubule and collecting duct.", "2": "About 20% is the fraction of plasma that filters into Bowman''s capsule.", "3": "The proximal tubule is the main site of reabsorption, so the share is far larger."}'),
  ('BIO-5023', 'Homeostasis', 'Selective reabsorption', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 16 (§14.1.3)', 'Which part of the nephron is the final site of water reabsorption?', '["Collecting duct", "Proximal convoluted tubule", "Bowman''s capsule", "Ascending limb of the loop of Henle"]', 0, 'The book calls the collecting duct the final site for water reabsorption; ADH acts on its walls.', '{"1": "This is the main, not the final, site of reabsorption.", "2": "Filtration, not reabsorption, takes place here.", "3": "The ascending limb is impermeable to water."}'),
  ('BIO-5024', 'Homeostasis', 'Tubular secretion', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 16 (§14.1.3)', 'According to the book, tubular secretion is carried out by the epithelial cells of the', '["glomerulus", "renal pelvis", "descending limb of the loop of Henle", "distal convoluted tubule"]', 3, 'Epithelial cells of the distal convoluted tubule take materials from the surrounding blood and secrete them into the filtrate.', '{"0": "The glomerulus is a capillary network where filtration occurs.", "1": "The renal pelvis collects urine and does not secrete into the filtrate.", "2": "This limb reabsorbs water."}'),
  ('BIO-5025', 'Homeostasis', 'Urine elimination', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 17 (§14.1.3)', 'On a hot day a person sweats a lot. Their kidneys are most likely to produce', '["no change in urine volume", "urine containing glucose", "a larger volume of dilute urine", "a smaller volume of concentrated urine"]', 3, 'In hot weather more water is lost through sweat, so the kidneys conserve water and produce a smaller volume of concentrated urine.', '{"0": "Water loss by sweating does change urine output.", "1": "Glucose is normally reabsorbed completely; sweating does not change that.", "2": "This happens when there is excess water in the body."}'),
  ('BIO-5026', 'Homeostasis', 'Counter-current mechanism', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 17 (§14.2.1)', 'In the counter-current mechanism, the water that leaves the collecting duct by osmosis returns to the blood through the', '["glomerular capillaries", "vasa recta", "afferent arteriole", "renal pelvis"]', 1, 'As urine passes down the collecting duct through the salty fluid, water moves out by osmosis and enters blood via the vasa recta.', '{"0": "These are inside Bowman''s capsule and are for filtration.", "2": "The afferent arteriole carries blood into the glomerulus.", "3": "The renal pelvis collects urine, not reabsorbed water."}'),
  ('BIO-5027', 'Homeostasis', 'Counter-current mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 17 (§14.2.1)', 'As the ascending limb of the loop of Henle actively pumps sodium ions out of the filtrate, which ions follow them into the surrounding fluid?', '["Chloride ions", "Potassium ions", "Calcium ions", "Hydrogen ions"]', 0, 'Sodium ions are actively transported out of the ascending limb and chloride ions follow, keeping the surrounding fluid salty.', '{"1": "Potassium ions are secreted into the filtrate under aldosterone''s influence.", "2": "The book names chloride, not calcium, as following sodium.", "3": "Hydrogen ions are secreted into the filtrate in the distal tubule."}'),
  ('BIO-5028', 'Homeostasis', 'Hormonal control', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 18 (§14.2, Hormonal control)', 'Apart from increasing water reabsorption, ADH raises blood pressure by', '["increasing sodium excretion", "dilating blood vessels", "causing blood vessels to constrict", "making the collecting ducts impermeable to water"]', 2, 'ADH increases blood volume and also causes blood vessels to constrict, which further raises blood pressure.', '{"0": "Sodium excretion would lower blood volume; ADH does not do this.", "1": "Vasodilation would lower blood pressure.", "3": "ADH makes them more permeable, not less."}'),
  ('BIO-5029', 'Homeostasis', 'Hormonal control', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 18 (§14.2, Hormonal control)', 'Aldosterone, which regulates the sodium and potassium levels of urine, is produced by the', '["adrenal cortex", "posterior pituitary", "hypothalamus", "adrenal medulla"]', 0, 'Aldosterone is produced by the adrenal cortex, the outer layer of the adrenal gland on top of the kidney.', '{"1": "The posterior pituitary releases ADH.", "2": "The hypothalamus produces ADH.", "3": "The adrenal medulla secretes adrenaline and noradrenaline."}'),
  ('BIO-5030', 'Homeostasis', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 19 (§14.3.2)', 'When urine is alkaline, the kidney stone that forms is usually made of', '["cellulose", "calcium oxalate", "uric acid", "calcium phosphate"]', 3, 'The book notes that alkaline urine gives calcium phosphate stones, while acidic urine is linked with calcium oxalate or uric acid stones.', '{"0": "Cellulose is the dialyzer membrane material, not a stone.", "1": "Calcium oxalate stones are linked with acidic urine, hypercalciuria and hyperoxaluria.", "2": "Uric acid stones form when urine is persistently acidic."}'),
  ('BIO-5031', 'Homeostasis', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 19 (§14.3.2)', 'Calcium oxalate kidney stones are formed because of', '["hyperkalaemia", "hypercalciuria and hyperoxaluria", "low ADH secretion", "hyperuricemia"]', 1, 'The most common stones (calcium oxalate, about 70%) form due to high calcium and high oxalate in urine.', '{"0": "Hyperkalaemia is high blood potassium, linked with heart arrhythmias.", "2": "Low ADH causes diabetes insipidus.", "3": "Hyperuricemia causes uric acid stones."}'),
  ('BIO-5032', 'Homeostasis', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 20 (§14.3.2)', 'Percutaneous nephrolithotripsy (PNL) is preferred over shockwave lithotripsy for', '["stones broken from outside the body without any incision", "urinary tract infections", "larger stones, reached through a tube inserted from the back", "very small stones that pass in urine anyway"]', 2, 'PNL is used for larger stones: a tube is inserted from the back into the kidney, a nephroscope views the stone, and ultrasound breaks it.', '{"0": "That describes extracorporeal shockwave lithotripsy.", "1": "UTIs are treated with antibiotics.", "3": "Small stones are suited to ECSWL or pass naturally."}'),
  ('BIO-5033', 'Homeostasis', 'Kidney failure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 20 (§14.3.3)', 'Which is a common underlying cause of chronic kidney failure?', '["A single course of NSAIDs", "Diabetes", "A blood clot in a renal arteriole", "A sudden blockage by a kidney stone"]', 1, 'Chronic kidney failure is often caused by diabetes, high blood pressure or long-term kidney disease.', '{"0": "Certain medicines such as NSAIDs are listed as causes of acute kidney failure.", "2": "This is listed under acute kidney failure.", "3": "Blockage of the urinary tract is a cause of acute failure."}'),
  ('BIO-5034', 'Homeostasis', 'Dialysis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 20 (§14.3.3)', 'In haemodialysis, the cleaned blood returns to the body through', '["a second catheter inserted in a vein", "the same catheter in the artery", "the ureter", "the peritoneal membrane"]', 0, 'Blood leaves through a catheter usually in an arm artery, passes through the dialyzer, and returns through a second catheter in a vein.', '{"1": "The book describes a second catheter, inserted in a vein, for the return.", "2": "The ureter carries urine, not blood.", "3": "This is used in peritoneal dialysis, which does not circulate blood outside the body."}'),
  ('BIO-5035', 'Homeostasis', 'Dialysis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 21 (§14.3.3)', 'Muscle cramps during dialysis are explained in the book as being due to', '["damage to the 11th and 12th ribs", "rejection of the dialyzer by the immune system", "changes in salt levels", "excess glucose in the dialysate"]', 2, 'The book lists muscle cramps during dialysis, caused by changes in salt levels, among dialysis problems.', '{"0": "This is unrelated to dialysis.", "1": "Rejection is a problem of kidney transplants.", "3": "The book does not link cramps to glucose."}'),
  ('BIO-5036', 'Homeostasis', 'Transplant', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 22 (§14.3.4)', 'A kidney donor who is a spouse, friend or co-worker of the recipient is called', '["a deceased donor", "a related donor", "a universal donor", "an unrelated donor"]', 3, 'An unrelated donor is not genetically related to the recipient; this is less common but works if blood group and tissue type match.', '{"0": "Deceased donors registered to donate organs after death.", "1": "Related donors are genetic relatives such as parents, siblings or children.", "2": "This term describes blood group O, not kidney donors."}'),
  ('BIO-5037', 'Homeostasis', 'Transplant', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 22 (§14.3.4)', 'The Pakistan Kidney and Liver Institute & Research Center (PKLI&RC) was established in 2017 in', '["Islamabad", "Peshawar", "Karachi", "Lahore"]', 3, 'The book states that PKLI&RC is a tertiary-care hospital in Lahore, established in 2017.', '{"0": "The book places PKLI&RC in Lahore.", "1": "The book places PKLI&RC in Lahore.", "2": "SIUT, Pakistan''s first kidney transplant centre (1985), is in Karachi."}'),
  ('BIO-5038', 'Homeostasis', 'Transplant', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 14 Human Urinary System, p. 23 (§14.3.4)', 'Which is listed in the book as a side effect of immunosuppressant medicines given after a kidney transplant?', '["Low blood pressure", "Increased risk of infections and cancers", "Weight loss", "Stronger rejection of the new kidney"]', 1, 'Immunosuppressants can cause high blood pressure, weight gain and a higher risk of infections and cancers.', '{"0": "The book lists high, not low, blood pressure.", "2": "The book lists weight gain.", "3": "These drugs are given to prevent rejection."}'),
  ('BIO-5039', 'Coordination and control', 'Nervous coordination', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 26 (§15.1)', 'In humans, chemical coordination is controlled by the', '["endocrine system", "nervous system", "immune system", "urinary system"]', 0, 'The book names two types of coordination: nervous coordination by the nervous system and chemical coordination by the endocrine system.', '{"1": "The nervous system controls nervous coordination, which uses electrical signals.", "2": "The immune system defends against pathogens.", "3": "The urinary system excretes wastes and osmoregulates."}'),
  ('BIO-5040', 'Coordination and control', 'Neuroglia', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 28 (§15.2.1)', 'Myelin sheaths around axons in the peripheral nervous system are produced by', '["Nissl''s granules", "nodes of Ranvier", "Schwann cells", "oligodendrocytes"]', 2, 'Schwann cells make myelin sheaths in the PNS, while oligodendrocytes make them in the CNS.', '{"0": "Nissl''s granules are ribosomes and rough ER in the cell body.", "1": "Nodes of Ranvier are gaps in the myelin sheath, not cells.", "3": "Oligodendrocytes form myelin in the central nervous system."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5041', 'Coordination and control', 'Neuroglia', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 28 (§15.2.1)', 'In the central nervous system, grey matter is made mainly of', '["Schwann cells only", "myelinated axons", "cerebrospinal fluid", "non-myelinated dendrites and cell bodies"]', 3, 'The book states that myelinated axons make white matter, while non-myelinated dendrites and cell bodies make grey matter.', '{"0": "Schwann cells are in the PNS and are not what grey matter is made of.", "1": "Myelinated axons make up white matter.", "2": "CSF fills the ventricles and central canal; it is not a tissue."}'),
  ('BIO-5042', 'Coordination and control', 'Types of neurons', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 28 (§15.2.2)', 'According to the book, sensory neurons are unipolar. This means that they have', '["only one long dendrite, whose terminal branches connect to receptors", "cell bodies inside the brain and spinal cord", "many highly branched dendrites", "no axon at all"]', 0, 'The book describes sensory neurons as unipolar, with one long dendrite whose end branches connect to receptors, and an axon on the other side of the cell body.', '{"1": "Their cell bodies are outside the CNS in ganglia.", "2": "This describes multipolar motor neurons.", "3": "Sensory neurons have an axon that carries impulses toward the CNS."}'),
  ('BIO-5043', 'Coordination and control', 'Membrane potential', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 30 (§15.3)', 'A nerve impulse is defined in the book as', '["a flow of electrons through the axoplasm, as in a copper wire", "a wave of electrochemical changes that travels along the length of a neuron", "the release of hormones into the blood", "the movement of neurotransmitter across a synaptic cleft"]', 1, 'The book defines a nerve impulse as a wave of electrochemical changes travelling along a neuron.', '{"0": "The impulse is a wave of ion movements across the membrane, not an electron current.", "2": "Hormone release is chemical coordination.", "3": "That is synaptic transmission, not the impulse itself."}'),
  ('BIO-5044', 'Coordination and control', 'Action potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 31 (§15.3.1)', 'During depolarization of a neuron, the membrane potential changes from –70 mV to a peak of about', '["–90 mV", "0 mV", "+50 mV", "+120 mV"]', 2, 'Na⁺ rushes in and the potential moves from –70 mV through zero to about 50 mV; this lasts about one millisecond in a region of the neuron.', '{"0": "A value more negative than resting occurs during hyperpolarization, not depolarization.", "1": "The potential passes zero and reverses; it does not stop there.", "3": "120 is the conduction speed in m/s of myelinated neurons, not a voltage."}'),
  ('BIO-5045', 'Coordination and control', 'Membrane potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 31 (§15.3.1)', 'Which of the following is NOT one of the factors the book gives for establishing the resting membrane potential?', '["Fixed anions such as proteins that cannot leave the cell", "The sodium–potassium pump moving 3 Na⁺ out for 2 K⁺ in", "Leakage of some K⁺ out of the cell through K⁺ gates", "Rapid inward movement of Na⁺ through open Na⁺ gates"]', 3, 'Na⁺ rushing in through open gates causes depolarization; RMP is set by fixed anions, the Na⁺–K⁺ pump and leakage of K⁺ out through K⁺ gates.', '{"0": "This is one of the three factors listed.", "1": "This is one of the three factors listed.", "2": "This is one of the three factors listed."}'),
  ('BIO-5046', 'Coordination and control', 'Action potential', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 31 (§15.3.1)', 'After the refractory period, the original ionic distribution across the neuron membrane is restored by', '["neurotransmitters binding to the axon", "the sodium–potassium pump", "calcium ions entering the synaptic knob", "opening of Na⁺ gates"]', 1, 'The sodium–potassium pump restores the original ion distribution so the membrane returns to –70 mV.', '{"0": "Neurotransmitters act on the postsynaptic membrane, not to restore the resting potential.", "2": "This triggers neurotransmitter release at a synapse.", "3": "This causes depolarization."}'),
  ('BIO-5047', 'Coordination and control', 'Propagation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 33 (§15.3.2)', 'Saltatory conduction in myelinated axons can be up to about how many times faster than impulses in the fastest non-myelinated axons?', '["50 times", "1000 times", "2 times", "10 times"]', 0, 'The book says saltatory conduction is up to 50 times faster; non-myelinated neurons conduct at 1–3 m/s, myelinated at up to 120 m/s.', '{"1": "This overstates the difference.", "2": "This greatly understates the difference.", "3": "The book gives a figure of up to 50 times."}'),
  ('BIO-5048', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 34 (§15.4.2)', 'Which group of excitatory neurotransmitters also functions as hormones?', '["GABA and glycine", "Fixed anions", "Biogenic amines", "Endorphins"]', 2, 'Biogenic amines (norepinephrine, epinephrine, dopamine, serotonin) are excitatory neurotransmitters that also act as hormones.', '{"0": "These are inhibitory neurotransmitters.", "1": "These are large negative molecules inside the neuron, not neurotransmitters.", "3": "Endorphins are inhibitory neurotransmitters released in the brain."}'),
  ('BIO-5049', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 34 (§15.4.2)', 'Norepinephrine, which regulates sleep patterns, focus and alertness, is made in the', '["CNS and sympathetic nerves", "intestine", "pineal gland", "posterior pituitary"]', 0, 'The book states that norepinephrine is made in the CNS and in sympathetic nerves.', '{"1": "About 90% of serotonin is made by intestinal neurons.", "2": "The pineal gland makes melatonin.", "3": "The posterior pituitary releases ADH and oxytocin."}'),
  ('BIO-5050', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 35 (§15.4.2)', 'Which neurotransmitter in the brain controls body movements and the pleasure linked to motivation?', '["Glycine", "GABA", "Acetylcholine", "Dopamine"]', 3, 'Dopamine controls body movements, motivation-related pleasure and emotional stimulation; loss of dopamine neurons causes Parkinson''s tremors.', '{"0": "Glycine is inhibitory, mainly in the spinal cord, brainstem and retina.", "1": "GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter and promotes relaxation.", "2": "Acetylcholine acts mainly at neuromuscular junctions and between many neurons."}'),
  ('BIO-5051', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 35 (§15.4.2)', 'Glycine acts as an inhibitory neurotransmitter especially in the', '["neuromuscular junctions of skeletal muscle", "spinal cord, brainstem and retina", "neurons of the intestine", "adrenal glands"]', 1, 'The book names glycine as an inhibitory neurotransmitter, especially in the spinal cord, brainstem and retina.', '{"0": "Acetylcholine, an excitatory transmitter, acts here.", "2": "Intestinal neurons make most of the body''s serotonin.", "3": "Epinephrine is released from the adrenal glands."}'),
  ('BIO-5052', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 35 (§15.4.2)', 'Many sedative drugs promote relaxation and sleep because they', '["block adenosine receptors", "increase Na⁺ entry into postsynaptic neurons", "enhance the binding of GABA to its receptors", "block the breakdown of acetylcholine"]', 2, 'Sedatives increase GABA binding, making this chief inhibitory neurotransmitter more effective at the synapse.', '{"0": "Blocking adenosine increases alertness, the opposite effect.", "1": "This would excite neurons, not calm them.", "3": "This is how nerve gases act, causing muscles to stay contracted."}'),
  ('BIO-5053', 'Coordination and control', 'Neurotransmitters', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 35 (§15.4.2)', 'Endorphins block pain signals by', '["increasing the release of glutamate", "destroying nociceptors in the skin", "opening Na⁺ gates in postsynaptic membranes", "reducing the release of excitatory neurotransmitters that carry pain messages"]', 3, 'Endorphins are inhibitory neurotransmitters that reduce release of the excitatory transmitters responsible for pain messages.', '{"0": "Glutamate is excitatory and would carry signals, not block them.", "1": "Endorphins act in the brain and do not destroy receptors.", "2": "This would generate impulses rather than block them."}'),
  ('BIO-5054', 'Coordination and control', 'Meninges', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 37 (§15.5.1)', 'Which meningeal layer adheres to the brain and spinal cord and contains capillaries that nourish them?', '["Corpus callosum", "Arachnoid", "Pia mater", "Dura mater"]', 2, 'The pia mater is a very delicate membrane on the surfaces of the brain and spinal cord with capillaries that nourish them.', '{"0": "This is a band of axons linking the cerebral hemispheres, not a meningeal layer.", "1": "The arachnoid is the thin middle layer acting as a barrier between CSF and blood.", "3": "The dura mater is the thick outermost layer with larger blood vessels."}'),
  ('BIO-5055', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 38 (§15.5.1)', 'Awareness of the position of the body and control of attention are functions of the', '["temporal lobes", "parietal lobes", "frontal lobes", "occipital lobes"]', 1, 'The parietal lobes process sensory information, give awareness of body position, control attention and plan movements.', '{"0": "The temporal lobes deal with visual memory, language comprehension and emotions.", "2": "The frontal lobes deal with executive functions such as planning and reasoning.", "3": "The occipital lobes control vision."}'),
  ('BIO-5056', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 38 (§15.5.1)', 'The visual cortex, which deals with information from the eyes, lies in the', '["occipital lobe", "temporal lobe", "frontal lobe", "cerebellum"]', 0, 'The book places the visual cortex in the occipital lobe and the auditory cortex in the temporal lobe.', '{"1": "The auditory cortex lies here.", "2": "This lobe handles executive functions.", "3": "The cerebellum coordinates muscle actions and posture."}'),
  ('BIO-5057', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 38 (§15.5.1)', 'A shallow groove on the surface of a cerebral hemisphere is called a', '["fissure", "ventricle", "gyrus", "sulcus"]', 3, 'Ridges are gyri; a shallow groove is a sulcus and a deep groove is a fissure.', '{"0": "A fissure is a deep groove.", "1": "A ventricle is a fluid-filled cavity inside the brain.", "2": "A gyrus is a ridge, not a groove."}'),
  ('BIO-5058', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 39 (§15.5.1)', 'Which part of the brainstem relays visual and auditory information and contains the reticular formation?', '["Thalamus", "Medulla oblongata", "Midbrain", "Pons"]', 2, 'The midbrain relays visual and auditory information and contains the reticular formation linking hindbrain and forebrain.', '{"0": "The thalamus is part of the diencephalon, not the brainstem.", "1": "The medulla controls heart rate and breathing.", "3": "The pons carries impulses between the cerebral hemispheres and the cerebellum."}'),
  ('BIO-5059', 'Coordination and control', 'Brain', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 40 (§15.5.1)', 'The third ventricle of the brain lies', '["in the cerebrum, as a pair of large cavities", "in the diencephalon, between the right and left thalamus", "in the centre of the spinal cord", "between the cerebellum and the brainstem"]', 1, 'There are two lateral ventricles in the cerebrum, the third in the diencephalon between the thalami, and the fourth between cerebellum and brainstem.', '{"0": "These are the two lateral ventricles.", "2": "This is the central canal, a continuation of the fourth ventricle.", "3": "This is the fourth ventricle."}'),
  ('BIO-5060', 'Coordination and control', 'Spinal cord', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 40 (§15.5.1)', 'The spinal cord is wider in the cervical and lumbar regions because these regions', '["handle sensory input and motor output for the arms and legs", "have no white matter", "are where the cranial nerves arise", "contain the medulla oblongata"]', 0, 'The cervical region serves the arms and trunk and the lumbar region serves the legs, so these regions are wider.', '{"1": "All regions of the cord have outer white matter.", "2": "Cranial nerves arise from the brain.", "3": "The medulla is part of the brainstem, above the spinal cord."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5061', 'Coordination and control', 'Cranial nerves', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 41 (§15.5.2)', 'The first two pairs of cranial nerves (olfactory and optic) arise from the', '["cerebrum", "spinal cord", "cerebellum", "brainstem"]', 0, 'The first two pairs arise from the cerebrum; the other 10 pairs arise from the brainstem.', '{"1": "Nerves arising from the spinal cord are spinal nerves.", "2": "No cranial nerves are described as arising from the cerebellum.", "3": "The remaining 10 pairs arise from the brainstem."}'),
  ('BIO-5062', 'Coordination and control', 'Cranial nerves', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 41 (§15.5.2)', 'Which cranial nerve is mixed, giving sensation in the face and controlling the muscles of chewing?', '["VII", "V", "XII", "III"]', 1, 'Cranial nerve V is mixed: sensation in the face and control of chewing muscles.', '{"0": "Nerve VII serves facial expressions and taste in the front of the tongue.", "2": "Nerve XII is motor and controls the tongue muscles.", "3": "Nerve III is motor and moves the eye."}'),
  ('BIO-5063', 'Coordination and control', 'Cranial nerves', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 41 (§15.5.2)', 'Taste in the back of the tongue and the muscles used in swallowing are served by cranial nerve', '["XI", "VII", "IX", "I"]', 2, 'Cranial nerve IX is mixed: taste in the back of the tongue and swallowing muscles.', '{"0": "Nerve XI controls neck and shoulder muscles.", "1": "Nerve VII serves taste in the front of the tongue.", "3": "Nerve I is the olfactory nerve for smell."}'),
  ('BIO-5064', 'Coordination and control', 'Spinal nerves', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 42 (§15.5.2)', 'Which statement about spinal nerves and their neurons is correct?', '["Some spinal nerves are purely sensory, like cranial nerve I", "The dorsal and ventral roots never join", "Somatic motor neuron cell bodies lie in the dorsal root ganglia", "All spinal nerves are mixed, and somatic motor neuron cell bodies lie inside the spinal cord"]', 3, 'All 31 pairs are mixed; sensory cell bodies lie in dorsal root ganglia, while somatic motor neuron cell bodies are within the spinal cord, not in ganglia.', '{"0": "The book states that all spinal nerves are mixed.", "1": "The two roots join just before the nerve leaves the vertebral column.", "2": "Dorsal root ganglia hold sensory neuron cell bodies."}'),
  ('BIO-5065', 'Coordination and control', 'Autonomic nervous system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 43 (§15.5.3)', 'The motor neurons of the parasympathetic nervous system originate from the', '["brain and sacral region of the spinal cord", "cerebellum only", "thoracic and lumbar regions of the spinal cord", "dorsal root ganglia"]', 0, 'Parasympathetic motor neurons arise from the brain and the sacral region; sympathetic ones arise from the thoracic and lumbar regions.', '{"1": "The cerebellum coordinates movement; it is not named as an origin.", "2": "This is the origin of sympathetic motor neurons.", "3": "These hold sensory neuron cell bodies."}'),
  ('BIO-5066', 'Coordination and control', 'Autonomic nervous system', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 43 (§15.5.3)', 'According to the table in the book, the urinary bladder responds to the autonomic nervous system by', '["contracting under both divisions", "relaxing under sympathetic and contracting under parasympathetic stimulation", "contracting under sympathetic and relaxing under parasympathetic stimulation", "showing no response to either division"]', 1, 'The table lists relaxation of the bladder for the sympathetic system and contraction for the parasympathetic system.', '{"0": "The two divisions have opposite effects on the bladder.", "2": "This reverses the effects in the table.", "3": "Both divisions affect the bladder."}'),
  ('BIO-5067', 'Coordination and control', 'Autonomic nervous system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 43 (§15.5.3)', 'Which effect does the parasympathetic nervous system have, according to the book''s table?', '["Stimulation of glucose secretion by the liver", "Decreased salivary secretion", "Increased motility of the digestive tract", "Dilation of the pupil"]', 2, 'The parasympathetic system increases digestive tract motility and secretions; the sympathetic system decreases them.', '{"0": "This is a sympathetic effect.", "1": "This is a sympathetic effect.", "3": "Pupil dilation is a sympathetic effect."}'),
  ('BIO-5068', 'Coordination and control', 'Receptors', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 44 (§15.6.1)', 'About how many taste buds do humans have, according to the book?', '["100", "1 million", "100 billion", "10,000"]', 3, 'The book states there are about 10,000 taste buds, mostly between the papillae of the tongue.', '{"0": "Far too few.", "1": "One million is the number of nephrons per kidney.", "2": "About 100 billion is the number of inter-neurons in the brain."}'),
  ('BIO-5069', 'Coordination and control', 'Receptors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 45 (§15.6.2)', 'In the olfactory epithelium, the cells that act as stem cells to replace old olfactory neurons are', '["supporting cells", "basal cells", "Merkel cells", "Schwann cells"]', 1, 'Olfactory neurons lie between supporting cells (protection) and basal cells, which act as stem cells.', '{"0": "Supporting cells provide protection.", "2": "Merkel cells are touch receptors in the epidermis.", "3": "Schwann cells make myelin in the PNS."}'),
  ('BIO-5070', 'Coordination and control', 'Receptors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 45 (§15.6.2)', 'Impulses from olfactory neurons reach the olfactory bulb through', '["the dorsal roots of spinal nerves", "cranial nerves VII, IX and X", "cranial nerve II", "cranial nerve I"]', 3, 'Smell impulses travel along the olfactory nerves (cranial nerve I) to the olfactory bulb, which produces the perception of smell.', '{"0": "Spinal nerves do not carry smell.", "1": "These carry taste impulses to the brainstem.", "2": "Cranial nerve II is the optic nerve for vision."}'),
  ('BIO-5071', 'Coordination and control', 'Receptors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 46 (§15.6.3)', 'Oval-shaped touch receptors found in the deepest layer of the epidermis, especially in fingertips, are', '["Nociceptors", "Ruffini endings", "Merkel cells", "Hair follicle receptors"]', 2, 'Merkel cells lie in the deepest layer of the epidermis and detect light touch in highly sensitive areas like fingertips.', '{"0": "Nociceptors are free nerve endings that detect pain.", "1": "Ruffini endings lie in the dermis and detect sustained pressure and stretching.", "3": "These lie around the base of hair follicles and detect hair movement."}'),
  ('BIO-5072', 'Coordination and control', 'Receptors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 45 (§15.6.3)', 'Thermoreceptors in the hypothalamus differ from those in the skin because they', '["detect the temperature of the blood and so monitor core body temperature", "only detect pain", "are enclosed in capsules in the dermis", "detect light touch and vibration"]', 0, 'Hypothalamic thermoreceptors detect blood temperature and monitor the body''s internal (core) temperature.', '{"1": "Pain is detected by nociceptors.", "2": "This describes Ruffini endings or Pacinian corpuscles.", "3": "These are functions of touch receptors such as Meissner''s corpuscles."}'),
  ('BIO-5073', 'Coordination and control', 'Drugs', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 47 (§15.7)', 'Which pair consists only of narcotic drugs, as defined in the book?', '["Caffeine and nicotine", "Nail polish remover and glue", "Alcohol and cannabis", "Codeine and oxycodone"]', 3, 'Narcotics are opioid drugs used for pain relief with high addiction potential, such as morphine, codeine and oxycodone.', '{"0": "These are psychoactive stimulants, not opioid narcotics.", "1": "These are inhalants.", "2": "These are psychoactive drugs but not opioid narcotics."}'),
  ('BIO-5074', 'Coordination and control', 'Drugs', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 47 (§15.7.1)', 'A state in which a person relies on a drug physically or emotionally in order to function is called', '["euphoria", "tolerance", "dependence", "withdrawal"]', 2, 'Dependence is relying on a drug physically or emotionally to function; it often leads to addiction.', '{"0": "Euphoria is intense happiness or excitement produced by some drugs.", "1": "Tolerance is reduced sensitivity, needing higher doses for the same effect.", "3": "Withdrawal is the set of symptoms when an addictive drug is stopped."}'),
  ('BIO-5075', 'Coordination and control', 'Drugs', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 48 (§15.7.1)', 'Nicotine produces pleasurable feelings because it', '["binds to nicotine receptors in the brain that release dopamine", "inhibits acetylcholinesterase", "blocks adenosine receptors", "resembles endorphins and binds their receptors"]', 0, 'Nicotine binds to nicotine receptors in the brain, which release dopamine and other neurotransmitters.', '{"1": "This is how nerve gases act.", "2": "This is how caffeine acts.", "3": "This describes opium derivatives such as morphine and heroin."}'),
  ('BIO-5076', 'Coordination and control', 'Drugs', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 48 (§15.7.2)', 'Heroin is an opioid drug derived from the', '["tobacco plant", "opium poppy plant", "Indian hemp plant, Cannabis sativa", "coffee plant"]', 1, 'Heroin is a highly addictive opioid derived from the opium poppy; it can cause respiratory depression.', '{"0": "Nicotine comes from tobacco.", "2": "Marijuana and hashish come from Cannabis sativa.", "3": "Caffeine is found in coffee and tea."}'),
  ('BIO-5077', 'Coordination and control', 'Disorders', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 49 (§15.8.1)', 'Aphasia, a common symptom of stroke, is', '["paralysis on the same side as the brain damage", "stiffness of the neck", "the inability to express oneself through words", "loss of the sense of smell"]', 2, 'The book defines aphasia as the inability to express through words.', '{"0": "Paralysis occurs on the opposite side, and it is not aphasia.", "1": "Neck stiffness is a symptom of meningitis.", "3": "This is not what aphasia means."}'),
  ('BIO-5078', 'Coordination and control', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 50 (§15.8.1)', 'A transient ischaemic attack (ministroke) is caused by', '["a temporary decrease in blood supply to part of the brain, which may last as little as five minutes", "permanent rupture of a large brain artery", "damage to the myelin sheath by the immune system", "bacterial infection of the meninges"]', 0, 'A TIA is a temporary decrease in blood supply to part of the brain that may last as little as five minutes.', '{"1": "A rupture causes a haemorrhagic stroke.", "2": "This is multiple sclerosis.", "3": "This is bacterial meningitis."}'),
  ('BIO-5079', 'Coordination and control', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 50 (§15.8.2)', 'Fungal meningitis usually occurs in', '["people who have had a stroke", "people with weak immune systems", "all healthy children", "people with Alzheimer''s disease only"]', 1, 'Fungal meningitis is rare and usually occurs in people with weak immune systems; it needs long-term antifungal treatment.', '{"0": "Stroke is a vascular disorder, not a cause of fungal infection.", "2": "It is rare and linked to weakened immunity.", "3": "The book links it to weak immunity, not Alzheimer''s."}'),
  ('BIO-5080', 'Coordination and control', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 50 (§15.8.3)', 'Brain tumours and spinal cord injuries are classified in the book as', '["functional disorders", "vascular disorders", "degenerative disorders", "structural disorders"]', 3, 'Structural disorders result from abnormalities or damage to the physical structure of the nervous system, such as brain tumours and spinal cord injuries.', '{"0": "Functional disorders, such as headache, have no apparent structural cause.", "1": "Vascular disorders, such as stroke, affect blood vessels.", "2": "Degenerative disorders, such as Alzheimer''s, involve progressive nerve cell deterioration."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5081', 'Coordination and control', 'Disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 51 (§15.8.4)', 'Migraine is classified in the book as', '["a degenerative disorder", "a primary headache", "a vascular disorder like stroke", "a secondary headache"]', 1, 'Primary headaches, such as migraine and tension headache, are due to the headache itself rather than another cause.', '{"0": "Degenerative disorders involve progressive nerve cell deterioration.", "2": "Headache is described under functional disorders.", "3": "Secondary headaches come from an underlying problem such as a tumour or bleeding."}'),
  ('BIO-5082', 'Coordination and control', 'Diagnostic tests', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 52 (§15.9.1)', 'On an EEG, which waves are associated with the normal alert state and active thinking?', '["Delta waves", "Alpha waves", "Beta waves", "Theta waves"]', 2, 'Beta waves go with normal alertness and active thinking; alpha with relaxation, theta and delta with sleep, gamma with high perception.', '{"0": "Delta waves show the deepest sleep.", "1": "Alpha waves show a physically and mentally relaxed state.", "3": "Theta waves show deep sleep with reduced consciousness."}'),
  ('BIO-5083', 'Coordination and control', 'Diagnostic tests', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 15 Human Nervous System, p. 53 (§15.9.2)', 'In a CT scanner, the doughnut-shaped part that contains a rotating X-ray tube is called the', '["gantry", "lithotripter", "electrode", "dialyzer"]', 0, 'The patient slides into the gantry, where an X-ray tube rotates and detectors record X-ray absorption.', '{"1": "A lithotripter generates shockwaves to break kidney stones.", "2": "Electrodes are attached to the scalp in EEG.", "3": "A dialyzer is the filter used in haemodialysis."}'),
  ('BIO-5084', 'Coordination and control', 'Chemical nature', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 59 (§16.1.1)', 'According to the book''s table, which thyroid hormone regulates body temperature and heart rate?', '["Norepinephrine", "Cortisol", "Calcitonin", "Tri-iodothyronine (T3)"]', 3, 'The table lists T3 as regulating body temperature and heart rate, and T4 as controlling the speed of metabolism.', '{"0": "Norepinephrine comes from the adrenal medulla, not the thyroid.", "1": "Cortisol comes from the adrenal cortex.", "2": "Calcitonin lowers blood calcium."}'),
  ('BIO-5085', 'Coordination and control', 'Hormones', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 59 (§16.1.1)', 'Where can receptors for hormones be found in a target cell?', '["On the cell membrane, in the cytoplasm or in the nucleus", "Only in the mitochondria", "Only on the cell membrane", "Only in the nucleus"]', 0, 'The book states that hormone receptors can be on the cell membrane, in the cytoplasm or in the nucleus.', '{"1": "Mitochondria are not named as hormone receptor sites.", "2": "Steroid hormones enter the cell, so receptors are also inside.", "3": "Peptide hormones bind to surface receptors."}'),
  ('BIO-5086', 'Coordination and control', 'Hypothalamus', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 61 (§16.2.1)', 'Thyrotropin releasing hormone (TRH) from the hypothalamus makes the anterior pituitary secrete', '["thyroxine", "thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH)", "growth hormone", "ACTH"]', 1, 'According to the table of hypothalamic hormones, TRH causes secretion of TSH.', '{"0": "Thyroxine is made by the thyroid gland in response to TSH.", "2": "GH secretion is triggered by growth hormone releasing hormone.", "3": "ACTH secretion is triggered by adrenocorticotropin releasing hormone."}'),
  ('BIO-5087', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 62 (§16.2.2)', 'Which statement about prolactin is correct according to the book?', '["It causes milk ejection by contracting smooth muscle", "Its secretion is increased by dopamine", "It is produced in both males and females but functions only in females", "It is stored and released by the posterior pituitary"]', 2, 'Prolactin stimulates milk production; it is made in both sexes but functions only in females.', '{"0": "Milk ejection is caused by oxytocin.", "1": "Dopamine is the prolactin inhibiting factor.", "3": "Prolactin is an anterior pituitary hormone."}'),
  ('BIO-5088', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 62 (§16.2.2)', 'The hypothalamus and posterior pituitary remain connected by a tract of axons because the posterior pituitary', '["makes its own hormones and sends them to the hypothalamus", "develops from the lining of the mouth like a gland", "is connected only through blood vessels", "develops in the embryo from an outgrowth of neural tissue"]', 3, 'During embryonic development the posterior pituitary forms from an outgrowth of neural tissue, so it stays linked to the hypothalamus by axons.', '{"0": "It makes no hormones; it stores those made by the hypothalamus.", "1": "The book describes a neural origin for the posterior pituitary.", "2": "Blood vessels link the hypothalamus with the anterior pituitary."}'),
  ('BIO-5089', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 62 (§16.2.2)', 'Melanocyte stimulating hormone (MSH) stimulates', '["the production and release of melanin by melanocytes in skin and hair", "the mammary glands to produce milk", "the bone marrow to produce red blood cells", "the thyroid to produce thyroxine"]', 0, 'MSH from the intermediate pituitary stimulates melanocytes to produce and release melanin.', '{"1": "This is the action of prolactin.", "2": "This is the action of erythropoietin.", "3": "This is the action of TSH."}'),
  ('BIO-5090', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 63 (§16.2.2)', 'Over-secretion of growth hormone in a growing child causes', '["acromegaly", "gigantism", "dwarfism", "cretinism"]', 1, 'In a growing child, excess GH causes gigantism (well-above-average height); in adults, it causes acromegaly.', '{"0": "Acromegaly is due to excess GH in adult life, when bones thicken rather than lengthen.", "2": "Dwarfism results from GH deficiency.", "3": "Cretinism is due to hypothyroidism in foetal life and childhood."}'),
  ('BIO-5091', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 63 (§16.2.2)', 'According to the book, over-secretion of oxytocin can cause', '["failure of normal labour", "gigantism", "diabetes insipidus", "rupture of the uterine wall"]', 3, 'Over-secretion of oxytocin can rupture the uterine wall, while under-secretion inhibits normal labour.', '{"0": "This results from under-secretion of oxytocin.", "1": "Gigantism results from excess growth hormone.", "2": "This is caused by under-secretion of ADH."}'),
  ('BIO-5092', 'Coordination and control', 'Pituitary', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 61 (§16.2.2)', 'Why does the book caution that growth hormone therapy may not be suitable for every person of short stature?', '["Growth hormone cannot be given to children", "Growth hormone always causes acromegaly in children", "Not every short person has growth hormone deficiency", "Growth hormone only acts on the thyroid"]', 2, 'GH treatment helps children who are short because of GH deficiency, but not every dwarf has GH deficiency, and doctors do not support GH for muscle building or slowing ageing.', '{"0": "The book says GH treatment is given to children with GH deficiency.", "1": "Excess GH in children causes gigantism, and therapy is aimed at deficient children.", "3": "GH acts on muscles, bones and other tissues."}'),
  ('BIO-5093', 'Coordination and control', 'Thyroid', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 63 (§16.2.3)', 'The thyroid gland is a butterfly-shaped gland located', '["on either side of the trachea in the front of the neck", "just above each kidney", "beneath the sternum between the lungs", "on the posterior surface of the parathyroid glands"]', 0, 'The thyroid has two connected lobes on either side of the trachea at the front of the neck.', '{"1": "This is where the adrenal glands are.", "2": "This is the location of the thymus.", "3": "The parathyroids lie on the posterior surface of the thyroid, not the reverse."}'),
  ('BIO-5094', 'Coordination and control', 'Thyroid', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 63 (§16.2.3)', 'Which is an effect of thyroid hormones T4 and T3 described in the book?', '["They lower blood calcium by depositing it in bones", "They enhance glucose catabolism and cholesterol synthesis in the liver", "They lower the heart rate and breathing rate", "They stimulate milk production"]', 1, 'T3 and T4 raise heart and breathing rates, set the basal metabolic rate, enhance glucose catabolism and cholesterol synthesis, and promote skeleton and brain development in children.', '{"0": "This is the action of calcitonin.", "2": "They increase both.", "3": "This is the action of prolactin."}'),
  ('BIO-5095', 'Coordination and control', 'Thyroid', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 64 (§16.2.3)', 'A person who has gained weight, feels lethargic, has constipation and a low heart rate most likely has', '["hyperthyroidism", "hypoglycaemia", "Cushing disease", "hypothyroidism"]', 3, 'Hypothyroidism causes growth retardation, lethargy, weight gain, constipation, high cholesterol, and low heart rate and body temperature.', '{"0": "Hyperthyroidism causes weight loss and high heart rate.", "1": "Hypoglycaemia is listed with lethargy, dizziness and nervousness; weight gain, constipation and low heart rate are listed for hypothyroidism.", "2": "Cushing disease causes high blood sugar, thin bones and muscle loss, among others."}'),
  ('BIO-5096', 'Coordination and control', 'Parathyroid', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 64 (§16.2.4)', 'Besides acting on bones and kidneys, parathormone raises blood calcium by', '["stimulating osteoblasts to lay down bone", "stimulating calcitonin release", "activating vitamin D, which is needed to absorb calcium from food", "increasing calcium excretion in urine"]', 2, 'Parathormone causes activation of vitamin D, needed for intestinal absorption of calcium.', '{"0": "It stimulates osteoclasts to dissolve bone matrix.", "1": "Calcitonin lowers blood calcium, opposing parathormone.", "3": "Parathormone makes the kidneys reabsorb calcium."}'),
  ('BIO-5097', 'Coordination and control', 'Parathyroid', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 64 (§16.2.4)', 'Over-secretion of parathormone, for example from a parathyroid tumour, leads to', '["goitre", "tetany due to hypocalcaemia", "soft, deformed bones and hypercalcaemia that depresses the nervous system", "increased excitability of neurons"]', 2, 'Excess parathormone releases too much calcium from bone, giving soft, deformed bones and hypercalcaemia, which depresses the nervous system and weakens muscles.', '{"0": "Goitre is a swelling of the thyroid due to iodine deficiency.", "1": "Tetany results from under-secretion of parathormone.", "3": "This is caused by hypocalcaemia from too little parathormone."}'),
  ('BIO-5098', 'Coordination and control', 'Pancreas', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 64 (§16.2.5)', 'In the islets of Langerhans, glucagon is secreted by', '["alpha cells", "beta cells", "interstitial cells", "exocrine cells of the pancreas"]', 0, 'Alpha cells secrete glucagon; the more numerous beta cells secrete insulin.', '{"1": "Beta cells secrete insulin.", "2": "Interstitial cells in the testes secrete testosterone.", "3": "These secrete pancreatic juice into the pancreatic duct."}'),
  ('BIO-5099', 'Coordination and control', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 65 (§16.2.5)', 'Which of the following is NOT an action of insulin described in the book?', '["Increasing glucose uptake by skeletal muscle and fat cells", "Stimulating gluconeogenesis", "Promoting conversion of excess glucose to fats", "Increasing the use of glucose in cellular respiration"]', 1, 'Insulin inhibits gluconeogenesis; it increases glucose uptake, promotes glycogenesis and the conversion of excess glucose to fat, and increases glucose use in respiration.', '{"0": "This is an action of insulin.", "2": "This is an action of insulin.", "3": "This is an action of insulin."}'),
  ('BIO-5100', 'Coordination and control', 'Pancreas', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 65 (§16.2.5)', 'People with Type II diabetes usually control their condition through', '["a kidney transplant", "surgical removal of the islets", "daily injections of insulin", "other medicines, diet and exercise"]', 3, 'Type II patients have normal insulin but reduced sensitivity, so they do not need insulin and use other medicines, diet and exercise.', '{"0": "Transplants treat end-stage kidney failure.", "1": "This would remove the source of insulin and worsen diabetes.", "2": "Daily insulin injections are the treatment for Type I diabetes."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5101', 'Coordination and control', 'Adrenal glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 66 (§16.2.6)', 'According to the book, over-secretion of the adrenal medulla hormones may cause', '["diabetes insipidus", "Cushing disease", "Addison''s disease", "hypertension and aggressive behaviour in routine life"]', 3, 'Over-secretion of epinephrine and norepinephrine may cause hypertension and aggressive behaviour; under-secretion causes failure to cope with emergencies.', '{"0": "This results from too little ADH.", "1": "Cushing disease results from excess cortisol.", "2": "Addison''s disease results from under-secretion of cortisol."}'),
  ('BIO-5102', 'Coordination and control', 'Adrenal glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 67 (§16.2.6)', 'Which group of features would be expected in Cushing disease?', '["High blood sugar, high blood pressure, thin bones and muscle loss", "Low blood sugar, weight loss and protruding eyeballs", "Excessive dilute urine and frequent thirst", "Muscle weakness and loss of salts that develop slowly"]', 0, 'Excess cortisol causes excessive protein breakdown, with high blood sugar, high blood pressure, thin bones, muscle loss, fragile skin and depression.', '{"1": "Protruding eyes and weight loss suggest Graves'' disease.", "2": "This describes diabetes insipidus.", "3": "This describes Addison''s disease."}'),
  ('BIO-5103', 'Coordination and control', 'Adrenal glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 66 (§16.2.6)', 'Aldosterone is classed among the corticosteroids as a', '["glycoprotein hormone", "mineralocorticoid", "glucocorticoid", "biogenic amine"]', 1, 'Aldosterone is a mineralocorticoid that regulates mineral balance; cortisol is a glucocorticoid.', '{"0": "Aldosterone is a steroid, not a glycoprotein.", "2": "Glucocorticoids, such as cortisol, maintain glucose homeostasis.", "3": "Biogenic amines include epinephrine and norepinephrine from the medulla."}'),
  ('BIO-5104', 'Coordination and control', 'Gonads', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 67 (§16.2.7)', 'Besides the corpus luteum, progesterone is produced and released during pregnancy by the', '["interstitial cells of the testes", "anterior pituitary", "placenta", "Graafian follicle"]', 2, 'Progesterone comes from the corpus luteum under LH, and also from the placenta during pregnancy.', '{"0": "These secrete testosterone.", "1": "The anterior pituitary secretes FSH and LH, not progesterone.", "3": "The Graafian follicle secretes estrogen in response to FSH."}'),
  ('BIO-5105', 'Coordination and control', 'Gonads', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 67 (§16.2.7)', 'Which is listed in the book as a function of estrogen?', '["Suppressing ovulation", "Causing milk ejection", "Repair of the uterine lining after menstruation", "Raising LDL (bad) cholesterol"]', 2, 'Estrogen develops secondary sexual characteristics, regulates the menstrual cycle, repairs the uterine lining, maintains bone density and lowers LDL, among others.', '{"0": "Progesterone suppresses ovulation.", "1": "Oxytocin causes milk ejection.", "3": "Estrogen lowers LDL."}'),
  ('BIO-5106', 'Coordination and control', 'Gonads', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 68 (§16.2.7)', 'Over-secretion of estrogen may lead to', '["sterility in adult females", "fibroids in the uterus and polycystic ovaries", "feminine characteristics in males", "miscarriage"]', 1, 'The book states that excess estrogen may lead to uterine fibroids and polycystic ovaries; deficiency leads to sterility in adults.', '{"0": "This is caused by estrogen deficiency.", "2": "This results from under-secretion of testosterone.", "3": "Miscarriage is linked with low progesterone in pregnancy."}'),
  ('BIO-5107', 'Coordination and control', 'Gonads', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 68 (§16.2.7)', 'Under-secretion of testosterone in males causes', '["acromegaly", "hypercalcaemia", "early menstruation", "development of feminine characteristics and male sterility"]', 3, 'Testosterone deficiency in males leads to feminine characteristics and sterility, since testosterone is needed for normal sperm production.', '{"0": "Acromegaly is due to excess GH in adults.", "1": "Hypercalcaemia results from excess parathormone.", "2": "This is linked with low progesterone in females."}'),
  ('BIO-5108', 'Coordination and control', 'Gonads', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 68 (§16.2.7)', 'Which is a long-term effect of anabolic steroid use listed in the book?', '["Liver damage leading to hepatitis and liver tumours", "Lower risk of stroke", "Prevention of depression", "Permanent increase in fertility"]', 0, 'Anabolic steroids can damage the liver (hepatitis, tumours), raise the risk of heart disease and clots, and cause infertility and mental health problems.', '{"1": "They increase the risk of stroke and blood clots.", "2": "They can cause depression and aggression.", "3": "They can cause infertility and impotence."}'),
  ('BIO-5109', 'Coordination and control', 'Other glands', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 68 (§16.2.8)', 'The thymus gland, which secretes thymosin, is located', '["attached to the hypothalamus by the infundibulum", "on top of each kidney", "beneath the sternum, between the lungs", "in the front of the neck on either side of the trachea"]', 2, 'The thymus lies beneath the sternum between the lungs; thymosin stimulates T cell maturation.', '{"0": "This is the pituitary gland.", "1": "The adrenal glands sit on the kidneys.", "3": "This is the thyroid."}'),
  ('BIO-5110', 'Coordination and control', 'Other glands', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 69 (§16.2.8)', 'When food is eaten, endocrine cells in the stomach wall secrete', '["gastrin, which stimulates the gastric glands to release gastric juice", "secretin, which stimulates the gallbladder", "insulin, which lowers blood glucose", "renin, which controls aldosterone production"]', 0, 'Gastrin from the stomach wall stimulates gastric glands; secretin and cholecystokinin come from the small intestine wall.', '{"1": "Secretin comes from the small intestine and stimulates the pancreas.", "2": "Insulin comes from beta cells of the pancreas.", "3": "Renin is produced by the kidneys."}'),
  ('BIO-5111', 'Coordination and control', 'Other glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 69 (§16.2.8)', 'Which kidney hormone controls the production of aldosterone in the adrenal glands?', '["Erythropoietin", "Renin", "ADH", "Calcitriol"]', 1, 'The kidneys produce renin, which controls aldosterone production; they also make erythropoietin and calcitriol.', '{"0": "Erythropoietin stimulates red blood cell production.", "2": "ADH is made by the hypothalamus.", "3": "Calcitriol raises blood calcium by increasing intestinal calcium absorption."}'),
  ('BIO-5112', 'Coordination and control', 'Other glands', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 69 (§16.2.8)', 'Calcitriol, a hormone produced by the kidneys, raises blood calcium by', '["depositing calcium into bones", "increasing calcium loss in urine", "stimulating red blood cell production", "increasing absorption of calcium from the intestines"]', 3, 'The book states that calcitriol increases blood calcium by absorption of calcium from the intestines.', '{"0": "This lowers blood calcium; it is the action of calcitonin.", "1": "This would lower blood calcium.", "2": "This is the role of erythropoietin."}'),
  ('BIO-5113', 'Coordination and control', 'Feedback', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 16 Human Endocrine System, p. 70 (§16.3)', 'What stops the alpha cells of the islets from releasing glucagon?', '["The return of blood glucose to its normal level", "A fall in blood glucose below normal", "Stronger uterine contractions", "A rise in oxytocin"]', 0, 'Glucagon release is controlled by negative feedback: once blood glucose rises to normal, the alpha cells stop releasing glucagon.', '{"1": "A fall in glucose stimulates glucagon release.", "2": "This is part of the positive feedback of oxytocin.", "3": "Oxytocin is involved in labour and milk ejection."}'),
  ('BIO-5114', 'Reproduction', 'Male system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 73 (§17.1.1)', 'According to the book, each human testis measures about', '["1–2 mm in length", "3–5 cm in length and 2–3 cm in width", "8–10 cm in length", "25 cm in length"]', 1, 'Each oval testis is about 3–5 cm long and 2–3 cm wide (the ovary is given the same size).', '{"0": "1–2 mm is the spacing of nodes of Ranvier.", "2": "8–10 cm is the length of the vagina.", "3": "About 25 cm is the length of a ureter."}'),
  ('BIO-5115', 'Reproduction', 'Male system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 74 (§17.1.1)', 'Where do the testes form during development?', '["In the scrotum from the start", "In the inguinal canal, where they stay throughout life", "In the pelvic cavity beside the bladder, where they stay", "Within the abdominal cavity, descending into the scrotum shortly before birth"]', 3, 'The testes form within the abdominal cavity and descend through an opening into the scrotum shortly before birth.', '{"0": "They form in the abdomen and only later descend.", "1": "The inguinal canal is the route of the vas deferens into the abdomen.", "2": "They descend into the scrotum, outside the abdomen."}'),
  ('BIO-5116', 'Reproduction', 'Male system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 74 (§17.1.1)', 'The two ejaculatory ducts open into the urethra after passing through the', '["bulbourethral glands", "seminal vesicles", "prostate gland", "epididymis"]', 2, 'The ejaculatory ducts from both testes pass through the prostate gland, where they open into the urethra.', '{"0": "These small glands add lubricating fluid; the ducts do not pass through them.", "1": "Each seminal vesicle''s duct joins the vas deferens to form the ejaculatory duct.", "3": "The epididymis is earlier in the pathway, before the vas deferens."}'),
  ('BIO-5117', 'Reproduction', 'Male system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 75 (§17.1.1)', 'According to the book, the penis contains the urethra, erectile tissue and', '["an artery and two veins", "the prostate gland", "the ejaculatory ducts only", "the seminiferous tubules"]', 0, 'The book states the penis contains the urethra, erectile tissue, an artery and two veins.', '{"1": "The prostate lies just below the urinary bladder.", "2": "The ejaculatory ducts open into the urethra inside the prostate.", "3": "These are inside the testes."}'),
  ('BIO-5118', 'Reproduction', 'Spermatogenesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 75 (§17.1.1)', 'In the walls of the seminiferous tubules, germline cells (2n) divide by mitosis. Some of the new cells become spermatogonia, while the others', '["become secondary spermatocytes directly", "remain as germline cells for future use", "die immediately", "become Sertoli cells"]', 1, 'Germline cells divide by mitosis; some daughter cells become spermatogonia and others stay as germline cells for future use.', '{"0": "Secondary spermatocytes come from meiosis I of primary spermatocytes.", "2": "The book says they are kept for future use.", "3": "Sertoli cells are a separate cell type that nourishes sperms."}'),
  ('BIO-5119', 'Reproduction', 'Sperm structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 75 (§17.1.1)', 'The tail of a sperm is formed of microtubules (axial filament) that arise from the', '["mitochondria", "nucleus in the head", "centriole of the midpiece", "acrosome"]', 2, 'The tail is made of microtubules (axial filament) arising from the centriole in the midpiece.', '{"0": "Mitochondria in the midpiece supply energy but do not form the tail.", "1": "The head holds the genetic material, not the origin of the tail.", "3": "The acrosome is a cap over the head, formed from the Golgi body."}'),
  ('BIO-5120', 'Reproduction', 'Sperm structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 75 (§17.1.1)', 'About how much of the sperm head is covered by the acrosome?', '["None; the acrosome is in the tail", "The whole head", "One-tenth", "Two-thirds"]', 3, 'About two-thirds of the head is surrounded by a thick cap called the acrosome.', '{"0": "The acrosome is a cap on the head.", "1": "It covers about two-thirds, not all of it.", "2": "The book says the acrosome covers far more of the head."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5121', 'Reproduction', 'Male system', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 75 (§17.1.1)', 'Mature spermatozoa in the lumen of the seminiferous tubules are non-motile. They reach the epididymis', '["carried by cilia lining the vas deferens", "carried by fluid secreted by Sertoli cells", "pushed by contractions of the urinary bladder", "by beating their tails"]', 1, 'Non-motile spermatozoa enter the epididymis with the help of fluid secreted by Sertoli cells; they gain motility in the epididymis.', '{"0": "The vas deferens comes after the epididymis.", "2": "The bladder is not involved in sperm transport from the testis.", "3": "They are not yet motile at this stage."}'),
  ('BIO-5122', 'Reproduction', 'Hormonal control (male)', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 76 (§17.1.1)', 'In the hormonal control of the male reproductive system, testosterone', '["stimulates the development of the germinal epithelium and maintains secondary sexual characteristics", "stimulates Sertoli cells to release inhibin", "is secreted by the seminal vesicles", "increases the release of GnRH and LH"]', 0, 'Testosterone from Leydig cells stimulates development of the germinal epithelium and maintains male secondary sexual characteristics.', '{"1": "FSH causes Sertoli cells to release inhibin.", "2": "It is secreted by Leydig cells.", "3": "Testosterone inhibits the release of LH and GnRH."}'),
  ('BIO-5123', 'Reproduction', 'Female system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.2)', 'Which are the accessory organs of the female reproductive system?', '["Ovaries, oviducts and uterus", "Ovaries only", "Oviducts, uterus and vagina", "Seminal vesicles and prostate"]', 2, 'The female system consists of gonads (ovaries), oviducts, uterus and vagina; the last three are called accessory organs.', '{"0": "The ovaries are the gonads, not accessory organs.", "1": "The ovaries are the gonads.", "3": "These are male accessory glands."}'),
  ('BIO-5124', 'Reproduction', 'Female system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.2)', 'After fertilization in the oviduct, the zygote', '["undergoes meiosis to form four haploid cells", "returns to the ovary to develop", "immediately forms the corpus luteum", "undergoes several mitotic divisions while still in the oviduct"]', 3, 'The book states that the zygote undergoes several mitotic divisions while still in the oviduct.', '{"0": "A zygote divides by mitosis, not meiosis.", "1": "It travels toward the uterus, not back to the ovary.", "2": "The corpus luteum forms from the ruptured follicle."}'),
  ('BIO-5125', 'Reproduction', 'Female system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.2)', 'The outermost thin layer of the uterine wall is the', '["perimetrium", "cervix", "myometrium", "endometrium"]', 0, 'The uterine wall has the perimetrium (outer, thin), myometrium (middle, smooth muscle) and endometrium (inner, spongy).', '{"1": "The cervix is the lower narrow part of the uterus, not a layer.", "2": "This is the thick middle layer of smooth muscle.", "3": "This is the inner spongy lining."}'),
  ('BIO-5126', 'Reproduction', 'Female system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 77 (§17.1.2)', 'During pregnancy, the placenta forms when', '["the basal layer of the endometrium is shed", "the corpus luteum moves to the uterus", "the myometrium turns into glandular tissue", "vascular spaces of the thickened functional layer of the endometrium fuse and interconnect"]', 3, 'The functional layer thickens to implant the embryo; its vascular spaces fuse and interconnect to form the placenta.', '{"0": "The basal layer holds stem cells and is not shed.", "1": "The corpus luteum stays in the ovary.", "2": "The myometrium is smooth muscle and does not form the placenta."}'),
  ('BIO-5127', 'Reproduction', 'Oogenesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 78 (§17.1.2)', 'During embryonic development of a female, primary oocytes (2n) are produced from oogonia by', '["meiosis I", "mitosis", "meiosis II", "binary fission"]', 1, 'Oogonia (2n) in follicles divide by mitosis to produce primary oocytes (2n), which then begin meiosis I.', '{"0": "Meiosis I begins in the primary oocytes after they form.", "2": "Meiosis II is completed by the secondary oocyte only after a sperm fuses with it.", "3": "Binary fission is a prokaryotic division."}'),
  ('BIO-5128', 'Reproduction', 'Oogenesis', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 78 (§17.1.2)', 'At which stage is the secondary oocyte arrested when it is released at ovulation?', '["Interphase", "Prophase I", "Metaphase II", "Telophase II"]', 2, 'The secondary oocyte stops division in metaphase II and completes meiosis II only if a sperm fuses with it.', '{"0": "The book places the arrest in metaphase II.", "1": "Primary oocytes are arrested in prophase I through childhood.", "3": "Telophase II would mean meiosis II is nearly finished, which happens only after a sperm enters."}'),
  ('BIO-5129', 'Reproduction', 'Oogenesis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 79 (§17.1.2)', 'If an ovulated secondary oocyte is not fertilized by a sperm, it', '["completes meiosis II and becomes an ovum", "returns to prophase I", "forms a corpus luteum", "dies"]', 3, 'The book states that an unfertilized ovulated secondary oocyte dies.', '{"0": "Meiosis II is completed only if a sperm fuses with it.", "1": "Meiotic arrest cannot be reversed this way.", "2": "The corpus luteum forms from the ruptured follicle, not the oocyte."}'),
  ('BIO-5130', 'Reproduction', 'Oogenesis', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 79 (§17.1.2)', 'If 10 primary spermatocytes and 10 primary oocytes complete meiosis (with fertilization for the oocytes), how many sperms and ova are formed?', '["10 sperms and 10 ova", "40 sperms and 40 ova", "40 sperms and 10 ova", "20 sperms and 20 ova"]', 2, 'Each primary spermatocyte gives four spermatids (sperms), while each primary oocyte gives one ovum and polar bodies.', '{"0": "Each primary spermatocyte gives four sperms, not one.", "1": "Each primary oocyte gives only one ovum; the other products are polar bodies.", "3": "Two cells come from meiosis I only; meiosis II doubles the sperm count again, and oocytes give one ovum each."}'),
  ('BIO-5131', 'Reproduction', 'Menstrual cycle', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 79 (§17.1.3)', 'According to the book, menstruation in the menstrual phase continues for about', '["14 days", "3–5 days", "1 day", "28 days"]', 1, 'The functional layer is shed during menstruation, which continues for 3–5 days.', '{"0": "Day 14 is the time of ovulation.", "2": "The book gives 3–5 days.", "3": "28 days is the average length of the whole cycle."}'),
  ('BIO-5132', 'Reproduction', 'Menstrual cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 79 (§17.1.3)', 'The sudden release of LH that causes ovulation occurs', '["at the end of the proliferative phase, around day 14", "at the start of the menstrual phase, on day 1", "on day 28, when the corpus luteum degenerates", "in the middle of the secretory phase, around day 21"]', 0, 'As estrogen rises, FSH falls by negative feedback and the anterior pituitary releases LH at the end of the proliferative phase (day 14), causing ovulation.', '{"1": "Estrogen and progesterone are low then and the cycle is just restarting.", "2": "Degeneration of the corpus luteum leads to menstruation, not ovulation.", "3": "Ovulation has already happened by the secretory phase."}'),
  ('BIO-5133', 'Reproduction', 'Menstrual cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 17 Human Reproductive Systems, p. 80 (§17.1.3)', 'If the ovulated oocyte is fertilized, human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) from the embryo prevents menstruation by', '["increasing FSH secretion", "destroying the corpus luteum", "maintaining the corpus luteum, so estrogen and progesterone levels stay high", "stimulating a new Graafian follicle to ovulate"]', 2, 'hCG maintains the corpus luteum, so estrogen and progesterone stay high and menstruation is prevented.', '{"0": "High FSH would start new follicles growing, which does not happen in pregnancy.", "1": "Destruction of the corpus luteum causes menstruation.", "3": "Progesterone suppresses ovulation during pregnancy."}'),
  ('BIO-5134', 'Inheritance', 'Law of segregation', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 86 (§18.1)', 'In modern terms, the book defines a gene as', '["the particular location of a gene on a chromosome", "a sequence of nucleotides in DNA that codes for the formation of a polypeptide", "the observable trait of an organism", "the combination of alleles in an individual"]', 1, 'The book defines a gene as a sequence of nucleotides, part of DNA, that codes for a polypeptide.', '{"0": "This is the locus.", "2": "The observable trait is the phenotype.", "3": "This is the genotype."}'),
  ('BIO-5135', 'Inheritance', 'Testcross', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 86 (§18.1)', 'A round-seeded pea plant is testcrossed with a wrinkled-seeded plant (rr). If the round plant is heterozygous (Rr), the offspring will be', '["round and wrinkled in a 1:1 ratio", "all wrinkled", "round and wrinkled in a 3:1 ratio", "all round"]', 0, 'A heterozygous Rr parent gives R and r gametes equally; with rr they give Rr (round) and rr (wrinkled) in a 1:1 ratio.', '{"1": "At least half receive the dominant R allele.", "2": "3:1 comes from crossing two heterozygotes, Rr × Rr.", "3": "All round offspring show the parent was RR."}'),
  ('BIO-5136', 'Inheritance', 'Independent assortment', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 86 (§18.2)', 'The F1 plants of Mendel''s cross RRYY × rryy are called dihybrids because they are', '["homozygous dominant for both genes", "pure-breeding for both traits", "crosses between two different species", "heterozygous for both genes"]', 3, 'The F1 (RrYy) plants are heterozygous for two genes, so they are called dihybrids.', '{"0": "Their genotype is RrYy, not RRYY.", "1": "Heterozygotes do not breed true.", "2": "Both parents are pea plants; dihybrid refers to two genes."}'),
  ('BIO-5137', 'Inheritance', 'Probability', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 89 (§18.2.2)', 'In a cross RrYy × RrYy, the book uses the product rule to find the probability of round, yellow offspring. The answer is', '["1/16", "3/16", "9/16", "3/4"]', 2, 'Probability of round = 3/4 and of yellow = 3/4; independent events multiply: 3/4 × 3/4 = 9/16.', '{"0": "This is the probability of wrinkled green (1/4 × 1/4).", "1": "This is the probability of round green (3/4 × 1/4) or wrinkled yellow.", "3": "This is the probability of only one of the two traits."}'),
  ('BIO-5138', 'Inheritance', 'Probability', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 89 (§18.2.2)', 'In a cross Aa × Aa, the book uses the sum rule to show that the probability of the dominant phenotype is 3/4 because', '["1/2 × 1/2 = 3/4 by the product rule", "AA, Aa and aA each have a probability of 1/4, and these mutually exclusive outcomes are added", "each parent passes on A with a probability of 3/4", "the recessive allele is never passed on"]', 1, 'The dominant phenotype can arise in three mutually exclusive ways (AA, Aa, aA), each 1/4; adding gives 3/4.', '{"0": "1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4, which is the chance of one particular genotype such as aa.", "2": "Each parent passes on A with a probability of 1/2.", "3": "Each parent passes on a with a probability of 1/2."}'),
  ('BIO-5139', 'Inheritance', 'Dominance', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 90 (§18.3)', 'In a Yy pea plant, the Y allele is fully expressed and y not at all, so no intermediate colour appears. This is', '["complete dominance", "incomplete dominance", "epistasis", "codominance"]', 0, 'When the dominant allele is fully expressed and the recessive not at all in a heterozygote, it is complete dominance.', '{"1": "In incomplete dominance the heterozygote is intermediate, such as pink four o''clock flowers.", "2": "Epistasis involves alleles of different genes.", "3": "In codominance both alleles are fully expressed together."}'),
  ('BIO-5140', 'Inheritance', 'Dominance', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 91 (§18.3.2)', 'The MN blood group system was discovered in 1927 by', '["Bayliss and Starling", "Landsteiner alone, in 1901", "Morgan and Sutton", "Landsteiner and Levine"]', 3, 'The ''L'' in Lᴹ and Lᴺ stands for Landsteiner and Levine, who discovered the system in 1927.', '{"0": "They discovered the hormone secretin.", "1": "Landsteiner discovered the ABO system in 1901.", "2": "Morgan worked on Drosophila linkage and sex linkage; Sutton proposed the chromosomal theory."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5141', 'Inheritance', 'Blood groups', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 92 (§18.4)', 'Which of the following blood group systems usually does NOT cause matching problems in blood transfusion, according to the book?', '["Rh", "Both ABO and Rh", "Duffy", "ABO"]', 2, 'The book lists MNS, Lutheran, Kell, Lewis and Duffy as other major systems that usually do not cause transfusion matching problems; ABO and Rh must be matched.', '{"0": "Rh must be matched for transfusion.", "1": "Both of these must be matched.", "3": "ABO must be matched for transfusion."}'),
  ('BIO-5142', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 93 (§18.4.1)', 'Some people secrete antigens A and B in body fluids such as saliva and tears because they possess', '["a dominant secretor gene, Se, on chromosome 19", "the genotype hh", "the I gene on chromosome 9", "the Rh allele d"]', 0, 'The book states that secretors have a dominant secretor gene ''Se'' on chromosome 19.', '{"1": "hh prevents formation of H substance and gives the Bombay phenotype.", "2": "The I gene determines which ABO antigens are made, not whether they are secreted.", "3": "The d allele means no Rh antigen."}'),
  ('BIO-5143', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 93 (§18.4.1)', 'A man with blood group A and his wife with blood group B have four children, each with a different blood group (A, B, AB and O). The parents'' genotypes must be', '["IᴬIᴬ and IᴮIᴮ", "Iᴬi and Iᴮi", "Iᴬi and IᴮIᴮ", "IᴬIᴬ and Iᴮi"]', 1, 'To produce an O child (ii) each parent must carry i; Iᴬi × Iᴮi gives Iᴬi, Iᴮi, IᴬIᴮ and ii.', '{"0": "All children would be AB.", "2": "No child could be group A or O.", "3": "No child could be group B or O."}'),
  ('BIO-5144', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 93 (§18.4.1)', 'The plasma of a person with blood group O contains', '["anti-Rh antibodies from birth", "neither anti-A nor anti-B antibodies", "anti-B antibodies only", "both anti-A and anti-B antibodies"]', 3, 'Genotype ii makes neither antigen, so by Landsteiner''s rule the plasma has antibodies against both A and B.', '{"0": "Anti-Rh antibodies are not naturally present.", "1": "This describes blood group AB.", "2": "This describes blood group A."}'),
  ('BIO-5145', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 93 (§18.4.1)', 'In the ABO system, the letter ''I'' of the gene I stands for', '["iso-haem-agglutinogen", "insulin", "inhibitor", "immunoglobulin"]', 0, 'The book states that I stands for iso-haem-agglutinogen.', '{"1": "Insulin is a hormone unrelated to blood groups.", "2": "The book does not say I stands for inhibitor.", "3": "Antibodies are immunoglobulins, but that is not what I stands for here."}'),
  ('BIO-5146', 'Inheritance', 'ABO system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 94 (§18.4.1)', 'A patient of blood group B receives blood from a donor of group AB. What happens?', '["No reaction, because AB is the universal donor", "Agglutination, because the donor''s anti-B antibodies attack the recipient", "No reaction, because both groups carry antigen B", "Agglutination, because the recipient''s anti-A antibodies attack the donor''s A antigens"]', 3, 'Group B plasma has anti-A antibodies, which react with the A antigens on AB red cells, causing clumping.', '{"0": "AB is the universal recipient; O is the universal donor.", "1": "Group AB plasma has no anti-A or anti-B antibodies.", "2": "The donor cells also carry antigen A, which the recipient''s antibodies attack."}'),
  ('BIO-5147', 'Inheritance', 'Erythroblastosis foetalis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 96 (§18.4.3)', 'In a surviving baby with erythroblastosis foetalis, the liver and spleen become enlarged because they', '["store excess anti-Rh antibodies", "are attacked by the mother''s anti-A antibodies", "try to make more red blood cells to replace those destroyed", "absorb bilirubin and stop jaundice"]', 2, 'The destruction of foetal RBCs causes the liver and spleen to enlarge as they try to make more RBCs.', '{"0": "Antibodies destroy RBCs; the organs enlarge from extra RBC production.", "1": "The disease is due to anti-Rh antibodies.", "3": "Bilirubin builds up and causes jaundice."}'),
  ('BIO-5148', 'Inheritance', 'Erythroblastosis foetalis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 96 (§18.4.3)', 'According to the book, the blood of a surviving baby with erythroblastosis foetalis is treated by', '["replacing it with Rh-positive blood containing anti-Rh antibodies", "replacing it with Rh-negative blood free of anti-Rh antibodies", "removing the liver and spleen", "giving the baby an injection of anti-Rh antibodies"]', 1, 'The surviving baby''s blood is replaced by Rh-negative blood that is free of anti-Rh antibodies.', '{"0": "Anti-Rh antibodies would keep destroying red cells.", "2": "This is not a treatment given in the book.", "3": "This would destroy more of the baby''s Rh-positive cells."}'),
  ('BIO-5149', 'Inheritance', 'Polygenic inheritance', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 97 (§18.5.1)', 'Which group of human traits is given in the book as polygenic?', '["Haemophilia and colour blindness", "ABO blood groups and MN blood groups", "Height, skin colour, weight and intelligence", "Hypertrichosis of the pinna and webbed toes"]', 2, 'Polygenic traits named include height, skin colour, weight, intelligence, and grain colour in plants.', '{"0": "These are X-linked traits controlled by single genes.", "1": "These are controlled by single genes with multiple or codominant alleles.", "3": "These are Y-linked traits."}'),
  ('BIO-5150', 'Inheritance', 'Polygenic inheritance', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 98 (§18.5.1)', 'In the F2 of dark red (AABBCC) × white (aabbcc) wheat, out of 64 plants how many show intermediate shades of red (neither darkest red nor white)?', '["62", "20", "32", "1"]', 0, 'Only one plant is darkest red and one is white; the other 62 show shades from moderate to very light red.', '{"1": "20 is the largest single class (the middle shade) in the 1:6:15:20:15:6:1 ratio, not the total of all shades.", "2": "This is not supported by the 1:6:15:20:15:6:1 distribution.", "3": "One plant is darkest red and one is white."}'),
  ('BIO-5151', 'Inheritance', 'Epistasis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 98 (§18.5.2)', 'The book gives which human example of an epistatic allele?', '["The allele Lᴹ, which is epistatic to Lᴺ", "The allele Iᴬ, which is epistatic to Iᴮ", "The allele D, which is epistatic to d", "The allele for baldness, which is epistatic to the alleles for black and blonde hair"]', 3, 'In humans, the baldness allele is epistatic to the alleles of black and blonde hair.', '{"0": "These are codominant alleles of one gene.", "1": "Iᴬ and Iᴮ are codominant alleles of the same gene.", "2": "D and d are alleles of the same Rh gene, showing dominance."}'),
  ('BIO-5152', 'Inheritance', 'Epistasis', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 99 (§18.5.2)', 'In Labrador retrievers, a dog with genotype bbEe has', '["a white coat", "a brown coat", "a black coat", "a yellow coat"]', 1, 'E allows pigment deposition, so the B gene shows: bb gives brown.', '{"0": "The book lists black, brown and yellow coats only.", "2": "Black needs at least one B allele.", "3": "Yellow is produced only by ee, which blocks pigment deposition."}'),
  ('BIO-5153', 'Inheritance', 'Epistasis', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 99 (§18.5.2)', 'The Bombay phenotype was discovered in 1952 and is mostly found in', '["Rhesus monkeys", "Northern Europe only", "India, Bangladesh, Pakistan and Iran", "Drosophila"]', 2, 'The book states the Bombay phenotype was discovered in Bombay in 1952 and is mostly found in India, Bangladesh, Pakistan and Iran.', '{"0": "The Rh antigen was first found in Rhesus monkeys.", "1": "The book places it mainly in South Asia and Iran.", "3": "Drosophila was used for linkage and sex-linkage studies."}'),
  ('BIO-5154', 'Inheritance', 'Epistasis', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 100 (§18.5.2)', 'In foxglove, what flower colour will a plant with genotype ddww have?', '["White", "Light red", "Dark red", "Pink"]', 1, 'ww allows uniform pigment distribution, and dd produces light red pigment, so the flowers are light red.', '{"0": "White needs at least one W allele, which limits pigment to small spots.", "2": "Dark red needs at least one D allele with ww.", "3": "Pink is the heterozygous colour in four o''clock plants, not foxglove."}'),
  ('BIO-5155', 'Inheritance', 'Linkage', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 100 (§18.6)', 'Mendel''s work was rediscovered in 1900 by the German geneticist', '["Karl Landsteiner", "Thomas Hunt Morgan", "Walter Sutton", "Karl Correns"]', 3, 'Karl Correns rediscovered Mendel''s work in 1900 and suggested the role of chromosomes in heredity.', '{"0": "Landsteiner discovered the ABO and Rh blood groups.", "1": "Morgan studied linkage and sex linkage in Drosophila.", "2": "Sutton proposed the chromosomal theory of inheritance in 1902."}'),
  ('BIO-5156', 'Inheritance', 'Linkage', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 100 (§18.6.1)', 'According to the book, humans have about 20,000 genes on 23 chromosomes. Drosophila has about', '["13,000 genes on 4 chromosomes", "13,000 genes on 8 pairs of chromosomes", "20,000 genes on 23 chromosomes", "60 genes on 3 chromosomes"]', 0, 'The book compares humans (about 20,000 genes on 23 chromosomes) with Drosophila (about 13,000 genes on 4 chromosomes), each present in two sets.', '{"1": "Drosophila has 4 chromosomes per set (3 autosomes and 1 sex chromosome).", "2": "These are the human figures.", "3": "About 60 genes control human skin colour; this is unrelated."}'),
  ('BIO-5157', 'Inheritance', 'Linkage', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 101 (§18.6.1)', 'A test cross for two genes produces offspring in a 1:1:1:1 ratio of parental and new combinations. This shows that the genes', '["show incomplete linkage", "are not linked and assort independently", "are on the Y chromosome", "show complete linkage"]', 1, 'Equal 1:1:1:1 parental and new phenotypes mean independent assortment; more parentals than recombinants means incomplete linkage; only parentals means complete linkage.', '{"0": "Incomplete linkage gives more parental than new combinations.", "2": "A 1:1:1:1 ratio says nothing about Y linkage.", "3": "Complete linkage gives only parental combinations."}'),
  ('BIO-5158', 'Inheritance', 'Crossing over', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 103 (§18.6.2)', 'After crossing over, a recombinant chromatid has', '["genes in a completely different order", "only maternal alleles", "half the number of genes", "the same genes in the same order, but with rearranged alleles"]', 3, 'DNA strands break and rejoin; the chromatids keep the same genes in the same order, but alleles are rearranged.', '{"0": "Gene order is unchanged by normal crossing over.", "1": "Crossing over mixes maternal and paternal alleles.", "2": "Sections are exchanged, so gene content is kept."}'),
  ('BIO-5159', 'Inheritance', 'Sex determination', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 103 (§18.7)', 'In the XX–XY system, the X and Y chromosomes are described in the book as', '["both rod-shaped and identical", "both ring-shaped", "rod-shaped X and hook-shaped Y", "hook-shaped X and rod-shaped Y"]', 2, 'The sex chromosome pair has a rod-shaped X and a hook-shaped Y chromosome.', '{"0": "They differ; that is why they are sex chromosomes and not autosomes.", "1": "The book does not describe them as ring-shaped.", "3": "This reverses the shapes."}'),
  ('BIO-5160', 'Inheritance', 'Sex determination', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 104 (§18.7)', 'In the grasshopper (XX–XO system), a sperm without any sex chromosome is called a', '["nullo gamete", "polar body", "homogametic sperm", "Y sperm"]', 0, 'A male XO grasshopper forms half its sperms with X and half without a sex chromosome (nullo gametes); fertilization by a nullo sperm gives an XO male.', '{"1": "Polar bodies are small cells formed in oogenesis.", "2": "Homogametic refers to the sex producing one kind of gamete, the XX female.", "3": "Grasshopper males have no Y chromosome."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5161', 'Inheritance', 'Sex linkage', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 106 (§18.8)', 'Morgan testcrossed an F1 red-eyed female Drosophila with a white-eyed male and obtained red- and white-eyed males and females in a 1:1:1:1 ratio. This showed that', '["the white-eye gene is on the Y chromosome", "the eye colour gene is on an autosome", "red eye is recessive to white eye", "white-eyed females can exist"]', 3, 'Getting white-eyed females in this testcross proved that a female could have white eyes.', '{"0": "Morgan concluded that the gene is on the X chromosome.", "1": "Later crosses showed it is X-linked.", "2": "His F1 results showed red is dominant."}'),
  ('BIO-5162', 'Inheritance', 'Embryonic screening', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 106 (§18.8)', 'In amniocentesis, the foetal cells taken from the amniotic fluid are', '["injected back into the mother to boost immunity", "examined only by ultrasound", "kept in a culture medium and checked for an abnormal number of chromosomes", "used to make Rh-antiserum"]', 2, 'The needle draws amniotic fluid containing foetal cells, which are cultured and checked for abnormal chromosome numbers.', '{"0": "This is not part of the procedure.", "1": "Ultrasound locates the foetus; the cells are cultured and examined.", "3": "Rh-antiserum is serum containing anti-Rh antibodies."}'),
  ('BIO-5163', 'Inheritance', 'X-linked traits', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 107 (§18.8.1)', 'Rett syndrome, with loss of speech and repetitive hand movements, is given in the book as an example of an', '["X-linked dominant trait", "X-linked recessive trait", "autosomal recessive trait", "Y-linked trait"]', 0, 'The book''s X-linked dominant examples are vitamin D-resistant rickets, Rett syndrome and Aicardi syndrome.', '{"1": "X-linked recessive examples are muscular dystrophy, colour blindness and haemophilia.", "2": "Haemophilia C is the book''s autosomal recessive example.", "3": "Y-linked examples are hypertrichosis of the pinna, porcupine man and webbed toes."}'),
  ('BIO-5164', 'Inheritance', 'Y-linked traits', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 108 (§18.8.1)', 'Y-linked genes that have no alleles on the X chromosome are called', '["pseudo-autosomal genes", "holandric genes", "polygenes", "hypostatic genes"]', 1, 'Genes only on the non-homologous region of the Y chromosome are holandric (hemizygous) genes, inherited from father to son.', '{"0": "Pseudo-autosomal genes have alleles on both X and Y.", "2": "Polygenes act together additively, as in skin colour.", "3": "Hypostatic alleles are masked by epistatic alleles."}'),
  ('BIO-5165', 'Inheritance', 'Y-linked traits', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 108 (§18.8.1)', 'The SRY gene on the human Y chromosome', '["produces a protein that starts development of testes and prevents female reproductive structures", "codes for the red and green opsins", "is found in both males and females", "causes haemophilia A"]', 0, 'SRY (Sex-determining Region Y) produces a protein that starts testis development and prevents development of female structures.', '{"1": "The opsin genes for red and green are on the X chromosome.", "2": "SRY is on the Y chromosome, so it is present in males.", "3": "Haemophilia A is due to abnormal factor VIII, an X-linked gene."}'),
  ('BIO-5166', 'Inheritance', 'Haemophilia', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 108 (§18.8.2)', 'What is the first step in normal blood clotting described in the book?', '["Fibrinogen is converted to fibrin", "Platelets adhere to the site of injury, forming a platelet plug", "Fibrin forms a mesh that traps blood", "Fibrin strands become cross-linked"]', 1, 'Platelets first form a plug; activated platelets and damaged tissue then release clotting factors, which convert fibrinogen to fibrin.', '{"0": "This happens in the cascade after clotting factors are released.", "2": "This follows the clotting cascade.", "3": "Cross-linking stabilizes the clot at the end."}'),
  ('BIO-5167', 'Inheritance', 'Colour blindness', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 109 (§18.8.2)', 'The gene for the blue opsin of cone cells is located on', '["the Y chromosome", "chromosome 9", "the X chromosome", "autosome 7"]', 3, 'Red and green opsin genes are on the X chromosome, while the blue opsin gene is on autosome 7.', '{"0": "The Y carries few genes, and none for opsins.", "1": "The ABO gene I is on chromosome 9.", "2": "This is where the red and green opsin genes are."}'),
  ('BIO-5168', 'Inheritance', 'Muscular dystrophy', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 109 (§18.8.2)', 'In Duchenne muscular dystrophy, about what proportion of cases are due to a new mutation rather than inherited from the mother?', '["Two-thirds", "None", "One-third", "All cases"]', 2, 'About two-thirds of cases are inherited from the mother and one-third result from a new mutation.', '{"0": "This is the share inherited from the mother.", "1": "The book says one-third arise from new mutations.", "3": "Most cases are inherited."}'),
  ('BIO-5169', 'Inheritance', 'Muscular dystrophy', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 110 (§18.8.2)', 'According to the book, a child with Duchenne muscular dystrophy typically needs a wheelchair by about age', '["4", "45", "12", "10"]', 2, 'It becomes evident around age 4 when the child walks; braces may be needed by 10, and by 12 most need a wheelchair.', '{"0": "Around age 4 the disease first becomes evident.", "1": "45 is the upper end of the typical lifespan of 15 to 45 years.", "3": "By age 10 the child may need braces."}'),
  ('BIO-5170', 'Inheritance', 'Sex-limited and sex-influenced', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 18 Inheritance, p. 110 (§18.8.3)', 'The growth of a beard in humans is described in the book as', '["a sex-influenced trait like baldness", "a Y-linked trait", "an X-linked recessive trait", "a sex-limited trait"]', 3, 'Beard-growth genes are present in both sexes but expressed only in males, so it is a sex-limited trait.', '{"0": "Sex-influenced traits have alleles dominant in one sex and recessive in the other.", "1": "Sex-limited genes are present in both sexes, not only on the Y.", "2": "Beard genes are not described as X-linked."}'),
  ('BIO-5171', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome structure', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 116 (§19.1)', 'The name ''chromosome'', meaning coloured bodies, was proposed in 1888 by', '["Waldeyer-Hartz", "Walther Flemming", "Walter Sutton", "Wilhelm Johannsen"]', 0, 'Flemming discovered chromosomes in 1882; the German anatomist Waldeyer-Hartz proposed the name in 1888.', '{"1": "Flemming discovered chromosomes in salamander larvae in 1882.", "2": "Sutton proposed the chromosomal theory of inheritance in 1902.", "3": "Johannsen introduced the term ''gene'' in 1909."}'),
  ('BIO-5172', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 116 (§19.1.1)', 'According to the book, the centromere is a specific DNA sequence of about', '["140 million nucleotides", "220 nucleotides", "146 nucleotides", "80 nucleotides"]', 1, 'The centromere is described as a specific DNA sequence of about 220 nucleotides, with the kinetochore attached to it.', '{"0": "This is the DNA content of an average human chromosome.", "2": "About 146 nucleotides wrap around each histone octamer.", "3": "About 80 nucleotides make up the linker DNA between nucleosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-5173', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 116 (§19.1.1)', 'The knob-like end of a chromosome beyond the secondary constriction is called the', '["kinetochore", "satellite", "telomere", "chromatid"]', 1, 'The knob-like end beside the secondary constriction is the satellite; it has highly repetitive ''junk'' DNA.', '{"0": "The kinetochore is a protein disc on the centromere.", "2": "Telomeres are the terminal ends that stop chromosomes joining end to end.", "3": "A chromatid is one of the two identical strands of a duplicated chromosome."}'),
  ('BIO-5174', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome structure', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 117 (§19.1.1)', 'A chromosome whose centromere lies exactly at one end is', '["telocentric", "acrocentric", "metacentric", "sub-metacentric"]', 0, 'Metacentric: centre; sub-metacentric: slightly off centre; acrocentric: near the end; telocentric: at the end.', '{"1": "Acrocentric chromosomes have it near, but not at, the end.", "2": "Metacentric chromosomes have the centromere at the centre.", "3": "Sub-metacentric chromosomes have it slightly away from the centre."}'),
  ('BIO-5175', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome composition', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 117 (§19.1.2)', 'The DNA in an average human chromosome is about', '["2 nm long, with 146 nucleotides", "700 nm long", "5 cm long, with about 140 million nucleotides", "1 m long, with 20,000 nucleotides"]', 2, 'The book states that DNA of an average human chromosome is about 5 cm long and has about 140 million nucleotides.', '{"0": "2 nm is the thickness of DNA; 146 nucleotides wrap one nucleosome.", "1": "700 nm is the thickness of a chromatid.", "3": "About 20,000 is the number of human genes, not nucleotides per chromosome."}'),
  ('BIO-5176', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome composition', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 117 (§19.1.2)', 'Histone proteins are positively charged because they are rich in', '["deoxyribose sugars", "phosphate groups", "acidic amino acids such as glutamic acid", "basic amino acids such as arginine and lysine"]', 3, 'Histones contain many basic amino acids such as arginine and lysine, so they bind the negatively charged phosphate groups of DNA.', '{"0": "Deoxyribose is part of DNA, not histones.", "1": "Phosphate groups give DNA its negative charge.", "2": "Acidic amino acids would give a negative charge."}'),
  ('BIO-5177', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome composition', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 117 (§19.1.2)', 'In a nucleosome, about how many nucleotides of DNA wrap around the histone octamer, and how many times?', '["About 80 nucleotides, wrapped once", "About 220 nucleotides, wrapped four times", "About 146 nucleotides, wrapped twice", "About 1000 nucleotides, wrapped ten times"]', 2, 'About every 146 nucleotides of DNA wrap twice around a histone octamer; nucleosomes are linked by about 80 nucleotides of linker DNA.', '{"0": "About 80 nucleotides is the linker DNA between nucleosomes.", "1": "About 220 nucleotides is the centromere sequence.", "3": "This does not match the book''s figures."}'),
  ('BIO-5178', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome composition', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 117 (§19.1.2)', 'Histone H1, the linker histone, lies at the base of the nucleosome and', '["helps in winding the chain of nucleosomes", "forms part of the histone octamer core", "joins Okazaki fragments", "breaks the hydrogen bonds between DNA strands"]', 0, 'H1 sits near the DNA entry and exit points of the nucleosome and helps wind the chain of nucleosomes.', '{"1": "The octamer has H2A, H2B, H3 and H4 only.", "2": "DNA ligase joins Okazaki fragments.", "3": "Helicase does this during replication."}'),
  ('BIO-5179', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosome composition', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 118 (§19.1.2)', 'The active portions of chromatin are called', '["heterochromatin", "nucleosomes", "satellites", "euchromatin"]', 3, 'Inactive portions that form dark bands are heterochromatin; active portions are euchromatin.', '{"0": "Heterochromatin is inactive and forms dark bands.", "1": "Nucleosomes are DNA wrapped around histones, found in both regions.", "2": "Satellites are knob-like ends of some chromosomes."}'),
  ('BIO-5180', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genes', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 119 (§19.1.3)', 'If one chromosome carries an allele for eye colour at a particular locus, its homologous chromosome carries', '["the same allele at a different locus", "an allele for eye colour at the same locus", "an allele for a different trait at that locus", "no gene at that locus"]', 1, 'Alleles, the alternative forms of a gene, lie at the same locus on homologous chromosomes.', '{"0": "Alleles occupy the same locus.", "2": "Homologous chromosomes carry genes for the same traits at the same loci.", "3": "Each homologue has the gene at the same locus."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5181', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Hershey–Chase', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 122 (§19.2.2)', 'In the Hershey–Chase setup with ³⁵S-labelled bacteriophages, radioactivity was found', '["equally in the cells and the medium", "in the new phage DNA", "in the bacterial cells, not in the medium", "in the medium, not in the bacterial cells"]', 3, 'The ³⁵S-labelled protein coats stayed outside the bacteria, so radioactivity remained in the medium.', '{"0": "The two locations gave clearly different results.", "1": "³⁵S labels protein, which does not enter the cell.", "2": "This was the result with ³²P-labelled DNA."}'),
  ('BIO-5182', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication models', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 123 (§19.3.1)', 'According to the dispersive model of DNA replication, after replication', '["one daughter DNA has both parental strands and the other has both new strands", "both daughter DNAs contain strands that are mixtures of old and new nucleotides", "no new DNA is made", "each daughter DNA has one parental strand and one new strand"]', 1, 'In the dispersive model the parent DNA breaks into nucleotides that mix with new ones, so every strand is a mixture of old and new.', '{"0": "This is the conservative model.", "2": "All models produce new DNA.", "3": "This is the semi-conservative model."}'),
  ('BIO-5183', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Meselson–Stahl', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 124 (§19.3.2)', 'If DNA replication were conservative, what would Meselson and Stahl have seen after the first replication in ¹⁴N medium?', '["Two bands, one heavy (¹⁵N) and one light (¹⁴N)", "A single heavy band", "A single band of intermediate density", "Two bands, intermediate and light"]', 0, 'A conservative copy would keep the heavy parent intact and make an all-light daughter, giving two bands; the single intermediate band they saw ruled this out.', '{"1": "This was seen only before any replication.", "2": "This is what they observed, matching semi-conservative (and dispersive) replication.", "3": "This was seen after the second replication."}'),
  ('BIO-5184', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Meselson–Stahl', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 125 (§19.3.2)', 'An experiment in which an organism is briefly exposed to a radioisotope and the labelled DNA is then followed over time is called a', '["testcross", "transformation experiment", "pulse-chase experiment", "ultracentrifugation"]', 2, 'In the ''pulse'' stage the organism takes up the isotope; in the ''chase'' stage the labelled DNA is followed by measuring its radioactivity.', '{"0": "A testcross finds whether a dominant phenotype is homozygous or heterozygous.", "1": "Transformation is the transfer of genetic material that changes the recipient, as in Griffith''s work.", "3": "This is a separation technique, not the name of the labelling experiment."}'),
  ('BIO-5185', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 127 (§19.3.3)', 'In prokaryotes, the role of DNA polymerase II is', '["laying down RNA primers", "replacing RNA primers with DNA", "repairing DNA damage during the life of the cell", "building most of the new strand"]', 2, 'Polymerase III is the main builder, polymerase I replaces primers, and polymerase II is involved in repairing DNA damage.', '{"0": "Primase lays down RNA primers.", "1": "This is done by DNA polymerase I.", "3": "This is the job of DNA polymerase III."}'),
  ('BIO-5186', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 127 (§19.3.3)', 'Where does the energy for adding each nucleotide during DNA replication come from?', '["Breaking the hydrogen bonds by helicase", "Splitting the deoxyribose sugar", "Hydrolysis of the RNA primer", "Breaking off two phosphate groups from the deoxyribonucleoside triphosphate"]', 3, 'As DNA polymerase III attaches a dNTP, it breaks off two phosphates, releasing energy for building the strand.', '{"0": "Helicase separates strands; this does not power nucleotide addition.", "1": "The sugar becomes part of the backbone; it is not split.", "2": "Primers are removed later; they do not power each addition."}'),
  ('BIO-5187', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 127 (§19.3.3)', 'How do the primer needs of the leading and lagging strands differ?', '["The leading strand can be extended from one primer, while the lagging strand needs a new primer for each Okazaki fragment", "Only the leading strand needs primers", "Neither strand needs a primer", "The leading strand needs a primer for every nucleotide"]', 0, 'The leading strand grows continuously from one primer; the lagging strand is made in Okazaki fragments, each needing its own primer.', '{"1": "The lagging strand needs many primers.", "2": "DNA polymerase III can only add to an existing 3′ end, so primers are needed.", "3": "It is built continuously from one primer."}'),
  ('BIO-5188', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Replication mechanism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 129 (§19.3.3)', 'In prokaryotes, Okazaki fragments are about', '["5 to 10 nucleotides long", "1000 to 2000 nucleotides long", "100 to 200 nucleotides long", "250 nucleotides long"]', 1, 'Prokaryotic Okazaki fragments are longer (1000–2000 nucleotides) than eukaryotic ones (100–200).', '{"0": "5–10 RNA nucleotides is the length of a primer.", "2": "This is the eukaryotic length.", "3": "About 250 A nucleotides make the poly-A tail."}'),
  ('BIO-5189', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 131 (§19.4.1)', 'The 1968 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine was shared by Nirenberg, Khorana and Robert Holley. Holley was recognised for', '["making poly-U mRNA", "proposing the central dogma", "discovering the chemical structure of transfer RNA", "decoding the remaining 17 codons"]', 2, 'Nirenberg was honoured for work on the genetic code, Khorana for synthesis of nucleic acids, and Holley for the structure of tRNA.', '{"0": "Nirenberg made poly-U mRNA.", "1": "Francis Crick used the term central dogma.", "3": "Khorana determined the remaining 17 codons."}'),
  ('BIO-5190', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 131 (§19.4.1)', 'Nirenberg and Philip Leder tested triplet codons and determined the amino acids specified by', '["47 codons", "3 codons", "64 codons", "17 codons"]', 0, 'Nirenberg and Leder decoded 47 codons; Khorana decoded the remaining 17.', '{"1": "Three codons are stop codons.", "2": "There are 64 codons in total, decoded by several scientists.", "3": "Khorana decoded the remaining 17."}'),
  ('BIO-5191', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 131 (§19.4.1)', 'The start codon AUG, present at the beginning of genes, specifies the amino acid', '["phenylalanine", "methionine", "glycine", "arginine"]', 1, 'AUG acts as the start codon and specifies methionine.', '{"0": "UUU specifies phenylalanine.", "2": "GGC and GGU specify glycine.", "3": "AGA specifies arginine."}'),
  ('BIO-5192', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 132 (§19.4.2)', 'The book explains that the slightly different genetic code of mitochondrial DNA reflects', '["the absence of DNA in mitochondria", "mutations caused by UV radiation", "the use of RNA primers", "the independent evolutionary history of mitochondria"]', 3, 'The mitochondrial code differs slightly from the chromosomal code, which reflects the independent evolutionary history of mitochondria.', '{"0": "Mitochondria do contain DNA.", "1": "The difference is a feature of the system, not random damage.", "2": "Primers are used in all DNA replication."}'),
  ('BIO-5193', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Genetic code', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 132 (§19.4.2)', 'The statement that the three nucleotides of a codon are read continuously, without gaps, describes which property of the genetic code?', '["Universality", "It is a triplet code", "It has no punctuation", "Degeneracy"]', 2, 'A codon is read continuously, without punctuation (gaps) between its three nucleotides.', '{"0": "Universality means the same code is used in almost all organisms.", "1": "Triplet means each codon has three nucleotides, not that they are read without gaps.", "3": "Degeneracy means several codons can specify the same amino acid."}'),
  ('BIO-5194', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Transcription', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 133 (§19.5)', 'The template strand of DNA used in transcription is also called the', '["antisense (–) strand", "lagging strand", "sense (+) strand", "leading strand"]', 0, 'The template strand is the antisense (–) strand; the coding strand is the sense (+) strand.', '{"1": "Lagging strand is a term from DNA replication.", "2": "This is the coding strand, which is not transcribed.", "3": "Leading strand is a term from DNA replication."}'),
  ('BIO-5195', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Transcription', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 133 (§19.5)', 'In prokaryotes, the promoter sequence TTGACA located 35 nucleotides before the start point is called the', '["TATA box", "CAAT box", "–10 sequence", "–35 sequence"]', 3, 'TTGACA is the –35 sequence and TATAAT the –10 sequence in prokaryotes.', '{"0": "The TATA box (TATAAA) is a eukaryotic –25 sequence.", "1": "The CAAT box (GGCCAATCT) is a eukaryotic –70 sequence.", "2": "The –10 sequence is TATAAT."}'),
  ('BIO-5196', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Transcription', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 134 (§19.5)', 'In prokaryotic RNA polymerase, which subunit binds with the template DNA?', '["β subunit", "β′ subunit", "α subunits", "σ subunit"]', 1, 'The two α subunits bind regulatory proteins, β′ binds the template DNA, β binds RNA nucleotides and σ recognizes the promoter.', '{"0": "The β subunit binds with RNA nucleotides.", "2": "The α subunits bind with regulatory proteins.", "3": "The σ subunit recognizes the promoter and is removed after binding."}'),
  ('BIO-5197', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Transcription', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 134 (§19.5)', 'In eukaryotes, ribosomal RNA (rRNA) is synthesized in the nucleolus by', '["RNA polymerase II", "DNA polymerase III", "RNA polymerase I", "RNA polymerase III"]', 2, 'RNA polymerase I makes rRNA in the nucleolus, II makes mRNA and III makes tRNA.', '{"0": "RNA polymerase II makes mRNA.", "1": "DNA polymerase III builds DNA during replication in prokaryotes.", "3": "RNA polymerase III makes tRNA."}'),
  ('BIO-5198', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Transcription', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 134 (§19.5)', 'During elongation of transcription, the 3′ end of the growing mRNA stays stable at the catalytic site because', '["the σ subunit stays bound to it", "a 5′ cap anchors it to the DNA", "a primer holds it in place", "its first 12 bases form a temporary helix with the template DNA strand"]', 3, 'The first 12 bases of new mRNA make a temporary helix with the template strand, keeping the 3′ end in position for incoming ribonucleotides.', '{"0": "The σ subunit is removed after binding to the promoter.", "1": "The 5′ cap is added later and protects mRNA from enzymes.", "2": "Transcription needs no primer."}'),
  ('BIO-5199', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'mRNA processing', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 136 (§19.5)', 'The 3′ poly-A tail added to eukaryotic mRNA consists of about', '["5–10 A nucleotides", "250 A nucleotides", "2000 A nucleotides", "12 A nucleotides"]', 1, 'About 250 A nucleotides are added at the 3′ end; the tail protects the transcript from degradation by nucleases.', '{"0": "5–10 nucleotides is the length of an RNA primer.", "2": "2000 is the upper length of prokaryotic Okazaki fragments.", "3": "12 is the number of bases in the temporary RNA–DNA helix."}'),
  ('BIO-5200', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Translation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 137 (§19.6)', 'How many kinds of aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase are there, according to the book?', '["20", "3", "61", "64"]', 0, 'There are 20 kinds of this enzyme, one for each amino acid; each also recognizes the tRNAs with anticodons for that amino acid.', '{"1": "3 is the number of stop codons.", "2": "The book gives one enzyme kind per amino acid, not per codon.", "3": "64 is the number of codons."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5201', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Translation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 138 (§19.6)', 'During elongation of translation, the enzyme that joins the amino acid on the P-site tRNA to the amino acid on the A-site tRNA by a peptide bond is', '["release factor", "RNA polymerase", "aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase", "peptidyl transferase"]', 3, 'Peptidyl transferase removes the amino acid (or chain) from the P-site tRNA and attaches it by a peptide bond to the amino acid at the A site.', '{"0": "The release factor ends translation at a stop codon.", "1": "RNA polymerase makes RNA during transcription.", "2": "This enzyme attaches amino acids to their tRNAs before translation."}'),
  ('BIO-5202', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Translation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 140 (§19.6.1)', 'Which is a difference between translation in prokaryotes and eukaryotes given in the book?', '["Eukaryotes start with N-formyl methionine", "Prokaryotic genes have more introns than eukaryotic genes", "Prokaryotic ribosomes are 70S, while eukaryotic ribosomes are 80S", "Prokaryotes translate only on rough endoplasmic reticulum"]', 2, 'Prokaryotes have smaller 70S ribosomes; eukaryotes have larger 80S ribosomes and translate in the cytoplasm and on rough ER.', '{"0": "Eukaryotes start with unmodified methionine.", "1": "Prokaryotic genes lack introns.", "3": "Prokaryotes have no ER; they translate in the cytoplasm."}'),
  ('BIO-5203', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Gene regulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 141 (§19.7)', 'In positive gene regulation, regulatory proteins called activators', '["remove introns from mRNA", "help RNA polymerase bind to the promoter, starting or speeding up transcription", "add a 5′ cap to mRNA", "block RNA polymerase to stop transcription"]', 1, 'Activators (inducers) bind to operators and help RNA polymerase bind the promoter, starting transcription or increasing its rate.', '{"0": "Splicing removes introns.", "2": "Capping is a post-transcriptional modification.", "3": "This is what repressors do in negative regulation."}'),
  ('BIO-5204', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Gene regulation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 141 (§19.7)', 'Regulation of gene expression at the post-translational level controls', '["the folding and stability of the protein", "the rate of transcription", "the rate of translation", "the modification of mRNA"]', 0, 'At post-translational level the modification of protein, such as folding and stability, is regulated.', '{"1": "This is transcriptional-level regulation.", "2": "This is translational-level regulation.", "3": "This is post-transcriptional regulation."}'),
  ('BIO-5205', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Mutation', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 143 (§19.8)', 'An organism or cell in which a mutation has occurred is called a', '["transposon", "mutagen", "mutant", "carrier"]', 2, 'The agent causing a mutation is a mutagen; the organism or cell in which it occurs is a mutant.', '{"0": "A transposon is a mobile piece of DNA.", "1": "A mutagen is the agent that causes mutation.", "3": "A carrier is a heterozygote for a recessive allele."}'),
  ('BIO-5206', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Mutation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 143 (§19.8.1)', 'Ultraviolet radiation causes mutations when', '["it inserts transposons into genes", "two adjacent pyrimidine nucleotides absorb it and form a covalent bond between them, which can block replication", "it forms free radicals that break phosphodiester bonds", "it removes amino groups from adenine"]', 1, 'When adjacent pyrimidines (C and T) absorb UV, a covalent bond forms between them, which can completely block replication.', '{"0": "Transposons move by themselves; UV does not insert them.", "2": "This is how ionizing radiation such as X-rays acts.", "3": "Some mutagen chemicals do this."}'),
  ('BIO-5207', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Mutation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 144 (§19.8.1)', 'Some mutagen chemicals cause point mutations by', '["forming a covalent bond between adjacent pyrimidines", "causing non-disjunction of chromosome 21", "breaking the centromere", "removing the amino group from adenine or cytosine, causing mis-pairing"]', 3, 'Some chemicals resemble nucleotides; some remove amino groups from A or C, and some add hydrocarbon groups, all causing mis-pairing. Mustard gas is an example released into the environment.', '{"0": "This is the effect of UV radiation.", "1": "Non-disjunction is a chromosome-number change, not a chemical point mutation.", "2": "This is not how the book describes chemical mutagens."}'),
  ('BIO-5208', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Mutation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 144 (§19.8.1)', 'The book gives which example of a single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP)?', '["A single base change that makes a person unable to digest milk (lactose)", "Trisomy 21 in Down syndrome", "Deletion of three nucleotides in cystic fibrosis", "A premature stop codon in muscular dystrophy"]', 0, 'SNPs involve a single nucleotide and are often harmless or subtle; one example is a base change that prevents digestion of lactose.', '{"1": "This is a chromosomal mutation.", "2": "This is an example of a deletion.", "3": "This is a nonsense mutation."}'),
  ('BIO-5209', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Point mutation disorders', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 145 (§19.8.1)', 'In sickle cell anaemia, the DNA codon change that replaces glutamic acid with valine is', '["CTC → CAC, a substitution of T by A", "GGC → GGU, a silent mutation", "insertion of a transposon", "deletion of three nucleotides"]', 0, 'The point mutation substitutes T with A, changing the DNA codon CTC (glutamic acid) to CAC (valine); it is a missense mutation.', '{"1": "This change keeps glycine and is the book''s silent mutation example.", "2": "Transposon insertion is the book''s example for haemophilia.", "3": "This is the common cause of cystic fibrosis."}'),
  ('BIO-5210', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Point mutation disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 146 (§19.8.1)', 'Which group of symptoms is typical of untreated phenylketonuria?', '["Small testicles and breast growth in males", "Intellectual disability, seizures, microcephaly and a musty odour", "Pain, jaundice and sickle-shaped red cells", "Webbed neck, short stature and infertility"]', 1, 'PKU symptoms include intellectual disability, seizures, behavioural problems, microcephaly and a musty odour of breath, skin and urine.', '{"0": "These are features of Klinefelter syndrome.", "2": "These are features of sickle cell anaemia.", "3": "These are features of Turner syndrome."}'),
  ('BIO-5211', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Point mutation disorders', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 146 (§19.8.1)', 'Tailoring drug treatment to an individual''s genetic make-up by analysing their DNA is called', '["cell therapy", "genetic counselling", "gene therapy", "pharmacogenomics"]', 3, 'Pharmacogenomics tailors drug treatment to a person''s genetic make-up.', '{"0": "Cell therapy replaces non-functioning cells, such as beta cells in type 1 diabetes.", "1": "Genetic counselling gives information, testing and family-planning guidance.", "2": "Gene therapy corrects or replaces a mutated gene."}'),
  ('BIO-5212', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 146 (§19.8.2)', 'A chromosomal mutation in which the orientation of a portion of a chromosome is reversed is called', '["non-disjunction", "a translocation", "an inversion", "a duplication"]', 2, 'In an inversion the orientation of a chromosome segment is reversed; in a translocation a segment joins another chromosome.', '{"0": "Non-disjunction is failure of chromosomes to separate in meiosis.", "1": "In translocation a segment becomes part of another chromosome.", "3": "A duplication repeats a segment."}'),
  ('BIO-5213', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 147 (§19.8.2)', 'The book gives which example of a human disorder caused by insertional inactivation?', '["Down syndrome", "Phenylketonuria", "X-linked haemophilia, when a transposon inserts into the clotting factor IX gene", "Sickle cell anaemia"]', 2, 'A human transposon inserted into the factor IX gene on the X chromosome places a stop codon in the middle, causing X-linked haemophilia.', '{"0": "Down syndrome is caused by non-disjunction.", "1": "PKU is caused by point mutations in the PAH gene.", "3": "Sickle cell anaemia is caused by a substitution."}'),
  ('BIO-5214', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 148 (§19.8.2)', 'About how often does Down syndrome occur, according to the book?', '["About 1 in every 750 children", "About 1 in every 5000 children", "About 1 in every 64 children", "About 1 in every 4 children"]', 0, 'The book states that about 1 in every 750 children has Down syndrome, first described by J. Langdon Down in 1866.', '{"1": "About 1 in 5000 female births is the figure for Turner syndrome.", "2": "1/64 is a probability from a trihybrid cross.", "3": "1 in 4 is the chance of a recessive child from two carriers, not the frequency of Down syndrome."}'),
  ('BIO-5215', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 149 (§19.8.2)', 'Which treatment is given in the book for Klinefelter syndrome?', '["Bone marrow transplant", "Testosterone at the onset of puberty", "Estrogen replacement therapy", "A low-phenylalanine diet"]', 1, 'Testosterone is given to compensate for its deficiency at puberty, with surgical breast removal and behavioural therapy as needed.', '{"0": "This can cure sickle cell anaemia.", "2": "Estrogen replacement is used in Turner syndrome.", "3": "This is the treatment for PKU."}'),
  ('BIO-5216', 'Chromosomes and DNA', 'Chromosomal mutations', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Chromosome and DNA, p. 149 (§19.8.2)', 'A girl with Turner syndrome can reach adult height if she is given', '["insulin injections", "Rh-antiserum", "testosterone at puberty", "growth hormone treatment early in childhood"]', 3, 'Growth hormone started early in childhood improves growth, and estrogen replacement develops secondary sexual characteristics.', '{"0": "Insulin treats type 1 diabetes.", "1": "Rh-antiserum prevents Rh sensitization in pregnancy.", "2": "Testosterone is the treatment for Klinefelter syndrome."}'),
  ('BIO-5217', 'Biotechnology', 'Genetic engineering', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 155 (§20.1)', 'According to the book, the two basic technologies that genetic engineering relies on are', '["vaccination and antibiotics", "electrophoresis and microscopy", "recombinant DNA technology and the polymerase chain reaction", "fermentation and selective breeding"]', 2, 'Genetic engineering relies on recombinant DNA technology (cut and paste of DNA) and PCR (rapid amplification of DNA).', '{"0": "These are medical treatments, not genetic engineering technologies.", "1": "These are lab techniques but not the two named technologies.", "3": "Fermentation is traditional biotechnology."}'),
  ('BIO-5218', 'Biotechnology', 'Tools', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 156 (§20.2.1)', 'Restriction sites recognized by restriction endonucleases are palindromic sequences that are usually', '["four to eight nucleotides long", "20 to 30 nucleotides long", "about 220 nucleotides long", "one nucleotide long"]', 0, 'Restriction sites are palindromic sequences of four to eight nucleotides.', '{"1": "20–30 nucleotides is the length of a PCR primer.", "2": "About 220 nucleotides is the centromere sequence.", "3": "A single nucleotide cannot be palindromic across two strands."}'),
  ('BIO-5219', 'Biotechnology', 'Vectors', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 157 (§20.2.1)', 'Which type of vector is used to carry genes of interest into monkey and human cells?', '["Bacteriophage lambda", "Animal viruses such as the human cold virus", "Bacterial plasmids", "Cosmids"]', 1, 'The book states that animal viruses (e.g., human cold virus) serve as vectors to carry genes into monkey and human cells.', '{"0": "Bacteriophages infect bacteria.", "2": "Plasmids are mainly used with bacterial host cells.", "3": "Cosmids are plasmids with a phage cos sequence, used with bacteria."}'),
  ('BIO-5220', 'Biotechnology', 'Vectors', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 157 (§20.2.1)', 'Which of the following is NOT listed in the book as a vector?', '["Bacterial artificial chromosome (BAC)", "Bacteriophage P1", "Yeast artificial chromosome (YAC)", "Restriction endonuclease"]', 3, 'Restriction endonucleases are enzymes that cut DNA; vectors include plasmids, bacteriophages, cosmids, BACs, YACs and P1 artificial chromosomes.', '{"0": "BACs are listed as vectors.", "1": "Bacteriophages such as P1 are listed as vectors.", "2": "YACs are listed as vectors."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5221', 'Biotechnology', 'Steps', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 158 (§20.2.2)', 'In cloning a recombinant vector, the bacteria that take up the recombinant DNA multiply to form', '["transgenic plants", "Okazaki fragments", "sticky ends", "bacterial clones carrying identical copies of the gene of interest"]', 3, 'Bacteria that take up the recombinant DNA form clones, each containing identical copies (clones) of the gene of interest.', '{"0": "Plants are grown from totipotent plant cells, not from bacterial clones.", "1": "Okazaki fragments are short DNA pieces of the lagging strand.", "2": "Sticky ends are produced by restriction enzymes before ligation."}'),
  ('BIO-5222', 'Biotechnology', 'Screening', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 159 (§20.2.2)', 'After a radioactive probe hybridizes with colonies carrying the gene of interest, these colonies are located on the master plate by', '["autoradiography on photographic film", "gel electrophoresis only", "growing them on ampicillin", "blue–white screening"]', 0, 'Autoradiography (photographic film) locates the colonies to which the radioactive probe has stuck.', '{"1": "The book uses autoradiography for this step.", "2": "This selects for cells with a plasmid.", "3": "Blue–white screening shows whether a plasmid is recombinant, not where the probe bound."}'),
  ('BIO-5223', 'Biotechnology', 'PCR', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 160 (§20.3)', 'Kary Mullis, who developed PCR in 1983, received the', '["Nobel Prize in Physics in 1993", "Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1983", "Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1993", "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1968"]', 2, 'Kary Mullis developed PCR in 1983 and won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1993.', '{"0": "His prize was in Chemistry.", "1": "1983 is the year PCR was developed.", "3": "The 1968 medicine prize went to Nirenberg, Khorana and Holley."}'),
  ('BIO-5224', 'Biotechnology', 'PCR', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 161 (§20.3.1)', 'Why is it not necessary to add more Taq polymerase in each new PCR cycle?', '["Taq polymerase is made by the primers", "The repeated heating does not harm this heat-stable enzyme", "Each cycle needs no enzyme after the first", "DNA ligase takes over after the first cycle"]', 1, 'Taq polymerase from Thermus aquaticus is stable and active at near-boiling temperatures, so heating does not destroy it.', '{"0": "Primers are short DNA sequences; they do not make enzymes.", "2": "Every cycle needs polymerase for extension; the same enzyme keeps working.", "3": "Ligase is not used in PCR extension."}'),
  ('BIO-5225', 'Biotechnology', 'PCR', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 161 (§20.3.1)', 'A collection of the total DNA from a single organism, which PCR can help to build, is called a', '["genomic library", "clone library of plasmids only", "restriction site", "karyotype"]', 0, 'PCR is used to build a genomic library, the collection of all the DNA of an organism, often used for DNA sequencing.', '{"1": "A clone library holds bacterial clones; the book''s term for an organism''s total DNA is genomic library.", "2": "A restriction site is a short palindromic sequence cut by an enzyme.", "3": "A karyotype is the complete set of chromosomes as they appear."}'),
  ('BIO-5226', 'Biotechnology', 'PCR', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 162 (§20.3.1)', 'Which application of PCR is mentioned in the book?', '["Breaking kidney stones", "Examining the DNA of extinct species from tiny amounts of remaining DNA", "Producing antibodies in plasma cells", "Joining insulin chains by disulphide bonds"]', 1, 'Applications include diagnosing infections, detecting mutations, DNA fingerprinting, testing early embryos and examining DNA of extinct species.', '{"0": "Lithotripsy breaks kidney stones.", "2": "Antibodies are made by the immune system, not by PCR.", "3": "This is done chemically after extracting the chains."}'),
  ('BIO-5227', 'Biotechnology', 'GMOs', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 162 (§20.4)', 'According to the book, approximately what percentage of the world''s canola, cotton, corn, soybean and sugar beet crops are transgenic?', '["10%", "100%", "90%", "50%"]', 2, 'Approximately 90% of canola, cotton, corn, soybean and sugar beets grown worldwide are transgenic.', '{"0": "The book gives a much larger figure.", "1": "Not all of these crops are transgenic.", "3": "The book gives about 90%."}'),
  ('BIO-5228', 'Biotechnology', 'Gene transfer', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 162 (§20.4)', 'To produce a whole multicellular GMO, the gene of interest is transferred into', '["only the skin cells of the adult", "red blood cells", "the bacterial plasmid only", "a gamete or gamete-producing tissue"]', 3, 'For a whole multicellular GMO the gene is put into a gamete or gamete-producing tissue, and such gametes are fused.', '{"0": "Changes in skin cells are not passed to a whole new organism.", "1": "Mature red blood cells are not used to produce GMOs.", "2": "A plasmid is a vector, not the target cell of a multicellular organism."}'),
  ('BIO-5229', 'Biotechnology', 'Gene transfer', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 162 (§20.4)', 'In which gene transfer method is a fine needle used to inject the gene directly into the nucleus of the target cell?', '["Biolistic transformation", "Microinjection", "Protoplast-mediated transfer", "Electroporation"]', 1, 'Microinjection uses a fine needle to inject the gene directly into the target cell nucleus.', '{"0": "This fires metal-coated DNA into cells with a gene gun.", "2": "This removes the cell wall and membrane and mixes the protoplast with the gene.", "3": "Electroporation makes temporary pores with an electric current."}'),
  ('BIO-5230', 'Biotechnology', 'Gene transfer', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 162 (§20.4)', 'Adenovirus, adeno-associated virus and lentivirus are used for', '["virus-mediated gene transfer", "blue–white screening", "producing Taq polymerase", "cutting DNA at palindromic sites"]', 0, 'The book lists adenovirus, adeno-associated virus, HIV, lentivirus and other retroviruses for virus-mediated gene transfer.', '{"1": "Blue–white screening uses the lacZ′ gene.", "2": "Taq polymerase comes from the bacterium Thermus aquaticus.", "3": "This is done by restriction endonucleases."}'),
  ('BIO-5231', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 163 (§20.4.1)', 'Transgenic bacteria make which product used to treat anaemia?', '["Chymosin", "Alpha-amylase", "Erythropoietin", "Interferon"]', 2, 'Transgenic bacteria produce erythropoietin to treat anaemia, interferon for some cancers and tissue plasminogen activator to dissolve clots.', '{"0": "Chymosin clots milk protein for cheese making.", "1": "Alpha-amylase converts starch to simple sugars.", "3": "Interferon is used to treat some cancers."}'),
  ('BIO-5232', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic bacteria', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 163 (§20.4.1)', 'Pectin-esterase produced by transgenic bacteria is used to', '["dissolve blood clots", "clot milk protein for cheese making", "convert starch to simple sugars", "improve the clarity of fruit juice"]', 3, 'The book lists pectin-esterase as improving fruit juice clarity.', '{"0": "This is tissue plasminogen activator.", "1": "This is chymosin.", "2": "This is alpha-amylase."}'),
  ('BIO-5233', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic animals', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 164 (§20.4.2)', 'Transgenic goats that produce the human enzyme lysozyme in their milk help to', '["produce spider-silk proteins", "reduce the development of Plasmodium", "make milk identical to human breast milk", "fight diarrhoea-causing bacteria in humans"]', 3, 'These goats produce human lysozyme in milk to fight diarrhoea-causing bacteria.', '{"0": "Other transgenic goats produce spider-web-like silk.", "1": "This describes malaria-resistant mosquitoes.", "2": "This is the goal of transgenic dairy cows."}'),
  ('BIO-5234', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic animals', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 164 (§20.4.2)', 'The first human biological drug produced from a transgenic goat was', '["lysozyme for diarrhoea", "an anticoagulant to reduce blood clots during surgery or childbirth", "hepatitis B vaccine", "insulin for diabetes"]', 1, 'The first human biological drug from a transgenic goat was an anticoagulant used during surgery or childbirth.', '{"0": "Lysozyme goats came as a separate example.", "2": "This is made by transgenic bacteria.", "3": "Human insulin is produced by transgenic E. coli."}'),
  ('BIO-5235', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic animals', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 163 (§20.4.2)', 'Since the mid-1980s, transgenic mice have been a key model for studying diseases because', '["they cannot get any diseases", "they have only four chromosomes", "their genome is fully sequenced and very similar to the human genome", "they are totipotent"]', 2, 'Mice are used because their genome has been completely analysed and is very similar to humans.', '{"0": "Mice are used precisely because diseases can be studied in them.", "1": "Drosophila has four chromosomes per set.", "3": "Totipotency is a property of many plant cells."}'),
  ('BIO-5236', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic plants', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 164 (§20.4.3)', 'Many crops have been genetically modified to be resistant to glyphosate, which is', '["a herbicide", "an antibiotic used in screening", "a precursor of vitamin A", "an insect toxin from Bacillus thuringiensis"]', 0, 'The book states that many crops have been modified to resist glyphosate, a herbicide.', '{"1": "Ampicillin is the antibiotic used for selection.", "2": "Beta-carotene is the vitamin A precursor in golden rice.", "3": "Bt genes give insect resistance, a different modification."}'),
  ('BIO-5237', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic plants', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 165 (§20.4.3)', 'In transgenic rice developed to increase iron content, the inserted genes were taken from', '["daffodil", "beans, Aspergillus fungus and wild rice", "Bacillus thuringiensis", "Thermus aquaticus"]', 1, 'Iron genes came from beans, Aspergillus and wild rice; beta-carotene genes came from daffodil.', '{"0": "Daffodil supplied the beta-carotene genes.", "2": "Bt genes provide insect resistance.", "3": "This bacterium is the source of Taq polymerase."}'),
  ('BIO-5238', 'Biotechnology', 'Transgenic plants', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 165 (§20.4.3)', 'GMO plants and plant cells are used to produce biopharmaceuticals such as', '["Taq polymerase only", "restriction endonucleases only", "cytokines, hormones, antibodies, enzymes and vaccines", "glyphosate herbicide"]', 2, 'The book lists cytokines, hormones, antibodies, enzymes and vaccines as biopharmaceuticals made in GMO plants.', '{"0": "Taq polymerase is from a bacterium.", "1": "These enzymes come from bacteria and are not the plant products named.", "3": "Crops are made resistant to glyphosate; they do not produce it."}'),
  ('BIO-5239', 'Biotechnology', 'Insulin', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 20 Biotechnology, p. 165 (§20.5)', 'According to the book, the first major triumph of genetic engineers was the production of', '["spider-silk goats", "golden rice", "malaria-resistant mosquitoes", "human insulin in bacteria"]', 3, 'Producing human insulin in bacteria was the first major triumph of genetic engineering.', '{"0": "These are a later transgenic animal example.", "1": "Golden rice came later as a transgenic plant example.", "2": "These were developed later in the laboratory."}'),
  ('BIO-5240', 'Immunity', 'First line of defence', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 170 (§21.1.1)', 'In an adult human, skin makes up about what percentage of total body weight?', '["15%", "2%", "50%", "20%"]', 0, 'Skin is the largest organ of the vertebrate body; in an adult human it is about 15% of total weight.', '{"1": "About 2% of body weight is the brain''s share.", "2": "Skin is far less than half of body weight.", "3": "About 20% is the share of plasma protein made of antibodies."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5241', 'Immunity', 'First line of defence', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 170 (§21.1.1)', 'The worn-out cells of the stratum corneum are replaced by new cells that', '["form in the dermis from fat cells", "form in the deep epidermis and migrate upward", "are made by sweat glands", "arrive from the bone marrow through the blood"]', 1, 'New cells form in the deep epidermis and migrate upward to replace damaged surface cells; they make keratin.', '{"0": "The book places new cell formation in the deep epidermis.", "2": "Sweat glands secrete sweat, including lysozyme.", "3": "Bone marrow makes blood cells, not skin cells."}'),
  ('BIO-5242', 'Immunity', 'First line of defence', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 171 (§21.1.2)', 'Apart from stomach acid, which secretion in the intestine helps kill bacteria swallowed with food?', '["Histamine", "Bile pigments", "Insulin", "Protein-digesting enzymes"]', 3, 'Strong stomach acids and protein-digesting enzymes in the intestine kill bacteria in food; saliva contains lysozyme.', '{"0": "Histamine starts inflammation; it is not an intestinal secretion that kills bacteria.", "1": "These are not named as antibacterial defences.", "2": "Insulin is a hormone that regulates blood glucose."}'),
  ('BIO-5243', 'Immunity', 'Germ-killer cells', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 172 (§21.2.1)', 'Besides fixed macrophages in organs, most macrophages', '["live only on the skin surface", "are stored inside red blood cells", "patrol in the blood, lymph and interstitial fluid", "stay permanently in the thymus"]', 2, 'Some macrophages are fixed in organs such as lungs, liver, spleen and brain, but most patrol the blood, lymph and interstitial fluid.', '{"0": "The skin surface is protected by the stratum corneum and secretions.", "1": "Macrophages are separate cells developed from monocytes.", "3": "T cells mature in the thymus."}'),
  ('BIO-5244', 'Immunity', 'Protective proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 173 (§21.2.2)', 'Which statement about the complement system is correct?', '["It has more than a dozen proteins that circulate inactive and activate one another in sequence", "All its proteins are always active in the blood", "It is made of antibodies produced by plasma cells", "It is released by virus-infected cells"]', 0, 'More than a dozen complement proteins circulate in an inactive state; on meeting a microbe the first activates the second and so on.', '{"1": "They circulate in an inactive state.", "2": "Complement proteins are made in the liver, not by plasma cells.", "3": "Interferons are released by virus-infected cells."}'),
  ('BIO-5245', 'Immunity', 'Protective proteins', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 174 (§21.2.2)', 'Besides alerting nearby cells to strengthen their antiviral defences, interferons also', '["form a membrane-attack complex", "activate natural killer cells and macrophages", "kill bacteria by digesting their cell walls", "make antibodies against viruses"]', 1, 'Interferons are cytokines that also activate other immune cells such as natural killer cells and macrophages.', '{"0": "The membrane-attack complex is formed by complement proteins.", "2": "This is the action of lysozyme.", "3": "Antibodies are made by plasma cells."}'),
  ('BIO-5246', 'Immunity', 'Temperature response', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 175 (§21.2.4)', 'According to the book, body temperatures above about which value are generally considered dangerous?', '["39.4 °C (103 °F)", "34 °C (93 °F)", "37 °C (98.6 °F)", "40.5 °C (105 °F)"]', 0, 'Temperatures above 103 °F (39.4 °C) are considered dangerous; above 105 °F (40.5 °C) they are often fatal.', '{"1": "About 34 °C is the temperature of the testes in the scrotum.", "2": "This is normal core body temperature.", "3": "This is the level that is often fatal."}'),
  ('BIO-5247', 'Immunity', 'Temperature response', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 175 (§21.2.4)', 'Which microbes does the book name as temperature-sensitive and killed at high fever?', '["Thermus aquaticus", "Escherichia coli and Bacillus thuringiensis", "Streptococcus pneumoniae and influenza", "Plasmodium only"]', 2, 'Some temperature-sensitive microbes, such as Streptococcus pneumoniae and influenza, are killed at high fever.', '{"0": "This bacterium lives in hot springs and is heat-tolerant.", "1": "These are not named as temperature-sensitive.", "3": "Plasmodium is mentioned in the context of malaria-resistant mosquitoes."}'),
  ('BIO-5248', 'Immunity', 'Types of immunity', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 175 (§21.3)', 'Non-specific defence (first and second lines), which is present at birth, is also called', '["artificial active immunity", "acquired immunity", "passive immunity", "inborn or innate immunity"]', 3, 'Non-specific defence is present at birth, so it is called inborn or innate immunity; specific defence is acquired.', '{"0": "This is gained by vaccination.", "1": "Acquired immunity develops during growth.", "2": "Passive immunity is a type of acquired immunity in which antibodies are given."}'),
  ('BIO-5249', 'Immunity', 'Types of immunity', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 175 (§21.3)', 'Which form of immunity does the book describe as the most effective?', '["Passive immunity", "Natural active immunity", "Inborn immunity", "Artificial active immunity"]', 1, 'In natural active immunity the body makes antibodies after natural exposure to antigens; the book calls it the most effective form.', '{"0": "Passive immunity is short-lived.", "2": "Inborn immunity is non-specific.", "3": "Vaccination is effective, but the book names natural active immunity as most effective."}'),
  ('BIO-5250', 'Immunity', 'Components of immune system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 176 (§21.3.1)', 'Where do B cells complete their maturation?', '["In the bone marrow", "In the spleen", "In the thymus", "In the liver"]', 0, 'B cells develop in the bone marrow and, unlike T cells, complete their maturation there rather than in the thymus.', '{"1": "The book says B cells complete maturation in the bone marrow.", "2": "T cells, not B cells, mature in the thymus.", "3": "The liver makes complement proteins."}'),
  ('BIO-5251', 'Immunity', 'Components of immune system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 176 (§21.3.1)', 'Which kind of T cell terminates the immune response?', '["Helper T cells", "Cytotoxic T cells", "Suppressor T cells", "Inducer T cells"]', 2, 'Helper T cells initiate, cytotoxic T cells destroy infected cells, inducer T cells supervise T cell development, and suppressor T cells terminate the response.', '{"0": "Helper T cells initiate the immune response.", "1": "Cytotoxic T cells destroy infected cells.", "3": "Inducer T cells supervise T cell development in the thymus."}'),
  ('BIO-5252', 'Immunity', 'Components of immune system', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 176 (§21.3.1)', 'No invader can escape being recognized by at least a few T cells because', '["T cells make antibodies against all microbes", "T cells are found only at the site of infection", "each T cell can recognize every antigen", "tens of millions of different T cells are made, each recognizing one particular antigen"]', 3, 'Tens of millions of different T cells are made, each specialized for one antigen.', '{"0": "Antibodies are made by plasma cells from B cells.", "1": "T cells circulate widely; recognition depends on their huge diversity.", "2": "Each T cell recognizes only one particular antigen."}'),
  ('BIO-5253', 'Immunity', 'Cell-mediated immunity', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 177 (§21.3.2)', 'Cell-mediated immunity, carried out by T cells, mainly protects the body from', '["bacteria on the skin surface", "toxins dissolved in blood plasma", "allergens such as pollen", "virus infections and cancer"]', 3, 'Cell-mediated immunity kills abnormal or virus-infected cells, protecting against viruses and cancer.', '{"0": "The skin barrier is first-line defence.", "1": "Antibodies inactivate toxic molecules in antibody-mediated immunity.", "2": "Allergic responses involve E-antibodies and mast cells."}'),
  ('BIO-5254', 'Immunity', 'Cell-mediated immunity', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 177 (§21.3.2)', 'When a macrophage engulfs a microbe, how does it help activate helper T cells?', '["It produces special proteins that helper T cells recognize through their receptors", "It releases histamine into the blood", "It travels to the thymus and becomes a T cell", "It releases antibodies against the microbe"]', 0, 'The macrophage produces special proteins; helper T cells recognize them through receptors and become activated.', '{"1": "Histamine is released by mast cells and injured cells.", "2": "Macrophages develop from monocytes and do not become T cells.", "3": "Antibodies come from plasma cells."}'),
  ('BIO-5255', 'Immunity', 'Cell-mediated immunity', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 177 (§21.3.2)', 'About how long after the start of a cell-mediated response do suppressor T cells become numerous enough to shut it down?', '["1 to 2 days", "A few minutes", "1 to 2 weeks", "Several years"]', 2, 'Suppressor T cells multiply more slowly; after 1 to 2 weeks they are numerous enough to shut down the cytotoxic response.', '{"0": "The book gives 1 to 2 weeks.", "1": "Their low initial numbers and slow growth prevent such a quick shut-down.", "3": "Shut-down happens after weeks; memory cells are what persist for years."}'),
  ('BIO-5256', 'Immunity', 'Antibody-mediated immunity', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 178 (§21.3.2)', 'Antibody-mediated immunity is also called', '["cell-mediated immunity", "humoral immunity", "inflammatory response", "innate immunity"]', 1, 'Antibody-mediated immunity, with B cells as key players, is also called humoral immunity.', '{"0": "Cell-mediated immunity is carried out by T cells.", "2": "Inflammation is part of the second line of defence.", "3": "Innate immunity is the non-specific defence present at birth."}'),
  ('BIO-5257', 'Immunity', 'Antibody-mediated immunity', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 179 (§21.3.2)', 'In antibody-mediated immunity, killer cells differ from natural killer cells because they', '["produce antibodies", "form the membrane-attack complex", "release pyrogens to cause fever", "have receptors that recognize antibody-coated antigens"]', 3, 'Killer cells are like natural killer cells but possess receptors that recognize antibody-coated antigens, then bind and kill the cell.', '{"0": "Antibodies are produced by plasma cells.", "1": "This is done by complement proteins.", "2": "Pyrogens are released by macrophages."}'),
  ('BIO-5258', 'Immunity', 'Antibody-mediated immunity', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 179 (§21.3.2)', 'A vaccine is made from killed or weakened pathogens and works by stimulating', '["the immediate transfer of ready-made antibodies", "the release of histamine from mast cells", "antibody production and formation of memory cells", "the destruction of self-proteins"]', 2, 'Vaccines stimulate antibody production and memory cell formation, giving artificial active immunity.', '{"0": "Giving ready-made antibodies is passive immunity.", "1": "This is part of an allergic response.", "3": "Self-proteins should not be destroyed."}'),
  ('BIO-5259', 'Immunity', 'Antibody structure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 181 (§21.3.2, Structure of antibody)', 'Each chain of an antibody has a constant region that', '["is similar in all antibodies of the same class", "joins the chains by peptide bonds only", "differs in every antibody", "forms the antigen-binding site"]', 0, 'Each chain has a constant region, similar in all antibodies of the same class, and a variable region, which differs and forms the binding site.', '{"1": "The four chains are held together by disulphide bridges.", "2": "This describes the variable region.", "3": "The variable regions form the binding site."}'),
  ('BIO-5260', 'Immunity', 'Monoclonal antibodies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 181 (§21.3.2, Monoclonal antibodies)', 'Antibodies that attach to different portions of the same antigen surface are called', '["E-antibodies", "polyclonal antibodies", "G-antibodies", "monoclonal antibodies"]', 1, 'When many different antibodies attach to different parts of an antigen, they are polyclonal; antibodies attaching to only one determinant are monoclonal.', '{"0": "E-antibodies are involved in allergies.", "2": "G-antibodies are produced by desensitization.", "3": "Monoclonal antibodies attach to only one determinant."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5261', 'Immunity', 'Monoclonal antibodies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 181 (§21.3.2, Monoclonal antibodies)', 'In 1984, Cesar Milstein and George Kohler were awarded the Nobel Prize for', '["learning how to produce monoclonal antibodies", "discovering interferons", "developing PCR", "discovering the ABO blood groups"]', 0, 'Milstein and Kohler produced monoclonal antibodies by fusing plasma cells with myeloma cells to make hybridomas.', '{"1": "The book credits them with monoclonal antibodies.", "2": "Kary Mullis developed PCR.", "3": "Landsteiner discovered the ABO system in 1901."}'),
  ('BIO-5262', 'Immunity', 'Monoclonal antibodies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 182 (§21.3.2, Monoclonal antibodies)', 'Which protective protein does the book say was purified using monoclonal antibodies?', '["Keratin", "Histamine", "Interferon", "Insulin"]', 2, 'Monoclonal antibodies can purify specific molecules from complex mixtures; interferon was purified this way.', '{"0": "Keratin is a skin protein, not mentioned as purified this way.", "1": "Histamine is a chemical signal released by mast cells.", "3": "Insulin is made in bacteria and joined chemically."}'),
  ('BIO-5263', 'Immunity', 'Allergies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 182 (§21.3.3)', 'In allergies, E-antibodies bind to mast cells, which are usually found', '["inside red blood cells", "in connective tissues surrounding blood vessels", "in the thymus only", "in the stratum corneum"]', 1, 'E-antibodies bind to mast cells, usually found in connective tissue around blood vessels; these release histamines.', '{"0": "Mast cells are not inside red blood cells.", "2": "The thymus is where T cells mature.", "3": "This is the outer skin layer of dead cells."}'),
  ('BIO-5264', 'Immunity', 'Allergies', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 182 (§21.3.3)', 'Common allergies are often given temporary relief by drugs that', '["stimulate E-antibody production", "activate mast cells", "increase histamine release", "block histamines"]', 3, 'Antihistamine drugs block histamines and give temporary relief.', '{"0": "E-antibodies cause the allergic response.", "1": "Activated mast cells release histamines.", "2": "More histamine would worsen symptoms."}'),
  ('BIO-5265', 'Immunity', 'Autoimmune diseases', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 183 (§21.3.3)', 'In which autoimmune disease does the patient form antibodies against their own connective tissues and major organs?', '["Type I diabetes", "Myasthenia gravis", "Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)", "Rheumatoid arthritis"]', 2, 'In SLE, antibodies are formed against the patient''s own connective tissues and major organs.', '{"0": "Here antibodies destroy insulin-producing cells.", "1": "In myasthenia gravis, antibodies target acetylcholine receptors.", "3": "Here antibodies target cartilage of joints."}'),
  ('BIO-5266', 'Immunity', 'Autoimmune diseases', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 183 (§21.3.3)', 'In rheumatoid arthritis, the patient forms antibodies against', '["insulin-producing cells in the pancreas", "the cartilaginous tissue of joints", "acetylcholine receptors on skeletal muscle", "red blood cells of an Rh-positive foetus"]', 1, 'In rheumatoid arthritis antibodies are formed against joint cartilage and damage it.', '{"0": "This happens in type I diabetes.", "2": "This happens in myasthenia gravis.", "3": "This is erythroblastosis foetalis, not an autoimmune disease."}'),
  ('BIO-5267', 'Immunity', 'Transplant rejection', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 183 (§21.3.3)', 'According to the book, the chance that an unrelated donor matches a transplant recipient is about', '["1 in 100,000–200,000", "1 in 4", "100%", "1 in 750"]', 0, 'Identical twins match 100%; matches are best within a family, and the chance of an unrelated match is 1 in 100,000–200,000.', '{"1": "This is a typical Mendelian probability, not a donor match figure.", "2": "Only identical twins have a 100% match.", "3": "About 1 in 750 children has Down syndrome."}'),
  ('BIO-5268', 'Immunity', 'Transplant rejection', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 21 Immunity, p. 183 (§21.3.3)', 'Besides using monoclonal antibodies against attacking T cells, the book describes which second approach to preventing transplant rejection?', '["Strengthening the recipient''s immune system", "Giving the patient antihistamines", "Giving Rh-antiserum", "Using stem cells to make a new immune system that recognizes the transplant as self"]', 3, 'The second approach uses stem cells to build a new immune system that recognizes the transplanted organ as self.', '{"0": "A stronger immune response would increase rejection.", "1": "Antihistamines treat allergies.", "2": "Rh-antiserum prevents Rh sensitization in pregnancy."}'),
  ('BIO-5269', 'Biostatistics', 'Components', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 187 (§22.1.1)', 'Using data from medical records, government reports and published research is called', '["primary data collection", "inferential statistics", "data cleaning", "secondary data collection"]', 3, 'Secondary data collection uses existing data from published research, medical records, government reports and databases.', '{"0": "Primary data come directly from experiments, surveys, interviews, observations and clinical trials.", "1": "Inferential statistics draw conclusions about populations; they are a type of analysis, not a source of data.", "2": "Data cleaning is part of data management, done after the data are collected."}'),
  ('BIO-5270', 'Biostatistics', 'Components', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 187 (§22.1.1)', 'Regression analysis and modelling are examples of', '["study design", "inferential statistics", "data collection methods", "descriptive statistics"]', 1, 'Inferential statistics, including hypothesis testing, regression analysis and modelling, allow conclusions about populations from sample data.', '{"0": "Study design covers the question, sampling, variables, randomization and sample size.", "2": "Collection is the step of gathering data, before any analysis.", "3": "Descriptive statistics such as mean, median, mode and SD only describe the data in hand."}'),
  ('BIO-5271', 'Biostatistics', 'Components', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 187 (§22.1.1)', 'Considering the context, limitations and assumptions of an analysis is stressed in which component of biostatistics?', '["Interpretation of results", "Data collection", "Data management and processing", "Presentation and communication"]', 0, 'Interpretation of results translates statistical findings into biological or health conclusions while considering context, limitations and assumptions.', '{"1": "Data collection is about gathering accurate and relevant primary or secondary data.", "2": "This covers cleaning, coding and storing data while keeping it confidential.", "3": "This covers charts, graphs and clear reporting for different audiences."}'),
  ('BIO-5272', 'Biostatistics', 'Components', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 187 (§22.1.1)', 'According to the book, transparency in reporting methods and findings is essential for', '["removing confounding factors", "reducing the sample size", "reproducibility and trust", "making the data normally distributed"]', 2, 'Under presentation and communication, the book says transparency in reporting methods and findings is essential for reproducibility and trust.', '{"0": "Confounding factors are controlled in the study design.", "1": "Sample size is fixed during study design, not by how results are reported.", "3": "Reporting does not change how the data are distributed."}'),
  ('BIO-5273', 'Biostatistics', 'Applications', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 188 (§22.1.2)', 'Biostatistics used to analyse huge genomic and proteomic datasets by developing algorithms and models is applied in', '["bioinformatics", "demography", "environmental health", "healthcare quality control"]', 0, 'In bioinformatics, biostatistics supports analysis of vast genomic and proteomic datasets by developing algorithms and models.', '{"1": "Demography analyses trends in births, deaths and other vital statistics.", "2": "Environmental health studies how pollution and toxins affect human health.", "3": "Quality control monitors diagnostic test accuracy, patient outcomes and consistency of procedures."}'),
  ('BIO-5274', 'Biostatistics', 'Applications', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 188 (§22.1.2)', 'Evaluating the accuracy of diagnostic tests and tracking patient outcomes to keep medical procedures consistent is an application of biostatistics in', '["epidemiology", "bioinformatics", "healthcare quality control", "environmental health"]', 2, 'The book lists evaluating diagnostic test accuracy, tracking patient outcomes and ensuring consistency of procedures under healthcare quality control.', '{"0": "Epidemiology studies the distribution and determinants of disease in populations.", "1": "Bioinformatics handles large genomic and proteomic datasets.", "3": "Environmental health deals with pollution and toxins."}'),
  ('BIO-5275', 'Biostatistics', 'Applications', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 188 (§22.1.2)', 'Measuring the impact of an immunization campaign is an example of biostatistics used to', '["tailor treatment to an individual''s genes", "analyse genomic data", "assess pollution exposure", "evaluate health programs and population interventions"]', 3, 'Biostatistics evaluates health programs and population interventions such as immunization campaigns, health education and training of health workers.', '{"0": "That is personalized medicine, under genetics and genomics.", "1": "That is genetics, genomics or bioinformatics.", "2": "That is environmental health."}'),
  ('BIO-5276', 'Biostatistics', 'Mean', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 190 (§22.2.1)', 'The weights of four rabbits are 1.8 kg, 2.2 kg, 2.0 kg and 2.4 kg. Their mean weight is', '["8.4 kg", "2.1 kg", "2.0 kg", "2.2 kg"]', 1, 'Mean = sum of values / number of values = (1.8 + 2.2 + 2.0 + 2.4) / 4 = 8.4 / 4 = 2.1 kg.', '{"0": "8.4 kg is the sum; it must still be divided by 4.", "2": "2.0 kg is one of the values, not the average of all four.", "3": "2.2 kg is one value; the four values add to 8.4 kg."}'),
  ('BIO-5277', 'Biostatistics', 'Mean', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 191 (§22.2.1)', 'Grouped lengths: 0–20 cm (f = 3), 20–40 cm (f = 5), 40–60 cm (f = 2). The mean length is', '["28 cm", "93.3 cm", "30 cm", "56 cm"]', 0, 'Midpoints are 10, 30 and 50. Σfixi = 30 + 150 + 100 = 280; Σfi = 10; mean = 280/10 = 28 cm.', '{"1": "This divides 280 by the 3 classes; the divisor must be the total frequency, 10.", "2": "30 cm is the midpoint of the most frequent class, not the weighted mean.", "3": "This divides 280 by the largest frequency, 5, instead of the total frequency."}'),
  ('BIO-5278', 'Biostatistics', 'Median', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 192 (§22.2.2)', 'Heights of seven seedlings (cm): 9, 4, 12, 7, 15, 6, 10. Their median height is', '["15 cm", "7 cm", "9 cm", "10 cm"]', 2, 'Ordered: 4, 6, 7, 9, 10, 12, 15. With n = 7 (odd), the median is the value at position (n + 1)/2 = 4, which is 9 cm.', '{"0": "15 cm is the 4th value in the unordered list; the data must be ordered first.", "1": "7 cm is the 3rd ordered value.", "3": "10 cm is the 5th ordered value, one place past the middle."}'),
  ('BIO-5279', 'Biostatistics', 'Mode', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 193 (§22.2.3)', 'Grouped data: 10–20 (f = 5), 20–30 (f = 12), 30–40 (f = 18), 40–50 (f = 10), 50–60 (f = 3). In the grouped mode formula, the value of f2 is', '["3", "10", "18", "12"]', 1, 'f2 is the frequency of the class just after the modal class. The modal class is 30–40 (f = 18), so f2 is the frequency of 40–50, which is 10.', '{"0": "3 is the frequency of the last class, not the one next to the modal class.", "2": "18 is fm, the frequency of the modal class itself.", "3": "12 is f1, the frequency of the class before the modal class."}'),
  ('BIO-5280', 'Biostatistics', 'Mode', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 193 (§22.2.3)', 'Grouped data: 0–5 (f = 3), 5–10 (f = 9), 10–15 (f = 15), 15–20 (f = 6). Using mode = l + [(fm − f1)/((fm − f1) + (fm − f2))] × h, the mode is', '["12.5", "13.0", "10.0", "12.0"]', 3, 'Modal class 10–15: l = 10, fm = 15, f1 = 9, f2 = 6, h = 5. Mode = 10 + [6/(6 + 9)] × 5 = 10 + 0.4 × 5 = 12.0.', '{"0": "12.5 is just the midpoint of the modal class; the formula shifts it toward the larger neighbour.", "1": "This uses 9/15 instead of 6/15 for the fraction.", "2": "10 is only the lower boundary l, before the correction is added."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5281', 'Biostatistics', 'Standard deviation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 194 (§22.2.4)', 'In the book''s figure of two bell-shaped curves, the curve that is tall and narrow around the mean has', '["no mean", "a higher standard deviation", "a lower standard deviation", "a larger range"]', 2, 'A curve clustered closely around the mean has a lower standard deviation; a more spread-out curve has a higher one.', '{"0": "Every such curve has a mean at its centre.", "1": "A higher SD gives a wider, more spread-out curve.", "3": "Narrow clustering means values lie close together, so the spread is smaller."}'),
  ('BIO-5282', 'Biostatistics', 'Standard deviation', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 195 (§22.2.4)', 'Five leaf lengths have a sum of squared deviations from the mean of 80 cm². Dividing by N, as in the book, the standard deviation is', '["2 cm", "4 cm", "16 cm", "about 8.9 cm"]', 1, 'Variance = Σ(xi − µ)²/N = 80/5 = 16 cm²; SD = √16 = 4 cm.', '{"0": "2 is √4; the variance here is 16, not 4.", "2": "16 is the variance; the SD is its square root.", "3": "8.9 is √80; the sum must first be divided by N = 5."}'),
  ('BIO-5283', 'Biostatistics', 'Standard deviation', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 194 (§22.2.4)', 'What is the first step in calculating a standard deviation?', '["Find the mean of the data", "Arrange the data and pick the middle value", "Take the square root of each value", "Find the largest and smallest values"]', 0, 'The steps are: find the mean, subtract it from each value, square the deviations, average them, then take the square root.', '{"1": "That finds the median.", "2": "The square root is taken only at the last step, of the averaged squared deviations.", "3": "Those give the range, a different measure of spread."}'),
  ('BIO-5284', 'Biostatistics', 'Range', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 195 (§22.2.5)', 'Body weights in mouse group P have a range of 4 g and in group Q a range of 15 g. This shows that', '["group P has the higher mean weight", "group Q has more mice", "group Q has the smaller standard deviation", "weights in group P are more closely clustered"]', 3, 'A smaller range means the data points are closely clustered; a larger range means they are more dispersed.', '{"0": "The range measures spread, not the average value.", "1": "The range does not depend on how many values there are, only on the extremes.", "2": "A wider range usually goes with greater spread, not less."}'),
  ('BIO-5285', 'Biostatistics', 'Percentile', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 197 (§22.2.6)', 'Ordered heights (cm): 160, 162, 165, 168, 170, 172, 175, 178, 180, 182. Using R = (P/100)(n + 1) and interpolation, the 60th percentile is', '["170 cm", "175 cm", "173.8 cm", "172 cm"]', 2, 'R = 0.6 × 11 = 6.6. The 6th value is 172 and the 7th is 175, so P60 = 172 + 0.6 × (175 − 172) = 172 + 1.8 = 173.8 cm.', '{"0": "170 cm is the 5th value.", "1": "175 cm is the 7th value.", "3": "172 cm is the 6th value only; the fractional rank 0.6 needs interpolation."}'),
  ('BIO-5286', 'Biostatistics', 'Bar chart', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 197 (§22.3.1)', 'Which statement about a bar chart is correct according to the book?', '["Bars can be drawn either vertically or horizontally", "The whole chart always represents 360°", "The bar widths show the values", "It is used mainly for continuous data such as time"]', 0, 'The book lists that bars in a bar chart can be oriented vertically or horizontally, and bar length is proportional to the value.', '{"1": "That describes a pie chart.", "2": "Bars are of equal width; their length or height shows the value.", "3": "A bar chart displays categorical data such as species or groups."}'),
  ('BIO-5287', 'Biostatistics', 'Bar chart', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 198 (§22.3.1)', 'In the insect survey, the highest count is 120 ants. The book scales the y-axis from 0 to 140 in steps of 20 mainly for', '["making the ant bar look smaller than it is", "good readability while fitting the largest value", "placing the species names on the y-axis", "showing percentages that add to 360"]', 1, 'The y-axis is scaled slightly above the greatest value (120), and increments of 10 or 20 are chosen for good readability.', '{"0": "Scaling must show values accurately, not distort them.", "2": "Species, the categories, go on the x-axis.", "3": "Angles summing to 360° belong to pie charts."}'),
  ('BIO-5288', 'Biostatistics', 'Pie chart', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 199 (§22.3.2)', 'In a survey of 200 patients, 25 have blood group AB. On a pie chart, the sector angle for AB is', '["12.5°", "25°", "90°", "45°"]', 3, 'Sector angle = (category value / total) × 360° = 25/200 × 360° = 45°.', '{"0": "12.5 is the percentage (12.5%), not the angle in degrees.", "1": "25 is the number of patients, not the angle.", "2": "90° would be one quarter of the patients, 50 out of 200."}'),
  ('BIO-5289', 'Biostatistics', 'X-axis data figure', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 201 (§22.3.2, X-axis data figure)', 'In the book''s enzyme activity figure, activity is 2, 4, 7, 5 and 3 µmol/min at 5, 10, 20, 30 and 40 °C. At which temperature was the highest activity recorded?', '["5 °C", "20 °C", "40 °C", "30 °C"]', 1, 'The highest y-value (7 µmol/min) was measured at 20 °C; activity rose to this peak and then fell at higher temperatures.', '{"0": "Activity at 5 °C was the lowest, 2 µmol/min.", "2": "Activity at 40 °C was only 3 µmol/min.", "3": "Activity at 30 °C was 5 µmol/min, lower than at 20 °C."}'),
  ('BIO-5290', 'Biostatistics', 'X-axis data figure', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 200 (§22.3.2, X-axis data figure)', 'According to the book, the x-axis of a data figure', '["usually shows the dependent variable", "is always vertical", "can never start at zero", "runs horizontally and can include negative values when needed"]', 3, 'The x-axis is always horizontal, usually starts at the origin and extends in the positive direction, though it can include negative values when needed.', '{"0": "The x-axis most commonly shows the independent variable.", "1": "The vertical axis is the y-axis.", "2": "It often starts at the origin (0, 0)."}'),
  ('BIO-5291', 'Biostatistics', 'Error bars', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 202 (§22.3.2, Error bars)', 'When drawing error bars on a bar chart, the book advises adding', '["a horizontal cap at each end of the vertical error bar", "a legend for each data point", "a separate pie chart for each group", "a line joining all the bar tops"]', 0, 'For clarity, a horizontal cap is added at each end of the vertical error bar.', '{"1": "A legend explains colours or patterns; it is not part of an error bar.", "2": "Error bars are drawn on the same bar chart.", "3": "Joining points with a line is done in an x-axis data figure to show a trend."}'),
  ('BIO-5292', 'Biostatistics', 'Error bars', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 203 (§22.3.2, Error bars)', 'Plant heights in a group are 14, 16 and 15 cm. Which pair of error bars is correct for this group?', '["Range bar from 0 to 16 cm; ±1 SD bar from 15 to 16 cm", "Range bar from 14 to 16 cm; ±1 SD bar from 12 to 18 cm", "Range bar from 14 to 16 cm; ±1 SD bar from 14 to 16 cm", "Range bar from 15 to 16 cm; ±1 SD bar from 13 to 17 cm"]', 2, 'Mean = 15 cm and SD ≈ 1 cm, so the ±1 SD bar runs from 14 to 16 cm. The range bar runs from the minimum (14) to the maximum (16). In this example both happen to coincide.', '{"0": "A range bar spans minimum to maximum, and an SD bar extends both above and below the mean.", "1": "An SD of about 1 cm gives 14 to 16 cm, not ±3 cm.", "3": "The range bar must start at the minimum, 14; ±1 SD is 14 to 16."}'),
  ('BIO-5293', 'Biostatistics', 'Experimental design', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 205 (§22.3 (Experimental design))', 'In the book''s mouse diet experiment, the mice are first selected so that they are matched by', '["the diet they will receive", "their final weight gain", "their weekly food preference", "age, strain and sex"]', 3, 'The procedure begins by selecting albino mice matched by age, strain and sex, then randomly assigning them to groups.', '{"0": "Diet is the independent variable, assigned randomly after selection.", "1": "Weight gain is the dependent variable, measured at the end.", "2": "Food preference is not a matching criterion in the book."}'),
  ('BIO-5294', 'Biostatistics', 'Experimental design', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 204 (§22.3 (Experimental design))', 'According to the book, a hypothesis is', '["the factor that is measured in the experiment", "the group that receives no treatment", "a statement of what you think will happen, based on existing knowledge", "the final conclusion drawn from the results"]', 2, 'Step 2 of experimental design is to formulate a hypothesis: what you think will happen, based on existing knowledge.', '{"0": "That is the dependent variable.", "1": "That is the control group.", "3": "Conclusions are drawn at the last step, where the hypothesis is accepted or rejected."}'),
  ('BIO-5295', 'Biostatistics', 'Experimental design', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 22 Biostatistics, p. 205 (§22.3 (Experimental design))', 'In the mouse diet experiment, all mice are kept in the same cages, light, temperature and humidity. This is done to', '["keep control variables constant so the test is fair", "measure the dependent variable", "create an extra experimental group", "change the independent variable"]', 0, 'Control variables (age, strain, sex, cage, light, temperature, feeding schedule) are kept the same so that only diet type differs between groups.', '{"1": "Weight gain is the measured variable; housing conditions are not measured outcomes.", "2": "Identical housing applies to both groups; it does not create a new group.", "3": "The independent variable is diet type; housing is deliberately kept identical."}'),
  ('BIO-5296', 'Pharmacology', 'Pharmacology', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 208 (chapter introduction)', 'According to the book, drugs are chemical substances used to', '["only kill bacteria", "diagnose, prevent or treat diseases", "produce energy in cells", "only replace missing vitamins"]', 1, 'Drugs are chemical substances used to diagnose, prevent or treat diseases; they may be natural or synthesized in laboratories.', '{"0": "Killing or stopping bacteria describes antibiotics, one group of drugs.", "2": "Energy production is a function of respiration, not of drugs.", "3": "Drugs have much wider uses than replacing vitamins."}'),
  ('BIO-5297', 'Pharmacology', 'Pharmacology', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 208 (chapter introduction)', 'Which statement about the sources of drugs is correct?', '["They may come from plants, animals and microorganisms or be synthesized in laboratories", "They are always synthesized in laboratories", "They come only from microorganisms", "They come only from plants"]', 0, 'The book states drugs may be obtained from plants, animals and microorganisms, or synthesized in laboratories.', '{"1": "Many drugs are natural, e.g. penicillin from Penicillium fungi.", "2": "Microorganisms are one of several sources.", "3": "Plants are one source; animals, microbes and laboratory synthesis are others."}'),
  ('BIO-5298', 'Pharmacology', 'Drug discovery', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 208 (§23.1.1)', 'In target identification, scientists look for', '["healthy volunteers for a safety trial", "a specific molecule, such as a protein or enzyme, involved in causing the disease", "rare side effects after marketing", "thousands of compounds that bind the target"]', 1, 'Target identification finds a specific molecule, such as a protein or enzyme, that causes the disease and can be affected by a drug.', '{"0": "Volunteers are used in Phase 1 clinical trials, during development.", "2": "That is post-marketing surveillance.", "3": "Screening compounds is lead compound identification, a later stage."}'),
  ('BIO-5299', 'Pharmacology', 'Drug development', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 209 (§23.1.2)', 'Before a drug is supplied to the market, all clinical trial data are examined to approve or reject it by', '["the scientists who found the lead compound alone", "the patients in Phase 3 trials", "regulatory agencies such as the FDA or EMA", "the bacteria being targeted"]', 2, 'In the FDA drug review stage, regulatory agencies (FDA or EMA) examine all clinical trial data to approve or reject the drug.', '{"0": "Approval is a regulatory decision, not made by the discoverers alone.", "1": "Patients take part in trials; they do not approve drugs.", "3": "Bacteria are the drug''s target, not reviewers."}'),
  ('BIO-5300', 'Pharmacology', 'Drug development', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 209 (§23.1.2)', 'Which order of drug development stages is correct?', '["Clinical trials → preclinical research → FDA review → marketing → surveillance", "Preclinical research → FDA review → clinical trials → marketing → surveillance", "Preclinical research → clinical trials → surveillance → FDA review → marketing", "Preclinical research → clinical trials → FDA review → manufacturing and marketing → post-marketing surveillance"]', 3, 'The book lists preclinical research, clinical trials, FDA drug review, manufacturing and marketing, then post-marketing surveillance.', '{"0": "Preclinical testing on cells and animals comes before any human trial.", "1": "Regulators review the data from clinical trials, so trials come first.", "2": "Surveillance happens after the drug is released."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5301', 'Pharmacology', 'Penicillin', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 210 (§23.2.1)', 'Penicillin antibiotics are obtained from', '["sulfanilamide", "Penicillium fungi", "Streptomyces bacteria", "the bark of the Cinchona tree"]', 1, 'Penicillin is a group of bactericidal antibiotics derived from Penicillium fungi.', '{"0": "Sulfonamides are derived from sulfanilamide.", "2": "Tetracycline is derived from soil-dwelling Streptomyces.", "3": "Quinine comes from Cinchona bark; fluoroquinolones are linked to its synthetic derivative."}'),
  ('BIO-5302', 'Pharmacology', 'Penicillin', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 210 (§23.2.1)', 'Which of these is listed in the book as a common side effect of penicillin?', '["Skin rashes", "Peripheral neuropathy", "Tendon rupture", "Kidney damage"]', 0, 'Common side effects of penicillin include nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, abdominal pain and skin rashes.', '{"1": "This is listed among fluoroquinolone adverse effects.", "2": "Tendon rupture is a rare adverse effect of fluoroquinolones.", "3": "Kidney damage is listed for sulfonamides."}'),
  ('BIO-5303', 'Pharmacology', 'Tetracycline', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 211 (§23.2.2)', 'A patient taking tetracycline should avoid taking it with', '["bread", "fruit", "dairy products or iron supplements", "water"]', 2, 'The book states tetracycline should not be taken with dairy or iron supplements.', '{"0": "No such restriction is given for bread.", "1": "No such restriction is given for fruit.", "3": "Tetracycline is an oral antibiotic normally swallowed with water."}'),
  ('BIO-5304', 'Pharmacology', 'Tetracycline', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 211 (§23.2.2)', 'Tetracycline is used in the book''s examples to treat', '["HIV infection", "influenza and herpes", "fungal skin infections", "chlamydia and syphilis"]', 3, 'Tetracycline is a bacteriostatic oral antibiotic used for infections such as chlamydia and syphilis.', '{"0": "HIV is a retrovirus, treated with antiretroviral drugs.", "1": "These are viral infections, treated with antiviral drugs.", "2": "Tetracycline is an antibacterial drug."}'),
  ('BIO-5305', 'Pharmacology', 'Fluoroquinolones', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 211 (§23.2.3)', 'Fluoroquinolones are synthetic agents that arose as a by-product of the synthesis of', '["sulfanilamide", "penicillin, from Penicillium", "chloroquine, a derivative of quinine", "tetracycline, from Streptomyces"]', 2, 'Fluoroquinolones were derived as a by-product of chloroquine synthesis; chloroquine is a modified form of quinine from Cinchona bark.', '{"0": "Sulfanilamide is the parent compound of sulfonamides.", "1": "Penicillin is a natural fungal antibiotic, not the parent of fluoroquinolones.", "3": "Tetracycline comes from soil bacteria."}'),
  ('BIO-5306', 'Pharmacology', 'Fluoroquinolones', 'hard', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 211 (§23.2.3)', 'In Gram-positive bacteria, fluoroquinolones mainly target', '["DNA gyrase, stopping DNA uncoiling", "the enzyme that makes folate", "the 30S ribosomal subunit", "topoisomerase IV, stopping separation of replicated DNA"]', 3, 'Topoisomerase IV is the main target in Gram-positive bacteria, where its inhibition stops separation of replicated DNA; DNA gyrase is the main target in Gram-negative bacteria.', '{"0": "DNA gyrase is the main target in Gram-negative bacteria.", "1": "This is the target of sulfonamides.", "2": "This is the target of tetracycline."}'),
  ('BIO-5307', 'Pharmacology', 'Fluoroquinolones', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 211 (§23.2.3)', 'Fluoroquinolones were discovered by', '["Gerhard Domagk in 1932", "George Lesher in 1962", "Alexander Fleming in 1928", "Louis Pasteur in 1885"]', 1, 'The book credits George Lesher (1962) with discovering fluoroquinolones.', '{"0": "Domagk discovered sulfanilamide-based sulfonamides in 1932.", "2": "Fleming discovered penicillin in 1928.", "3": "Pasteur is not linked to fluoroquinolones in the book."}'),
  ('BIO-5308', 'Pharmacology', 'Sulfonamides', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 212 (§23.2.4)', 'Sulfonamides block bacterial folate synthesis by acting as', '["competitive inhibitors in the para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) pathway", "trappers of type II topoisomerases", "blockers of the 30S ribosomal subunit", "enzymes that break the beta-lactam ring"]', 0, 'Sulfonamides are competitive inhibitors: they bind the enzyme that makes folate, taking the place of para-aminobenzoic acid in the folate pathway.', '{"1": "That is how fluoroquinolones act.", "2": "That is how tetracycline acts.", "3": "Beta-lactamases are bacterial enzymes that cause penicillin resistance."}'),
  ('BIO-5309', 'Pharmacology', 'Sulfonamides', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 212 (§23.2.4)', 'Because sulfonamides are bacteriostatic, the bacteria in an infection are finally removed by', '["beta-lactamase enzymes", "the body''s immune system", "double-strand breaks in DNA", "the drug lysing the cell wall"]', 1, 'Sulfonamides inhibit bacterial growth rather than killing immediately, allowing the immune system to destroy the bacteria.', '{"0": "These are bacterial enzymes that destroy penicillin.", "2": "These are caused by fluoroquinolones.", "3": "Lysis through cell wall damage is how penicillin kills."}'),
  ('BIO-5310', 'Pharmacology', 'Sulfonamides', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 212 (§23.2.4)', 'Sulfonamides are used mainly for', '["urinary tract and skin infections", "HIV infection", "cancer", "viral hepatitis"]', 0, 'Sulfonamides are used primarily for urinary tract and skin infections; the book also mentions acne and respiratory infections.', '{"1": "HIV needs antiretroviral drugs.", "2": "Monoclonal antibodies, not sulfonamides, are described for cancer.", "3": "Hepatitis is viral and needs antiviral drugs."}'),
  ('BIO-5311', 'Pharmacology', 'Antivirals', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 212 (§23.3)', 'Antiviral drugs are described as able to combat', '["Gram-positive bacteria", "only HIV", "Penicillium fungi", "hepatitis, influenza and herpes viruses"]', 3, 'Antiviral drugs prevent growth and multiplication of viruses, including hepatitis, influenza and herpes viruses.', '{"0": "Bacteria are treated with antibiotics.", "1": "HIV is treated by antiretrovirals, a subset of antivirals; antivirals cover many viruses.", "2": "Penicillium is the source of penicillin, not a disease target."}'),
  ('BIO-5312', 'Pharmacology', 'Monoclonal antibodies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 212 (§23.4)', 'Besides cancer, monoclonal antibodies are described as effective for treating', '["chromosomal disorders", "vitamin deficiencies", "autoimmune disorders and infectious diseases", "only bacterial cell wall defects"]', 2, 'Monoclonal antibodies target specific antigens, making them effective against cancer, autoimmune disorders and infectious diseases.', '{"0": "No such use is described.", "1": "These are treated by supplying the missing vitamin.", "3": "Monoclonal antibodies bind specific antigens; they do not act on cell walls like penicillin."}'),
  ('BIO-5313', 'Pharmacology', 'Monoclonal antibodies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 213 (§23.4.2)', 'Which of these is listed in the book as an advantage of monoclonal antibodies?', '["They have more side effects than chemotherapy", "They are taken with dairy to improve absorption", "They act on every antigen at once", "They reduce the risk of drug interactions"]', 3, 'The listed advantages include lower toxicity, fewer side effects, high specificity, targeted cancer therapy, ease of preparation for research and reduced risk of drug interactions.', '{"0": "They have fewer side effects than conventional chemotherapy.", "1": "Avoiding dairy is advice for tetracycline.", "2": "They are highly specific, binding a single target."}'),
  ('BIO-5314', 'Pharmacology', 'Monoclonal antibodies', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 23 Pharmacology, p. 212 (§23.4.2)', 'Monoclonal antibodies are called integral to personalized medicine because they', '["replace the need for diagnosis", "allow treatment to be tailored to an individual patient''s profile", "are the same for every patient and every disease", "are made naturally by the patient after a vaccine"]', 1, 'The book says monoclonal antibodies enable tailored treatment approaches based on individual patient profiles.', '{"0": "Tailoring depends on knowing the patient''s profile.", "2": "Their value is specificity and tailoring, not one-size-fits-all.", "3": "They are artificially produced in the laboratory."}'),
  ('BIO-5315', 'Evolution', 'Concept of evolution', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 215 (§24.1)', 'The early belief that living creatures can develop from non-living matter was called', '["creationism", "endosymbiosis", "spontaneous generation", "natural selection"]', 2, 'Early people believed living creatures could arise from non-living matter, the idea of spontaneous generation, later disproved by experiments.', '{"0": "Creationism holds that each type of organism was created in its present form.", "1": "Endosymbiosis explains the origin of eukaryotic organelles from engulfed prokaryotes.", "3": "Natural selection is Darwin''s mechanism acting on inherited variation."}'),
  ('BIO-5316', 'Evolution', 'Concept of evolution', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 215 (§24.1)', 'Which was one of the basic speculations of evolution in its earliest form?', '["Today''s organisms are the result of innumerable changes from lower to higher forms", "Acquired characters are passed to offspring", "Mitochondria came from engulfed bacteria", "All organisms were created at once in their present form"]', 0, 'The earliest speculations were that the universe and organisms did not always exist in their present form, were not created at once, and arose by innumerable changes from lower to higher forms.', '{"1": "This is Lamarck''s later mechanism, not the basic speculation.", "2": "This is the much later endosymbiotic theory.", "3": "This is the creationist view that early evolutionary ideas opposed."}'),
  ('BIO-5317', 'Evolution', 'Darwinism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 216 (§24.2)', 'Finding rocks with fossils of gigantic extinct mammals during the Beagle voyage suggested to Darwin that', '["living things change over time", "all species were created on the Galápagos", "species never become extinct", "acquired characters are inherited"]', 0, 'Fossils showed organisms looked very different in the past, suggesting that living things change over time.', '{"1": "The fossils were not evidence of creation in one place.", "2": "The fossils were of extinct mammals, so extinction does happen.", "3": "Darwin did not draw this Lamarckian idea from fossils."}'),
  ('BIO-5318', 'Evolution', 'Darwinism', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 216 (§24.2)', 'The Galápagos Islands visited by Darwin are described as a group of', '["one single island", "2 large continents", "90 islands near Australia", "16 small islands"]', 3, 'The book describes the Galápagos as a group of 16 small islands, where plants and animals differed from island to island.', '{"0": "Darwin compared tortoises and finches between different islands.", "1": "The Galápagos are small oceanic islands.", "2": "They lie in the Pacific near South America, not near Australia."}'),
  ('BIO-5319', 'Evolution', 'Darwinism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 216 (§24.2)', 'According to Darwin''s thinking on the finches, a new species arises when', '["an individual stretches its organs during its lifetime", "it is created in a new habitat in its present form", "a population separated from the original habitat gradually accumulates adaptations to a different environment", "two species merge into one by interbreeding"]', 2, 'Darwin reasoned that a new species arises from an ancestral form by gradual accumulation of adaptations to a different environment after separation, until the populations are dissimilar enough to be separate species.', '{"0": "This is Lamarck''s use-and-disuse idea.", "1": "This is creationism.", "3": "Darwin described divergence of populations, not merging."}'),
  ('BIO-5320', 'Evolution', 'Natural selection', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 217 (§24.2.1)', 'When Darwin''s and Wallace''s ideas were presented to the Linnaean Society in 1858, Wallace', '["proposed the inheritance of acquired characters", "agreed to attribute the theory to Darwin and urged him to publish", "rejected the idea of evolution", "claimed sole credit for natural selection"]', 1, 'Wallace, who had reached a very similar mechanism, agreed to attribute the theory to Darwin and convinced him to publish On the Origin of Species.', '{"0": "That was Lamarck''s idea, published in 1809.", "2": "Wallace had independently proposed a very similar mechanism.", "3": "Wallace attributed the theory to Darwin."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5321', 'Evolution', 'Natural selection', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 217 (§24.2.1)', 'In Darwin''s theory, a genetic variation that is neither helpful nor harmful is called', '["vestigial", "neutral", "adaptive", "acquired"]', 1, 'Variations arising by random mutation may be advantageous, harmful or neutral (neither helpful nor harmful).', '{"0": "Vestigial refers to structures that have lost their usefulness.", "2": "Adaptive traits help individuals survive and reproduce.", "3": "Acquired characters are Lamarck''s term for changes through use or disuse."}'),
  ('BIO-5322', 'Evolution', 'Natural selection', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 218 (§24.2.1)', 'In Darwin''s theory, individuals must struggle for survival because', '["many more offspring are produced than resources can support", "mutations never occur", "organs weaken when used", "all individuals are exactly alike"]', 0, 'Because far more offspring are produced than resources can support, individuals compete; those with disadvantageous variations die or fail to reproduce.', '{"1": "Variation arises from random mutation.", "2": "This reverses Lamarck''s idea and is not part of Darwin''s theory.", "3": "Darwin stressed that individuals are seldom exactly alike."}'),
  ('BIO-5323', 'Evolution', 'Hardy-Weinberg', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 218 (§24.2.2)', 'In a population of 100 plants, 40 have genotype RR. The genotype frequency of RR is', '["0.8", "0.16", "0.6", "0.4"]', 3, 'Genotype frequency = number of individuals with that genotype / total = 40/100 = 0.4 (40%).', '{"0": "0.8 would count each RR plant''s two R alleles against 100 individuals.", "1": "0.16 squares 0.4, which would treat 0.4 as an allele frequency.", "2": "0.6 is the proportion of plants that are not RR."}'),
  ('BIO-5324', 'Evolution', 'Hardy-Weinberg', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 218 (§24.2.2)', 'Which is one of the Hardy-Weinberg conditions for no evolution?', '["Individuals must choose mates with similar traits", "The population must be small", "All genotypes have an equal chance of survival and reproduction", "Individuals must migrate in and out freely"]', 2, 'Conditions are a large population, random mating, no migration, no mutation (or mutational equilibrium) and equal survival and reproduction of all genotypes.', '{"0": "Mating must be random.", "1": "The population must be large; small populations are prone to genetic drift.", "3": "Migration causes gene flow, which changes allele frequencies."}'),
  ('BIO-5325', 'Evolution', 'Mutation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 219 (§24.2.2)', 'A measure of the tendency for gene frequencies to change through mutation is called', '["gene flow", "mutational equilibrium", "mutation pressure", "genetic drift"]', 2, 'Mutation pressure measures the tendency for gene frequencies to change through mutation.', '{"0": "Gene flow is change due to migration of individuals.", "1": "Mutational equilibrium is when forward and back mutations balance, so frequencies do not change.", "3": "Genetic drift is change in frequencies by chance events."}'),
  ('BIO-5326', 'Evolution', 'Mutation', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 219 (§24.2.2)', 'According to the book, most mutations are', '["always neutral", "caused by use and disuse of organs", "beneficial in every environment", "deleterious"]', 3, 'The effects of mutations vary; most are deleterious, while some may be neutral, or harmful in one environment and helpful in another.', '{"0": "Some are neutral, but most are deleterious.", "1": "Use and disuse is Lamarck''s idea; it does not cause mutations.", "2": "Only some mutations help, and often only in a particular environment."}'),
  ('BIO-5327', 'Evolution', 'Genetic drift', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 219 (§24.2.2)', 'The effects of genetic drift are most obvious in', '["populations with heavy immigration", "small populations", "populations of prokaryotes only", "very large, randomly mating populations"]', 1, 'Genetic drift changes gene frequencies by chance and is most obvious in small populations.', '{"0": "Immigration causes gene flow, a different factor.", "2": "Drift affects any population, and is linked to size, not cell type.", "3": "Large random-mating populations tend to stay near Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium."}'),
  ('BIO-5328', 'Evolution', 'Biogeography', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 220 (§24.3.1)', 'Many animals of Australia and Tasmania, such as kangaroos, differ greatly from animals elsewhere in similar environments. Biogeography explains this as', '["separation by geographical barriers allowing distinct evolution", "creation of separate species on each continent", "inheritance of characters acquired by use", "convergent evolution from the same ancestor"]', 0, 'Organisms separated by geographical barriers are often very different in spite of similar environments, as with Australian kangaroos.', '{"1": "This is creationism, not a biogeographic explanation.", "2": "This is Lamarckism.", "3": "Convergent evolution involves different ancestors and analogous structures."}'),
  ('BIO-5329', 'Evolution', 'Comparative anatomy', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 221 (§24.3.2)', 'The forelimbs of bats, whales, horses and climbing lizards share a common bone arrangement. This illustrates', '["analogy, showing convergent evolution", "homology, showing divergent evolution", "genetic drift", "vestigial organs"]', 1, 'Vertebrate appendages have a common arrangement of bones though used for flying, swimming, running or climbing; these homologous structures show divergent evolution from a common ancestor.', '{"0": "Analogous organs share function but differ in structure.", "2": "Drift is a chance change in allele frequencies, not an anatomical pattern.", "3": "These limbs are fully functional, not remnants."}'),
  ('BIO-5330', 'Evolution', 'Comparative anatomy', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 221 (§24.3.2)', 'According to the book, the similarities between the wings of birds and insects simply reflect that', '["they contain the same bones", "birds evolved from insects", "to fly, an animal must have a broad, flat gliding surface", "they are vestigial organs"]', 2, 'The similarity of bird and insect wings is not due to common ancestry; any flying animal needs a broad, flat gliding surface.', '{"0": "Insect wings contain no bones.", "1": "Bird and insect wings are not homologous; they come from different ancestors.", "3": "Both are functional flight organs."}'),
  ('BIO-5331', 'Evolution', 'Vestigial organs', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 221 (§24.3.2)', 'About how many vestigial structures are said to be present in the human body?', '["About 90", "None", "About 9", "About 500"]', 0, 'The book states that there are about 90 vestigial structures in the human body, such as the appendix and coccyx.', '{"1": "Humans do have vestigial structures, such as the coccyx and wisdom teeth.", "2": "The number given is far higher.", "3": "This is far more than the number given."}'),
  ('BIO-5332', 'Evolution', 'Vestigial organs', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 221 (§24.3.2)', 'Which pair correctly matches a human vestigial structure with what it represents?', '["Appendix — remnant of a hindlimb", "Ear muscles — vestige of the caecum", "Wisdom teeth — remnant of a tail", "Coccyx — remnant of a tail"]', 3, 'The coccyx (tailbone) is the remnant of a tail; wisdom teeth are vestigial third molars that ancestors used to grind plant tissue.', '{"0": "Hindlimb remnants are found in boas and pythons; the appendix is a vestige of the caecum.", "1": "The appendix is the vestige of the caecum; ear muscles are minimally developed muscles.", "2": "Wisdom teeth are vestigial third molars."}'),
  ('BIO-5333', 'Evolution', 'Molecular biology', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 221 (§24.3.3)', 'Related animals have similar proteins mainly because', '["they live in the same habitat", "proteins are inherited by use and disuse", "they have similar DNA, which carries the codes for their proteins", "they eat similar foods"]', 2, 'Related animals have similar DNA derived from a common ancestor, and because DNA codes for proteins, they also have similar proteins.', '{"0": "Shared habitat can produce analogous features, not shared protein sequences.", "1": "Protein structure is coded genetically; acquired characters are not inherited.", "3": "Diet does not determine protein structure."}'),
  ('BIO-5334', 'Evolution', 'Endosymbiotic theory', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 222 (§24.4)', 'According to the endosymbiotic theory, chloroplasts arose from engulfed small prokaryotes that', '["resembled spirochetes", "could carry out aerobic respiration", "formed from the folded cell membrane", "could use sunlight to make food"]', 3, 'Small engulfed cells that could use sunlight to make food shared it with the host and became chloroplasts; aerobic ones became mitochondria.', '{"0": "Flagella may have come from spirochete-like prokaryotes.", "1": "These became mitochondria.", "2": "Membrane infolding explains the nucleus and ER in the invagination theory."}'),
  ('BIO-5335', 'Evolution', 'Endosymbiotic theory', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 222 (§24.4)', 'According to the book, eukaryotic flagella may have been derived from ingested prokaryotes similar to', '["viruses", "spirochetes", "aerobic bacteria", "photosynthetic prokaryotes"]', 1, 'The book suggests flagella may have come from ingestion of prokaryotes similar to spirochetes.', '{"0": "Viruses are not cells and are not linked to flagella in the theory.", "2": "Aerobic prokaryotes are thought to have become mitochondria.", "3": "These are thought to have become chloroplasts."}'),
  ('BIO-5336', 'Evolution', 'Endosymbiotic theory', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 222 (§24.4)', 'Which observation is given as evidence for the endosymbiotic theory?', '["Mitochondrial membranes are more similar in composition to prokaryotic membranes than to eukaryotic ones", "Mitochondria divide by mitosis like eukaryotic cells", "Mitochondria have no DNA of their own", "Mitochondria are much larger than any prokaryote"]', 0, 'Evidence includes mitochondria and chloroplasts being prokaryote-sized, having circular DNA, dividing by binary fission and having membranes more like prokaryotic ones.', '{"1": "They divide by binary fission; eukaryotic cells divide by mitosis.", "2": "They have their own circular DNA.", "3": "They are about the same size as prokaryotic cells."}'),
  ('BIO-5337', 'Evolution', 'Endosymbiotic theory', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 222 (§24.4)', 'According to the book, the power and efficiency of eukaryotic cells formed by endosymbiosis enabled them to evolve', '["a smaller size than prokaryotes", "reproduction only by binary fission", "the ability to live without any organelles", "multicellularity, cell specialization and large size"]', 3, 'Eukaryotic cells were powerful and efficient, which enabled new characteristics such as multicellularity, cell specialization and large size.', '{"0": "Large size is one of the new characteristics listed.", "1": "Eukaryotic cells reproduce by mitosis.", "2": "Eukaryotes gained organelles through endosymbiosis."}'),
  ('BIO-5338', 'Evolution', 'Lamarckism', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 223 (§24.5)', 'Jean-Baptiste Lamarck was a', '["German physician", "French naturalist", "English mathematician", "British naturalist who sailed on HMS Beagle"]', 1, 'Lamarck (1744–1829) was a French naturalist and an early proponent of evolution.', '{"0": "Wilhelm Weinberg was a German physician.", "2": "Godfrey Hardy was an English mathematician.", "3": "That describes Charles Darwin."}'),
  ('BIO-5339', 'Evolution', 'Lamarckism', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 24 Evolution, p. 224 (§24.5.1)', 'Besides the lack of a genetic basis for acquired characters, the book gives which other reason for rejecting Lamarckism?', '["Fossils show no change over time", "Mutations were found never to occur", "Organs are not modified by the wish or requirements of an organism", "Lamarck did not believe organisms change"]', 2, 'Lamarckism was disproved because acquired characters have no genetic basis, and because organs are not modified by an organism''s wish or needs.', '{"0": "Fossils show that organisms change over time.", "1": "Mutations do occur and are a source of variation.", "3": "Lamarck did propose that organisms change from simple to complex."}'),
  ('BIO-5340', 'Ecology', 'Levels of organization', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 228 (§25.1)', 'Ecology is defined in the book as the study of', '["the relationships of organisms with other organisms and the environment", "the statistical characteristics of populations only", "the structure of cells and their organelles", "the inheritance of traits from parents to offspring"]', 0, 'Ecology is the study of the relationships of organisms with other organisms and with the environment.', '{"1": "That is demography, one part of population ecology.", "2": "That is cell biology.", "3": "That is genetics."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5341', 'Ecology', 'Levels of organization', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 228 (§25.1)', 'The Indus River dolphin is called a distinct species because Indus dolphins', '["all live in the same stretch of river", "breed with one another but cannot produce fertile young with other types of dolphins", "interact with fish and birds in the river", "live in fresh water"]', 1, 'A species is a group that shares characteristics and can interbreed to produce fertile offspring; Indus dolphins cannot do so with other dolphin types.', '{"0": "Living in one area at one time describes a population.", "2": "Interacting populations of different species form a community.", "3": "Habitat alone does not define a species."}'),
  ('BIO-5342', 'Ecology', 'Levels of organization', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 229 (§25.1)', 'Mangrove trees, mudskippers, crabs and shorebirds living and interacting in a mangrove forest near Karachi together form a', '["community", "population", "species", "biosphere"]', 0, 'A community consists of all the populations of different species living and interacting in the same area.', '{"1": "A population contains only one species.", "2": "A species is a single interbreeding type of organism.", "3": "The biosphere is the global sum of all biomes."}'),
  ('BIO-5343', 'Ecology', 'Levels of organization', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 229 (§25.1)', 'The Thal and Thar regions, with low rainfall and succulent plants such as cacti, are given as examples of the', '["coniferous forest biome", "freshwater biome", "mangrove biome", "desert biome"]', 3, 'The desert biome in Pakistan is located in the Thal and Thar regions, with low rainfall and succulent plants.', '{"0": "This is found at high altitude in Murree and Swat.", "1": "The Indus River system is the example of a freshwater biome.", "2": "Mangroves are coastal salt-tolerant forests."}'),
  ('BIO-5344', 'Ecology', 'Levels of organization', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 229 (§25.1)', 'The biosphere is often called the', '["primary reservoir", "carrying capacity", "Global Ecosystem", "trophic level"]', 2, 'The biosphere, a thin envelope where land, water and air interact to support life, is often called the Global Ecosystem.', '{"0": "A primary reservoir is where an element of a cycle is stored for long periods.", "1": "Carrying capacity is the maximum population an environment can support.", "3": "A trophic level is a feeding group in an ecosystem."}'),
  ('BIO-5345', 'Ecology', 'Biogeochemical cycles', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 230 (§25.2)', 'In gaseous biogeochemical cycles, the primary reservoir is', '["the bodies of producers", "the root nodules of legumes", "the Earth''s crust", "the air or the oceans"]', 3, 'In gaseous cycles (nitrogen, oxygen, carbon, water) the reservoir is the air or the oceans; in sedimentary cycles it is the Earth''s crust.', '{"0": "Organisms hold elements only briefly, not as a long-term reservoir.", "1": "Root nodules hold nitrogen-fixing bacteria, not a reservoir.", "2": "The crust is the reservoir of sedimentary cycles such as phosphorus."}'),
  ('BIO-5346', 'Ecology', 'Water cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 231 (§25.2.1)', 'During condensation in the water cycle, the droplets form clouds by', '["clinging to dust particles in the air", "absorbing heat from the sun", "falling to the ground due to gravity", "seeping into the soil"]', 0, 'As rising vapour cools, it turns into droplets or ice crystals that cling to dust particles to form clouds.', '{"1": "Vapour loses heat as it rises; heat absorption drives evaporation.", "2": "Falling under gravity is precipitation.", "3": "Seeping into soil is infiltration."}'),
  ('BIO-5347', 'Ecology', 'Water cycle', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 231 (§25.2.1)', 'The book describes sleet as', '["water flowing over land into rivers", "rain mingled with snow or hail", "water released from leaf stomata", "water vapour rising from oceans"]', 1, 'Depending on temperature, precipitation falls as rain, snow or sleet, which is rain mingled with snow or hail.', '{"0": "That is surface runoff.", "2": "That is transpiration.", "3": "That is evaporation."}'),
  ('BIO-5348', 'Ecology', 'Water cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 231 (§25.2.1)', 'Surface runoff occurs when', '["decomposers break down dead bodies", "water vapour cools in the atmosphere", "water can no longer infiltrate into the soil", "plants absorb water through root hairs"]', 2, 'Surface runoff is the unconfined flow of water over land, occurring when water can no longer infiltrate; it flows into streams, rivers and oceans.', '{"0": "Decomposition releases some water but is not runoff.", "1": "That leads to condensation.", "3": "That is water entering the biotic component."}'),
  ('BIO-5349', 'Ecology', 'Water cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 231 (§25.2.1)', 'Which is NOT one of the routes by which water leaves the biotic component, according to the book?', '["Transpiration from leaves", "Perspiration through skin pores", "Excretion in urine", "Absorption through root hairs"]', 3, 'Water leaves organisms by transpiration, metabolic water from respiration, excretion and egestion, perspiration and decomposition. Root hairs take water into plants.', '{"0": "Transpiration is the major exit point for water.", "1": "Sweat evaporates into the atmosphere, an exit route.", "2": "Urine returns water to the soil or water bodies."}'),
  ('BIO-5350', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 232 (§25.2.2)', 'Which of these is a free-living (non-symbiotic) nitrogen-fixing organism?', '["Azotobacter", "Pseudomonas", "Rhizobium", "Nitrobacter"]', 0, 'Free-living nitrogen fixers include cyanobacteria, Azotobacter and Clostridium; Rhizobium is symbiotic in legume root nodules.', '{"1": "Pseudomonas is a denitrifying bacterium.", "2": "Rhizobium lives symbiotically in root nodules of legumes.", "3": "Nitrobacter carries out nitrification (nitrite to nitrate)."}'),
  ('BIO-5351', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 232 (§25.2.2)', 'About what share of nitrogen is fixed by lightning (atmospheric fixation)?', '["78%", "5–10%", "About 50%", "More than 90%"]', 1, 'Only a small amount, about 5–10%, of nitrogen is fixed by atmospheric (lightning) fixation; more than 90% is biological.', '{"0": "78% is the proportion of nitrogen gas in the atmosphere.", "2": "The book gives a much smaller share.", "3": "More than 90% is fixed by soil microorganisms."}'),
  ('BIO-5352', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 233 (§25.2.2)', 'Industrial nitrogen fixation for fertilizers produces ammonia by combining atmospheric nitrogen with', '["carbon dioxide", "oxygen", "hydrogen", "nitrate"]', 2, 'In industrial fixation, atmospheric nitrogen and hydrogen are combined to produce ammonia for fertilizers.', '{"0": "CO2 is not used to make ammonia.", "1": "Nitrogen combines with oxygen in atmospheric fixation by lightning.", "3": "Nitrate is formed by nitrification, not used as a raw material here."}'),
  ('BIO-5353', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 233 (§25.2.2)', 'Nitrification is needed because ammonia', '["cannot be formed by any organism", "is the gas that makes up 78% of the air", "is used directly by animals to make proteins", "is often toxic or difficult for plants to use in large amounts"]', 3, 'Ammonia is often toxic or hard for plants to use in large amounts, so soil bacteria convert it to nitrate, which plants absorb easily.', '{"0": "Ammonia is formed by nitrogen fixation and ammonification.", "1": "That is nitrogen gas (N2).", "2": "Animals assimilate nitrogen by eating plants and other animals."}'),
  ('BIO-5354', 'Ecology', 'Nitrogen cycle', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 233 (§25.2.2)', 'Animals obtain nitrogen for their proteins by', '["absorbing nitrates through their skin", "fixing atmospheric nitrogen in their lungs", "consuming plants and other animals", "converting nitrite to nitrate"]', 2, 'Animals assimilate nitrogen by consuming plants and other animals; plants and microbes absorb nitrates from the soil.', '{"0": "Nitrate absorption from soil is done by plants and microbes.", "1": "Animals cannot fix N2.", "3": "That is done by Nitrobacter."}'),
  ('BIO-5355', 'Ecology', 'Trophic levels', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 234 (§25.3.1)', 'Besides carnivores such as frogs and snakes, which organisms are placed in trophic level 3?', '["Wolves and hawks", "Parasites of animals that feed on herbivores", "Rabbits and insects", "Green plants and algae"]', 1, 'Trophic level 3 contains secondary consumers (carnivores) and the parasites of animals that feed on herbivores.', '{"0": "These are tertiary consumers in trophic level 4.", "2": "These are herbivores in trophic level 2.", "3": "These are producers in trophic level 1."}'),
  ('BIO-5356', 'Ecology', 'Energy flow', 'easy', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 234 (§25.3.2)', 'According to the book, an ecosystem generally has how many trophic levels?', '["Three or four", "Exactly two", "Ten or more", "One"]', 0, 'There are generally three or four trophic levels, because energy is lost at each transfer.', '{"1": "Most ecosystems have more than producers and herbivores.", "2": "So much energy is lost at each step that long chains are not supported.", "3": "At least producers and consumers are needed."}'),
  ('BIO-5357', 'Ecology', 'Survivorship', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 236 (§25.4)', 'Birds, lizards and rodents, with a constant probability of death throughout life, show which survivorship curve?', '["Type III (concave)", "Type II (diagonal)", "Exponential", "Type I (convex)"]', 1, 'Type II populations have a constant probability of death throughout life; birds, lizards and rodents are examples.', '{"0": "Type III applies to fish and many invertebrates with very high juvenile death.", "2": "Exponential describes a growth model, not a survivorship curve.", "3": "Type I applies to humans and mammals that mostly live to old age."}'),
  ('BIO-5358', 'Ecology', 'Population dispersion', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 237 (§25.4)', 'Plants whose wind-dispersed seeds germinate wherever they fall in a favourable spot are an example of', '["clumped spacing", "logistic growth", "random spacing", "uniform spacing"]', 2, 'In random spacing individuals do not interact strongly; it occurs in some plants with wind-dispersed seeds, and is not common in nature.', '{"0": "Clumped spacing is seen when seeds drop straight to the ground or animals live in groups.", "1": "Logistic growth is a growth model, not a dispersion pattern.", "3": "Uniform spacing results from inhibition of neighbours or territories."}'),
  ('BIO-5359', 'Ecology', 'Population density', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 237 (§25.4)', 'When a dense population approaches its carrying capacity, the book says', '["competition becomes severe, birth rate falls and mortality risk rises", "birth rate rises because mates are easy to find", "growth becomes exponential", "dispersion always becomes random"]', 0, 'At high density near carrying capacity, competition for resources can be severe, leading to a lower birth rate, higher risk of death and high pollution.', '{"1": "The book says birth rate decreases at high density.", "2": "Growth slows near carrying capacity, as in the logistic model.", "3": "Density does not fix the dispersion pattern."}'),
  ('BIO-5360', 'Ecology', 'Human population', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 238 (§25.4.1)', 'According to the book, by weight humans make up about what share of Earth''s biomass?', '["16%", "13%", "82%", "0.01%"]', 3, 'Humans make up only about 0.01% of Earth''s biomass; plants make about 82% and bacteria about 13%.', '{"0": "16% is the share of world population living in developed countries.", "1": "13% is the share made by bacteria.", "2": "82% is the share made by plants."}')
) AS s JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Biology', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('BIO-5361', 'Ecology', 'Human population', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 238 (§25.4.1)', 'Starting in the early 1700s, humans were able to expand the carrying capacity of their habitats mainly because of', '["decreasing global temperatures", "technology giving more control over food, defence and disease", "a sharp rise in natural predators", "a fall in the number of births"]', 1, 'Changes in technology gave humans control over food supply, better weapons against predators and remedies for diseases, and made them less vulnerable to climate.', '{"0": "Climate change was not the reason given for expansion.", "2": "Humans developed weapons to ward off predators.", "3": "Population grew rapidly over this period."}'),
  ('BIO-5362', 'Ecology', 'Greenhouse effect', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 239 (§25.5)', 'According to the book, carbon dioxide contributes about what share of global warming?', '["11%", "3%", "80%", "6%"]', 2, 'CO2 is the most significant greenhouse gas, contributing about 80% of global warming; methane about 11%, nitrous oxide about 6% and fluorinated gases about 3%.', '{"0": "11% is the contribution of methane.", "1": "3% is the contribution of fluorinated gases.", "3": "6% is the contribution of nitrous oxide."}'),
  ('BIO-5363', 'Ecology', 'Greenhouse effect', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 239 (§25.5)', 'Nitrous oxide (N2O) is emitted mainly from', '["photosynthesis by plants", "enteric fermentation in cattle only", "old refrigerators and air conditioners only", "agricultural and industrial activities and burning of fossil fuels"]', 3, 'N2O is emitted during agricultural and industrial activities and combustion of fossil fuels; it contributes about 6% of global warming.', '{"0": "Photosynthesis takes in CO2 and gives off oxygen.", "1": "Cattle are a major source of methane.", "2": "These release fluorinated gases."}'),
  ('BIO-5364', 'Ecology', 'Greenhouse effect', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 239 (§25.5)', 'Before industrialization, the global atmospheric CO2 concentration was about', '["280 ppm", "1000 ppm", "78 ppm", "50 ppm"]', 0, 'Before industrialization, atmospheric CO2 was about 280 ppm; the book reports it has since risen above 420 ppm.', '{"1": "This is far above both pre-industrial and current values.", "2": "78% is the nitrogen content of air, unrelated to CO2 ppm.", "3": "This is far too low."}'),
  ('BIO-5365', 'Ecology', 'Climate effects', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 239 (§25.5)', 'Why do temperatures above about 50 °C, as in Jacobabad and Sibi, often cause fatal heatstroke?', '["Sweat glands stop forming in the skin", "The human body can no longer keep its normal body temperature", "Oxygen in the air is used up", "The air contains more nitrogen"]', 1, 'When atmospheric temperature crosses 50 °C, the human body cannot adjust its normal temperature, leading to fatal heatstroke.', '{"0": "Sweat glands are present; the problem is losing enough heat.", "2": "Heat does not remove oxygen from the air.", "3": "Air composition does not change with heat."}'),
  ('BIO-5366', 'Ecology', 'Climate effects', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 240 (§25.5)', 'A cloudburst is', '["seawater moving into coastal farmland", "slow seepage of rainwater into the soil", "a sudden release of water from a glacial lake", "a sudden, extremely heavy rainfall over a small area in a short time"]', 3, 'A cloudburst is sudden and extremely heavy rainfall in a short period over a small area.', '{"0": "That is seawater intrusion from sea-level rise.", "1": "That is infiltration.", "2": "That is a glacial lake outburst flood (GLOF)."}'),
  ('BIO-5367', 'Ecology', 'Climate effects', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 240 (§25.5)', 'A warmer atmosphere leads to more intense rainfall and floods mainly because it', '["holds more moisture", "contains less carbon dioxide", "increases the size of glaciers", "stops evaporation from the oceans"]', 0, 'A warmer atmosphere holds more moisture, leading to erratic, intense rainfall, stronger monsoons and floods.', '{"1": "Warming is linked to more CO2, not less.", "2": "Warming melts glaciers.", "3": "Heat increases evaporation."}'),
  ('BIO-5368', 'Ecology', 'Climate effects', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 241 (§25.5)', 'According to the book, high temperatures reduce the grain yield of wheat and rice most when they occur during', '["ploughing of the field", "the winter dormancy of seeds", "the flowering stage", "seed storage after harvest"]', 2, 'High temperatures during the flowering stage of wheat and rice can significantly reduce grain yield, adding to food insecurity.', '{"0": "Ploughing is a farming practice, not a crop growth stage.", "1": "Not described in the book.", "3": "The book links yield loss to heat during flowering."}'),
  ('BIO-5369', 'Ecology', 'Ecosystems of Pakistan', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 241 (§25.6.1)', 'Shogran, Neelam Valley, Murree and parts of Kaghan, with 750–1500 mm of annual rainfall and humus-rich soil, are locations of', '["temperate deciduous forest", "tropical thorn forest", "mangrove forest", "alpine meadow"]', 0, 'Temperate deciduous forests occur in moist northern mountains (Shogran, Neelam, Murree, Kaghan) with 750–1500 mm rainfall and grey-brown humus-rich soil.', '{"1": "Thorn forests are on the Indus plains with 200–750 mm rainfall.", "2": "Mangroves are on the Sindh and Balochistan coasts.", "3": "Alpine meadows are on high mountain tops with heavy snow."}'),
  ('BIO-5370', 'Ecology', 'Ecosystems of Pakistan', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 242 (§25.6.2)', 'Plants of the tropical thorn forest have thorns mainly to', '["trap insects for nitrogen", "help them breathe in waterlogged soil", "catch snow in winter", "deter herbivores and reduce water loss"]', 3, 'The tropical thorn forest has xerophytic, drought-resistant plants with thorns to deter herbivores and reduce water loss.', '{"0": "No such function is given.", "1": "Breathing roots are a mangrove feature.", "2": "These are hot, arid plains."}'),
  ('BIO-5371', 'Ecology', 'Ecosystems of Pakistan', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 242 (§25.6.2)', 'Jhand (Prosopis cineraria), Peelu (Salvadora oleoides) and Karir (Capparis aphylla) are key flora of the', '["temperate deciduous forest", "tropical thorn forest", "mangrove forest", "alpine meadow"]', 1, 'These drought-resistant plants, along with Kikar, are the key flora of the tropical thorn forest of the Indus plains.', '{"0": "Its flora includes yew, blue pine and Himalayan cedar.", "2": "Its main tree is Avicennia marina.", "3": "Its flora includes stunted junipers, birch and willow."}'),
  ('BIO-5372', 'Ecology', 'Ecosystems of Pakistan', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 243 (§25.6.3)', 'Snow leopard, Himalayan ibex, musk deer and marmots are key fauna of', '["tropical thorn forest", "mangrove forest", "alpine and sub-alpine meadows", "temperate deciduous forest"]', 2, 'These animals live in the high, cold alpine and sub-alpine meadows such as the Deosai Plains and mountain tops of Gilgit-Baltistan.', '{"0": "Its fauna includes camels, chinkara and houbara bustard.", "1": "Its fauna includes mudskippers, crabs and shrimps.", "3": "Its fauna includes rhesus monkeys and black bears."}'),
  ('BIO-5373', 'Ecology', 'Ecosystems of Pakistan', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 244 (§25.6.4)', 'Mangrove trees in Pakistan have breathing roots because they grow in', '["dry, sandy desert soil", "humus-rich forest soil", "frozen soil under heavy snow", "anaerobic, oxygen-poor mudflats"]', 3, 'Mangroves grow on anaerobic (oxygen-poor) mudflats in brackish water, so the trees have breathing roots.', '{"0": "That describes desert or thorn-forest habitats.", "1": "That describes the temperate deciduous forest.", "2": "That describes alpine meadows."}'),
  ('BIO-5374', 'Ecology', 'Ecosystems of Pakistan', 'moderate', 'Biology 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 25 Ecology, p. 244 (§25.6.4)', 'The mangrove forests of Pakistan are threatened mainly by', '["the lumber industry in Kaghan", "heavy snowfall", "reduced freshwater flow from the Indus and pollution from Karachi", "canal irrigation clearing the Punjab plains"]', 2, 'Mangroves are threatened by reduced freshwater flow from the Indus and pollution from Karachi, though restoration projects have helped.', '{"0": "This pressures the temperate deciduous forest.", "1": "Mangroves are coastal and do not receive heavy snow.", "3": "This mainly degraded the tropical thorn forest."}')
